      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-05-18_WidnesExaminer_art0009" n="art0009">
        <head>' LADIES' LETTER.</head>
        <p>' LADIES' LETTER.</p>
        <p>MY DEAR NELLIE,</p>
        <p>There appears to be a frequent display of flat. crowned hats, and most beautiful straws are used for them. Among them are the silver laces and the gold, of exquisite daintiness and so decorative in themselves that it wohld be foolish to attempt to trim them with anything more elaborate than a scarf of mousseline de glze and a bunch or two of flowers. The black cravat, whether it be in tulle, satin, or taifetas, is the finishing touch to the Mouse or shirt correct at present; a note of black there must be somewhere or other in the toilette. This is far from being displeasing, for this little touch of black has the knack of imparting a dis- tinction to a simple toilette which would otherwise 'oe lacking. It has the merit of showing in relief the colouring of the face, and has a far greater chic than the coloured scarf which may people seem to consider the indispensahls aniedo..; uuoch.</p>
        <p>A bodice which I saw yesterday was niacte with a deep yoke of jetted lace over silk, outlined with a band of ribbon having two or three rows of the latter round the tops of the close-fitting sleeves. The front of the bodice was pouched so as to slightly overhang the ribbon waistband, which should have a jet clasp. Another suggestion that has occurred to me is to edge the skirt with five narrow tucks, and make the 'bodice with a falling collar forming revers in front, of jetted guipure over silk, and a pointed or heart shaped vest of tucked chiffon, black or white. The bodice should be tucked a little, the tucks running downwards if you are at all stout, round thP figure if you are slender.</p>
        <p>Grey and blue appear to have become popular. The former is of that elusive grey tint which forms such an admirable background for the rich creamy tones of old lace. Blue is well suited to blondes and those blessed with an irreproachable com- plexion, but it is distinctly trying to less fortunate individuals. The somewhat previous touch of summer we have been favoured with lately has called forth from their seclusion our last year's en-tout-cas and parasols. These items of the present year have all the most elegant handles, a ball of crystal mounted in gold or silver, or adorned with clover leaves in the same metals, being the newest thing. One I saw had a deli. cately-tinted jade stick. with a bee, larger than the laziest drone that over disgraced a hive, in painted metal, poised on the top. The parasols of this season are large and very domed shaped, and the fashionable ones are chiefly of taffetas, with an insertion of guipure or chiffon round the edge. One I saw yesterday was of blue chiffon, the chiffon set irto inch-wide tucks right up to the ferrule.</p>
        <p>The introduction of the double sleeve was such an unexpected event that the definite partnership of itself and the chemisetto..passEed unnoticed. Yet it will be observed that the two ore now staunch allies, leaving the rest of a blice to be dominated by an Eton, a bolero, or a coat. It is a pretty conceit that pretends that the soft delicata mousse- line de soie vest has sleeves of the same which are worn beneath the elbow length ones of the bolero, though such is not the case. The efFeet is just as if it were, however, and charming it is. I am informed on very reliable authority that lace coats will be a great feature of summer dress. Most of them are of the b01.h.0 order, whilo others fit at the back like art ordinary bodice, and are made with or without a basque. Others hang per- fectly loose from the shoulders and are unlined, so that the figure of the wearer can be seen through the transparent meshes. Lace sleeves and yoke are sold in sets, and sometimes a lace panel for the skirt forms an addition. In embroidered net and satin fronts jet and steel is preferred, as being more the fashion of the moment than jet alone, or even steel alone. Some lovely enabroideries are in a mixture of silk, chenille, and very fine beads, with small jewelled sequins and opalescent tint-. ings. The erect of some of these is admirable, so soft and refined, as compared with the strong, coarse, beady glitter of the cheaper sorts, always eschewed by Your loving, JENNIE.</p>
