      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-05-18_WidnesExaminer_art0019" n="art0019">
        <head>Organs and Harmoniums, 5/- per month. CRANE AND SONS, LIMITED, LIVERPOOL.</head>
        <p>Organs and Harmoniums, 5/- per month. CRANE AND SONS, LIMITED, LIVERPOOL.</p>
        <p>"WOMEN'S CHAT."</p>
        <p>There was some expectation that the Queen would visit the Royal Aeademy during her recent stay in London. At one time Her Majesty was an annual visi- tor to the galleries at Burlington House and later she seems to have grown both younger and more active under war's alarms, and the first suggestion of national danger. The Queen however, did n it go to the Academy though she is always well represented there, as few members f the Royal Family fail to pay it at least one visit during the eeeson. Ibis year no fewer than 14,000 pictures were offered to the Royal Academy, of which only about one seventh appear in the Exhibition. Among the twelve thousand reject, d canvasses were those of the well-known policeman ewh ~ except for the undue publicity given to his works,might have stood a better chance with the Selec:ing Committee. e Thu National Baz tar, of which the Queen is patron, is one of the principal events of the London season, and it is hoped that the three days' sale on May 24, h,2sth, and 26th, will result in rah lug £so,ooo—in aid of the War Fund. It is therefore a more imp rtant event than that of a mere society diversion, especially as it is associated with the celebration of the Queen's birthday, and Her Majesty has taken great interest in the arrangements. There is a stall for nearly every regiment at the front, no pains being spared to make them as pretty, and as attractive as ceuld be imagined, there is also an in- terestiog exhibition of relief from the war, and the bazaar is thoroughly representative of Eaelend.Scotland Ireland and Wales, and the Colonies who have played such a noble aud impertant part in the preeent war. In few things are filets and legend more inextricably confused than in tee stories of aalanDO. Now, accord- ing to a telegram from Pretoria, it is proposed to add to the narrativr sof fact by enrolling on the Boer side a corps of wemen, whose opposition would he something of an embarrassment to our troops, and possibly itill more inconvenient to the side on which they fought. If tee idea ware carried into effect, it would nes be the Britiah Arms 's first experience of a female warrior, aithoueh one has to go a good way back to find the ex- amples, and in these cases one might wish that the incidents had never occurred. Boadicea, Qiieen of an ancient British state led a mot- ley army of 230,000 to elaughter against a small Roman force with the resu!t that soneeteing like 80,000 perished. ! and the Queen herself it supposed to have pat an end to her own lice. There can be no doubt that Boadicea had been terribly wronged but according to the old chronic- ! lers the was not a very amiable pereonage, and Glides described her as "the crafty lioness." On a second !occasion Joan of Arc was unsuccessful in her opposition to the Eneish force, tut althoueh we, in the year 1900 are not responsible for the acts of our forefathers, in 1431, yet no Briton in these days recalls the event with- out a feeling of the most extreme regret. The war has not been permitted to interfere with the Opera season at Covent Garden, though it has ceused many gaps among familiar laces usually teen on these occasions. The terribly cold winds played havoc with the artistes for the openir g night neeessitating several changes ha the cast, h:.t tee performance was remark- ably good, aro the house was crowded with a brilliant audience. The Prince and Princess of Wales who have greatly interested themselves in making the present Opera season a success.were preserit,ae were also several other men-theca of the Royal Family. The ierluence of these entertainments upon the London season is con- siderable, and the Prifcce is anxious that trade should not suffer more than is inevitable in consequence of the war. The Prince is a shareholder in the Grand Opera Ssndicate which owns the lease of Covent Garden. Altogether there are sixty-ono shareholders, and among them are the Duche3s ef Marlborough, tbe Duke of Port- laud, Lord Iveagis,Maelame Pattnand the Earl of Crewe. Mice Florence Nightingale who has just entered upon her eighty-first year, owes her Christian name to the Italian city in which she was born in 1620. The pre- sent war has illustrated the inestimable value of the work which she beans, in the Crimea. Not only has she livid to see war deemed of some of its horrors by the excel- knee of the Red Cross organisation. and the care that is now given to the sick, and wounded, but she has herself taken the greatest interest in the efforts teat have been made to provide sufficient hospital aceommcdatiou and medical comforts for the troops in South Africa. Oely quite recently Miss Niehtingaie sent £lBO to the Lord Provo! t of Edinburgh, for the benefit of the South Africa Hospital, which has been (quipped by the special efforts of that city, and the East of Scotland. This is but one of many instances of ready help the has given durieg the war, to the good work with whica her name will ever be associated. Another link with the past long ago, has been several by the death of Miss Perceval who took an aCtiVt! inter- est in social questions in the earlier part of the Queen's reign. She was the youngest daughter ,and het survivor of tke twelve chilchen ths Right den. Spencer Pet- coval.'who wee Prime lilirsister, and Chancellor of the Exchequer, and who was assassinated as he was ere er- log tee House of Corea:oos on May 11th 1312. Miss Percevel was then barely seven years ola, !reviser been born in 1805,but :he well rememtered the tragic circum- stances, of her father's death, and she could give a viviri account of the shrivel of the itwe of the Battle of \Vetere o.</p>
        <p>'Elie death of the Iti,ht Lino. Sper,ecr Pereaval, wts rtruarka.hle as ha.?isg been fore.seen in a crtam by a certain John Wiiliams in Cornw,ii. Ed rOated thg circonsEnnees to others, before the trigeoy was known, and afterwards he pointel out the Epot in the EIGII,q-.! of Ccrernots where it tiarrx.nr-d, ar d which be had 6( en in his vision. A recori of this singular diem siory is pre-</p>
        <p>IFerved by the Terceira' family, and its good faith has never bees sellouts' challenged or disputed. The leafyi month of June is almost with us, but as yet we have had scsnty opportunity of ,xhibiting our spring clothes. Instead the majority of us have unearthed furs, and winter coats aad skirts ere sallying forth. The unhappy few who preferred appearance to comfort are now mostly, in seclusion, where it i to be hoped they will learn wisdom. Modistes are complaining bitterly of lack of ordors. When the warm weather does come to stay there will undoabt...cily be a groat rush for clothes, hut who cues to select muslin and;chiffons in, what has felt like, mid-winter ? Same of the flowered muslins are exquisite. In many instances it is indeed difficalt to distinguish bqween the printed and hand painted, spnimelis the only indication being, the price asked. These printed =mhos are said to clean, and even wash, and moreover defy the hottest of suns. Concerning the latter assurance, however, some doubt may be expressed. There would probably be a slight fading of the colours, were the muslin subjsc- tea to very severe tests.</p>
        <p>MAZ GE."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-05-18_WidnesExaminer_art0020" n="art0020">
        <head>WAR: ANOTHER VIEW.</head>
        <p>WAR: ANOTHER VIEW.</p>
        <p>When British armies by commend Go forth on War's career, Enthusiasm fills the land, And loud the people cheer. Orations too and prayers are made The frenzied scene to bless As if the soldier's awful trade Were based en righteousness. But few, alas, regard with pain The prospects of the strife ; Or reck that thousands will be slain, And thousand! maimed for life. Nor will dead men alone bestrew Our Moloch's reeking path ; For troops of Old'e dumb creatures too Will perish in His wrath. And 0, with tears and fainting breath, What souls will stricken be, When in the daily roll of death Some names beloved they see! Yet heedless of this tale of woe, And deaf to Pity's cry; Our rulers still more reckless grow, And call more men to die. 0 that mankind in these lan days Would lay all this to heart; And see that war, in all its ways But acts an evil part. For what is war, wherever waged, By saints or savage horde? A force t-•y weieh all right's outraged, And blood like water poured. And as a nation don't we pose As Christians sound and true! Enjoined to love'and bless our foes, And only peace pursue. Warrington. J. H. W. CLINDINNING.</p>
