      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-05-18_WidnesExaminer_art0024" n="art0024">
        <head>STEVE, THE OUTLANDER.</head>
        <p>STEVE, THE OUTLANDER.</p>
        <p>By ARTHUR LAYCOCK, Author of "The Young Gaffer," " Seaside Sketches)!</p>
        <p>"Lancashire Sketches," iirb.</p>
        <p>CHAPTER XXV.</p>
        <p>On the morning of January Ist, 1899, Henry Cartleigh, the English missionary at Umhlana, stood at the door of his corrugated iron hut and looked out at the country before him. For four months he had laboured amongst the dusky dwellers in this hilly region, and as he retrospectively mined back a feeling of dissatisfaction came upon Tam. His mission work had not been as successful as he could have desired it to be. He had not made many converts. The natives still maintained great faith in their witch-doctors and medicine men, and the prophecies of the former were held in high reverence by the superstitious Matounby, his servant man, had come to him a few minutes before, and said:</p>
        <p>"Umbombo, the witch-doctor, away yonder by the hill with the white crest, he tells us many strange things, chief."</p>
        <p>"You should not listen to hie tales," the missionary had answered. "No, chief, no, incose, I do not listen, but our people do. You tell them to listen only to the Great White Chief, lapa panzi (and he pointed heavenward), but they heed you not, but go to hear what Umbombo tells them, and he tells strange things.</p>
        <p>"He says that he is a greater chief than you, the heaven-sent chief. He can tell what will happen to-morrow, the day after, and even the day after that, but you cannot tell so much. And he said to Mashla, the induna, and to the umfahn (boy) who herds the cattle: Drive the cows from the pasture and send them to my cattle kraal, and if the red ones eat the food that I give them there will be blood in the land before many new moons have risen. And if the white cattle eat the food</p>
        <p>1d the black eat, we and.all our tribes shall go to battle with the men across the hills; but if the black kine do not eat the food but the speckled ones do, then the men across the hills will fight against the uhite men, against your white queen and against the white chief of the big hut,' meaning. you, _ incose. " _</p>
        <p>"Don't tell me anything more of its" the missionary said.</p>
        <p>"Yes, &amp;it listen," continued Matounby. "The cattle were driven to Umbombo's kraal, and he held out his hand and gave them medicine food, and the red cows eat it, and the white cows eat it, but the black cattle would not touch it, but turned away, and the speckled cows came up and all tasted of the food. And the people said: Wow! Um- bombo is a mighty witch, greatest of the witches!' And TJnibombo said: 'Listen, ye people I tell you of the blood that will be shed, and which will roll down these hills and into these valleys; and the big river (Tugela) and the good river (Mooi) will be rivers of blood. But it' will not be ours. No, I, Umbombo, have seen what is to come to pass and I tell you the men from over the hills, the Boermen, and the white chiefs, the Inglesmen (Englishmen) will fight, and shed their blood, and our crops will be under foot, and our mealies will be trampled, and we shall lack of food if we stay here, for the battle will be terrible.'</p>
        <p>"And," continued Matounby, "when our people heard this they cried out aloud: %ow I Wow Umbombo speaks true. We will heed him, and leave this country before the blood washes our mealies and before we starve for food.' And, white chief, they are going away soon, and what will you do ? "</p>
        <p>"fill remain her.," Henry Cartleigh had said. "Now ring the bell and call the people to service." To himself he said: "We must cure these poor, simple people of these superstitions. How gullible they are. Umbombo has heard the white men talk of the Transvaal trouble, and, being a trifle cuter and more long-headed than the rest, he thinks he can guess how the trouble will end. His medicine and elaborate rigmarole are used as a blind to the folks. I must disillusion them."</p>
        <p>The bell began to tinkle and presently a stalwart Kaffir came up to the missionary. "Umza, incose 1 Mashlabe he go to lo kyah ? " (to the huts). "Yes, go bring the people up, Mashlaba. Tell them service will begin at once." Shouting and gesticulating the big fellow bounded down the slope and soon his dusky form was seen on the opposite hillside, flitting from hut to hut and kraal to kraal. A stream of black figures came from the surrounding hills and valleys, and as the bell ceased its monotonous tinkling a crowd of a hundred were gathered in a circle round the missionary.</p>
        <p>A hymn was sung and the deep heavy voices of men and women mingled inharmoniously with the shriller voices of the children, waking faint echoes .;.:31. the hills and causing the browsing cattle to look up in wonder. Then the congregation knelt in prayer as the minister bared his head and invoked God's blessing. A most impressive scene. But it was lost upon Umbombo, who stood apart, a scoffer, conscious of his own importance. The address follom;ed, simple, yet forceful and convincing. Henry Cartleigh exhorted his hearers to seek the Almighty, the Great Chief, Chief of all chiefs, the All-Seeing, the All-Knowing. A dog on the fringe of the crowd now interrupted the proceedings by barking loudly. Suddenly it darted down the hillside, snarling fiercely. The missionary followed the noisy animal with his eyes. It ran towards a low cactus bush, then fell back with a piercing howl as a white man rushed upon it and struck it viciously with a heavy stick. "Wow! Wow!" cried the astonished natives.</p>
        <p>The enraged animal flew to the attack again, but this time the man struck a blow that killed it at once.</p>
        <p>"Ah, white wan, you've killed me dog!" cried Umbombo. "You've killed it! You shall be killed, you dog.!" and, spitting in the direction of the white man, who advanced threateningly towards him, Umbombo took to his heels. _</p>
        <p>"Rice reception this to give a fellow," said the man, coming up to the crowd of worshippers. "How are you, Harry ? " "Ralph 1 The natives stepped aside, staring with ' open mouths as the missionary held out his hand to the fagged new-comer. "What are you doing here ? " "Well, I've come to your Sunday service," replied Ralph Cartleigh, as flippantly as ever, "but you can bet your best boots that I've not tramped all this way merely to hear you spout. Well, what are you staring at ? " turning to the Baffirs who were crowding around. "Off you go. Vootsach I Skedaddle!"</p>
        <p>The service so rudely interrupted was brought to a sudden close as the missionary dismissed his flock with a blessing. As the people dispersed Ralph, who had, mean- while, lighted a cigarette, followed hi g brother into the hut.</p>
        <p>astonished to see you here," began Henry. "Indeed! Well, I'm not astonished to see you. I heard that you were hanging out Colenso way, so I came up," said Ralph. "I've been on the road a month." "On the road from where ? "</p>
        <p>"From Durban. Came part of the distance by rail, then, as the money wouldn't pan out, I footed it from 'Maritzburc.,,."</p>
        <p>"Dear ! dear! What a long tramp, and through such a country." "Yes it's pretty rough for me. You appear tp be used to roughing it every way out: here." lie glanced round the scantily-furnished hut.. "Not got many • luxuries here, I notice. You might as well be in Ancoats."</p>
        <p>"I have all that I need" Henry answered. "Alt, well, that's good. But I haven't, and that brings me to business, brother. I'm stony broke. I need a lot of t4ings, you'll see, if you cast your eye over me. I need boots, and socks, and shirts, and a suit or two of clothes wouldn't come amiss." _ _ _</p>
        <p>"I heard that you had left home," Henry re- marked, as if to turn the conversation in other channels. -</p>
        <p>"Oh. you did, eh? I suppose they gave you all particulars?" "I don't know."</p>
        <p>_ " Ah, well, I reckon you know enough. YoW d better know, though, that that five hundred was honestly mine. I've not had half enough money from the guv'nor for years, and look at me now. You see how far that little lot has gone." Matounby now brought food for his master and the visitor, and Ralph, uninvited, commenced to eat. _ _</p>
        <p>"How much can you tip me?" he asked during the muLnchine process.</p>
        <p>4*llow much can I what " said his brother, n/t comprehending, the slang. "Tip me—give me. Have you been out hero so long that you've forgotten plain _English r now much brass' can you let me have ? ' "Not much, I'm afraid, said Henry, after a brief mental calculation. "I have but a few pounds in the Ladysmith bank. What there is you may take." "Thanks. I'll have it all. You don't need much, I should think, living out here. I'll have a coat and a vest, and a pair of trousers, too, or anything else you can spare. It doesn't matter if the togs are of clerical cut. I can pass off as a sanctimonious Boer if I wear 'em." His brother was about to remonstrate when Ralph continued: "I've come across a few Dutchmen between here and 'Maritzburg. I can't speak the teal, but I can say caffee, and whenever I've said that the old jokers have generally trotted the stuff out." He had now eaten the greater portion of the boiled fowl which the Kaffir had placed before them. "A rare country for one's appetite," he remarked, as he stretched out his legs and yawned lazily. "It's almost as good as being in quad for that."</p>
        <p>Then with a sly gesture he added: "Suppose you didn't know I'd been in gaol in this delightful country?" "In gaol! Surely not, Ralph?" his brother cried, in amazement. "Fact, though. Got run in for lambasting a cheeky ricksha buy. Darn'd scamps, these niggers, Harry. Couldn't pay the fine, as I'd lost all my cash, or had had it stolen, so I had to go down for fourteen days. That was six weeks ago. I've been hungry ever since." Henry Cartleigh was deeply shocked when he heard this news.</p>
        <p>"Don't worry about me," said Ralph, guessing his thought. 'l'm all right, and so are you. As for our family name, well, I don't carry it abmit with me now, so it isn't likely to get smeared. My name is Percival now, Harry. Matounby again entered the hut and cleared away the things from the table. "A fine lot of rascals these niggers are," said Ralph, when the Kaffir had gone. "I've not been in this country long, but I've made that discovery. Liars and thieves and rogues • every one of 'em. Flogginq 'em, kicking them, that's the only way to civilise em. Did you hear what that black rascal said to me when I'd knocked his cursed dog over? A lot of ebony scamps, they are. Did you notice the cheeky way they swarmed round us when I came up? That's because you've spoilt 'em. If that howling dog had only kept quiet your service wouldn't have been interrupted. I was enjoying it, especially the singing part. Your congregation are beautiful singers." He talked on all subjects in his old, flippant, cynical fashion, Henry scarce making any answer - mg remarks. When Ralph spoke of going to Johannesburg, however, his brother endeavoured to dissuade him.</p>
        <p>"Trade is very bad there now," said Henry, "in consequence of the unsettled state of political affairs. I believe there will be war in the Transvaal before many months are past. I have spoken with some of our local Dutchmen and they state that their brethren in the Transvaal will fight rather than grant further concessions to the Outlanders." "Oh, well," said Ralph, lightly, "if there's going to be fighting I might as well be in it. War is what I like. I seem to have been in it, especially the domestic sort, all my life. But the fun of it is I fight against those who are nearest and ought to be dearest to me. At home, as you know, father and I were always having wordy battles. If there's fighting out here, I shouldn't wonder if, in my 'natural cussedness,' I should be again found on the wrong side, fighting against my fatherland. Ah, well; it's all in a lifetime. Perhaps if I am in at the fighting it will be a death-time for me. No matter; I never shall be missed."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-05-18_WidnesExaminer_art0025" n="art0025">
        <head>CHAPTER MI.</head>
        <p>CHAPTER MI.</p>
        <p>Ralph remained at the mission station a week, then, arrayed in Henry's best clothes and with all his brother's spare cash in his pocket, he took train for Johannesburg. At Volksrust, on the Transvaal border, a lengthy stay was made while the passengers' luggage was examined by the custom's officials. Not having any luggage Ralph sauntered out of the station and proceeded to an adjacent wayside canteen. Here he commenced'• gambling with a number of disreputable-looking fellows, , forgetting altogether his train. -</p>
        <p>"Reckon it's about time you caught your train, boss," said one of the card-players, when Percival drew back from the gaming-table, having lost every shilling. "He'll have to be pretty spry to catch it, 'for it's fifty miles along the road by this," said another. "What's the ticket worth ? " asked Ralph, hold- ing up his second-class single to Johannesburg. "Not much, but I'll give you half a quid for it," said one of the men. The gambling re-commenced, and Ralph, having won back a considerable sum, ceased playing. His late partners demurred, but he refused to be drawn into play again. They succeeded, however, in making him helplessly drunk before nightfall, and the next morning Ralph found himself penniless again in a strange out-of-the-way place two hundred miles from the Golden City. That day and the next he loafed about the station and the canteen in the hope of encountering some good. Samaritan who would help him in his difficulty. On the third day a Dutchman travelling to Standerton with his ox-waggon offered to give him a lift. Ralph gladly joined the man, who found him a comfortable seat on the lumbering, jolting vehicle.</p>
        <p>Although the distance to Standerton was7com- paratively short the journey occupied many days, much time being lost by continuous outspanning. At the last outspan a thunderstorm burst over the caravan, and everyone was drenched to the skin by the accompanying downpour of tropical rain. When the town was reached on the following day Ralph was in a high state of fever. His Dutch friend, seeing his dangerous condi- tion, took him to the hotels in the little town ; but none of the proprietors cared to have a penniless invalid on their hands. "I'll take him to my brother," said Jan Schultz at last. "Tanta shall nurse him and the English- man shall get well and go his way." Wherewith the big, brown-bearded Boer took Ralph out to the straggling farm, three miles out across the veld. When Jan made his business known he was not well received by his brother Paul. "Nurse the sick Englishman!" cried Paul, indignantly. "Nurse the voordomned rooinek, you say, Jan Schultz ? No, I will not. . No, Tanta shall not. Why should we nurse him? Because you are a fool, Jan Schultz; because you pick up the Englishman and put him on your waggon and he take sick. But we are not such fools, Tanta and I." "But you will have him, Paul. You have helped the Englishmen before," said Jan. "Yes, Jan Schultz, we have had these tramp rooineks before, but who brought them, Jan ? You did, you know. You have given them room on your waggon before. When they have come from Natal poor and sick you have pitied them, the cursed Englishmen! and you have brought them here that we should give them food and shelter them. And we have done it Tanta and I have done it. But we shall do it no more. You bring the sick Englishron but we will not have him. Tanta shall not nurse him. Why should we ? Nurse him and get him well and strong, and what for? Yes, what for, Jan Schultz? That he may go to Johannesburg and become Outlander. Yes, become Outlander and become our enemy, say we 'press (oppress) him, and he send papers to the Rand and cause much trouble. That's what your sick Englishman will do, Jan Schultz. He will become Outlander and talk of Frank-eyes, Frank- eyes. There will be no peace for us, Jan Schultz, until we shoot every voordonmed rooinek and Frank-eyes in the country. Yes, I know, Jan Schultz, of what I speak. I have been to Pretoria and to Johannesburg, and I tell you it is so. This is what your Englishman will do. They come threuev here every day, every year. We see them go on the railway—ssh ! ssh ! And they cannot travel quick enough they so want the gold, the gold. They do not till the soil, grow the grain and breed the cut ho ; they want the gold, and they must travel c,ziek for it, and become Outlanders and call the Boorman a stupid fool. We are stupid fools. Jan t,eluw-7, especially you. When the Eng,lishhein ino;;;.7 is done, and he is tramp, and he can't, on railway—piff I piff !—you give him lit.; es_ :jeer waggon and he is glad to travel slew, ever so slow-, for Jan Schultz is good- natured fool p.ml wilt feed the Englishman well, and will teal,: aner him."</p>
        <p>So rani ra,,A and stormed, but in thb end consoltod onion again to lake in the "cursed Enqlishman." -- - -</p>
        <p>``.l3ut he is the last, Jan Schultz. We shall not torso or rive food to one more, to make him strong</p>
        <p>_ sand well so that he may fight against us. les, .Z r know of what I speak. I have been to Pretoria and I do read Oni Land, and they tell us that du% Englishman, the voordomned Outlander, he will. Afight us. And did not our Field Cornet tell us so at Heidelburg ? But we have our Mausers, and ;.:we have our carriage-guns that go rap! rap! boom I 7.Ah, well, let the Englishman fight. We sbal idrive him into the sea and never more will yolP ; bring him here for Tanta to nurse, and never more ! will you be a fool after that, Jan Schultz. Yes, let ' him fight, and we will tell him of Amajuba, of Laing's Nek, of Ingorr°o, and we will drive him again from the land. Then you will be no more fool, Jan. You will see it is well for you to gee, much gold as the Outlander does. You will be rich man then, instead of driving your poor teBm. of ten oxen, getting little money, only just s'ill4 will keep you and the vrouw and the children ie billow and caffee." "I have all I want," replied Jan Schultz. "1 not want much. I am happy. Ido not like the Englesman, but one Englesman did me much good, Paul Schultz, and I do not forget, I do not forget. Ralph Percival remained at the Boer homestead' a fortnight. Tanta , Schultz, a pleasant, homelY body, tended him with care. She spoke to him ill broken English, but the invalid understood bet little of her speech. Paul Schultz came into 11,ie room each day, grunting his dissatisfaction as ne noted the Englishman's sure recovery'. Neverthe less, he told Tanta to let the rooinek have the best food the house contained. So touched was Ralph by the kindness of the, curious old Couple that, as he left them, tears ieL gratitude welled into his eyes and he broke dowe completely. "Paul Schultz," he said, as he grasped the hand of his rugged host, "I have not money to pay Yell for your kindness, but-" "I do not want money, Englishman," glufgl interrupted the Boer. "It is you and your cowl° men who always cry' Money! Money! Fre e eyes I Frank-eyes' !" (franchise). "How can I ever hope to pay you ? " "By thinking as kindly of a Boerman as all Englishman can. I want no money, I tell you." "I have a brother in Natal," continued Ralpb;, "He is a man, Paul Schultz. He is a missionary. The Boer shrugged his big shoulders. "If ever you come across him he will repay Yell for your kindness to me. I cannot; I can never hope, to do. My brother is a good man. He cal repay a debt to a good man. Good-bye, Paw' Schultz; good-bye, Tanta." And such was Ralph's leave-taking. Such expressions as he had just given utterance to were rare with him, hence the more significant. Being very selfish himself, he regarded all other people as selfish. His father, mother, sister, his old associates, Ella Rayburn, all selfish, hard, unkind, All with whom he had lately come in contact, with the exception of his brother Henry, were of this class. Yet here he had found utter strangers wile had treated him kindly and from wholly die" interested motives. He was much impressed, Henceforth the recollection of this incident had e softening, humanising effect upon him. For the first time in his life he felt grateful to another. i In Standerton Jan Schultz, with waggon loadee, up ready for the long trek to Heidelberg, await the Englishman. On this journey the Boer was accompanied by bi._: family, and Ralph was accommodated in the carte' with them. Then once again in the bright glare of the glorious sun the caravan moved slowly forward across the sea of veld. At every' outspan little Pretorius, Jan's eldest, a lad of fourteen, toot Ralph out on a shooting expedition, and with thie diversion Ralph did not grow impatient at the slog progress made day by day. At length Heidelberg was reached, and the waggon drew up in the market-place. Here, thirty miles from Johannesburg, Relit left his Boer friend. ...• . _</p>
        <p>"Tell me Jan Schultz," he said, before parting' "tell me why have you been so kind to me? I WO a stranger, I was penniless, I was sick." "You were an Englesman, that was enough, answered the Boer. "An Englesman, one of your prospectors, once saved my life. I was sick, I Noll like to die. He nursed me, he was kind. That hi all. But I never forget, I never forget. You are an Englesman. Perhaps you will be an Outlander soon and will fight against us, as brother Paul says. I don't know, I cannot tell; I am not learned. But I do not forget." "Some day, perhaps, I shall be able to pay your debt," said Ralph. "My life is not worth much, but if ever you ask me to give it for you or your country it is yours, Jan Schultz. That is the worc3 of an Englishman." "I want nothing. I want not your life. But it is well. I shall not forget. Good-bye, Engles- . _ man." "Good-bye, Jan. !here is my- name—Rale Cartleigh," and handing him a slip of paper Rallw hastened away.</p>
        <p>(To be continued.)</p>
      </div>
