        <head>TRADES UNIONISM.</head>
        <p>TRADES UNIONISM.</p>
        <p>Sunday's Demonstration at</p>
        <p>Warrington.</p>
        <p>At the invitation of the President (Mr. A C „.Binka), the members of the Warrington Trades and ,-4Rhour Council, and of the societies affiiated with it, attended divine service at St. Paul's Church, on Sunday afternoon. This is the firs:, time since the formation of the Council that :such an event has t,alren place.but considering the success of Sunday's! q,emenstration, it will no doubt become an annual 'unction. At 1.45 the members of the Council and Of 26 different societies assembled on the lawn in Iront of the Town Hall, and marched in procession is/9 way of Winmarleigh-straet, Palmyra-square, ,hez-street,Bridge-street,blersey-street,lrlam-etreet tiattermarket - street, Horsemarket - street and e_._wsey-street, to St. Paul's Church. The procession ",4s beaded by Ste Paul's band which Played selections in an efficient manner. The embers of the council followed, wearing rosettes 3?llieh bore the letters W.T.L.C. The officials pre- Were the President (Mr A C Binks), the Vic- ,Jesident (Mr J Edwardson). the Secretary (Mr .!yilliam Bullock), and the Treasurer (Mr James I.ittttersby). The members of the Amalgamated Lniol2 of Engineers came next and displayed a nvilutiful silk :banner. They were succeeded by the follly,Tving societies :—Vigredrawers, Bewsey Iron a linrzers, Sheet Metal Workers,Coach Makers, Boiler Coap.rs, Newsagents, Skip and Basket irners, Tailors, Bakers, Amalgamated Joiners, Frituinet Makertanhop Assistants, Wheelwrights, Bed- -6e4cl Makers, Assurance Agents, Gas Workers, 4Vlsral Labourers, File,miths, General Union of Ifaillers, General Railway Servants. Hairdressers, weawv an Servants, Engine and Bailer men, Wire Bak„eore,enti Amalgamated Society of Horsemen The and ociety was headed by the Borough Band eeveral large trade banners were carried. Mr. E. nston discharged the duties of chief marshall. eiarg`3 numbers of spectators witnessed the proms- ,ori 'which included about 600 or 700 Trades unionists and reached the church shortly before three o'clock. The service,which was conducted by the Vicar (Rev. E. R. Tarbuck, 8.A.), was of a most hearty character and commenced with the singing Of the hymn "Onward Christian Soldiers." The Choir creditably rendered the anthem "Behold how Rood and joyful a thing it is brethren to dwell to- Rather in unity," Mr. E. Wilkes, A,L.C.M., ably Presiding at the organ. The Rev. E. R. TAUBITCB preached an eloquent sennon, during the course of which he remarked that it had been said that economical and social 'questions were unpopular with the clergy. Ile lloubted the universality of such a statement even ti regarded the first three-quarters of this century. ",.hen they looked back upon the unselfish labours ?r clergy like Morris and Kingsley at a time when uqUstice to the working man was rife, and interfer- en .ree by any clergyman or philanthropist was full of danger, they had these men amongst others to Doint out, and to show them that the statement was prong when it was used universally. But it was a :et, hai no doubt that with some of the clergy tthese2, questions had been unpopular in times past, t"u Why? Because the clergyman of the Church 4ad in the past felt so strongly the import- flee of teaching and persuading men to ~ "eelc first the Kingdom of God and His ißhteonsness. We had felt like others before hiln that their wealth consisted in the firmness tlf their wants and that if a man lived a self-iniul- lent life he Must have mere wages or he must deny wife and family that which was necessary to steep them respectable and to bring them up ' Property whereas the virtuous man could be happy .t.'th lees. But that view thoueh not uneympathe- ..le, was perhaps what they should say out of per- ;hective—badly balanced. There was surely no teason why at any rate the virtuous man should not rewarded and have more wages if it was pO9- zl,ole to get them and that even the vicious man, if Ts work deserved an increase of wages,being a free should have a right to spend his money as he jelled and should have more wages if his work de- served it. Then there was another reason—which Probably the central reason. The clergyman , to go into a profession where the study of the trAie was all. in all. Consequently most of them up the study of classics in one of the older t niversities becatre dastical knowle.:lge was necessary Proper and full understanding of the Bible.lf he was a clarical scholar the orly thing at that time that "'allied to be o;)ea to him was mathematics. aut. whilst mathematics made him a correct ''esoner on main things it did no help ,t.,l4!•very much either in the study of the Bible or of 4.lai questione. They found, however, that within the or; 25 years the young meu who were training for Holy ti3Ots if they had not taken up classics as the main ,4,71,ct of their study to helo them to understand and tt'atu teach intelligently their Bible, they had gone in for vitlPral science, biology or mental and marat science tioleh directly b*re upon e•.-ery mint of sacial ques- t! ft? and on man himself. Finally there was the hatred th "4." immediate results of social upheavals. Be claimed 1&amp;.al. the Clergy of the Church had at least been the tr'4,4% in helping those wno through strikes or loss of t.,,410 or anything el that kind bad been in beell3Porary trouble hut Wry had shrunk from 11. R the leaders in agitating for erotiornic, social and thnltaty reform. They had a Uninn of their own in Ibte church. It was not a TradFs Union but went by the (Ili, of the Christian Social Union. The new Bishop raehl°,('rPonl was a member of the Union, and there were litte-,!,rt it of the ereatest preaching powers in the world Gore and Scett-Holland. The purpose of the Union as riot quite the same as that of the Trades Union. It att;tnot to agitate or to lead, but simply to learn and to the:.'Y mental, moral and social questions especially, and refore to understand and be able at any rate to Intel- Sympathise with the sada' questions of the day</p>
