        <p>EVERY CHILD born into the world with an inherited or early developed tendency to distress- ing, .disfiguring humours of the skin, scalp, and blood, becomes an object of the most tender solicitude, not only because of its suffering but because of the dreadful fear that the disfiguration is to be lifelong and mar its future happiness and prosperity. Hence it becomes the duty of mothers of such afflicted children to acquaint themselves with the best, the purest, and most effective treatment available.•</p>
        <p>That warm baths with CtrricußA SOAP to cleanse tho skin and scalp of crusts and scales and gentle applications of CUTICURA Ointment to instantly allay itching, irritation, and inflammation, and soothe and heal, to be followed in tho severest cases by mild doses of CUTICURA RESOLVENT are all that can be desired for the alle- viation of the suffering of skin-tortured infants and children and the comfort of worn- out worried mothers has been demonstrated in countless homes in every land. Their absolute safety, purity, and sweetness, instantaneensandgzatc.ful cure, and great economy leave nothing more to be desired by anxious parents. Complete External and Internal Treatment for Every 50..7 rl',Ar, consisting of CUTICUB.A SOAP, to cleanse the skin and scalp of crusts an scaler, and wilco' the thickened cuticle, CUTICUE.A Ointment, to instantly allay itching and irritation, aii-d soothe and heal, and Ctrrieunx RESOLVENT. to cool and cleanse the. blond. Tri SET, 65., or, SOAP, Is., OINTmENT, 26. 6d., RESOLVENT, 2s. 6d. Sold throughout tin, Or, postpaid of F. NEWBERY .t SONS, 27, Charterhouse Square, London, li. C. " Cure 13tiby Rumours," free. :POTTER DRUG AND cHEM. CORP., Sole Pror!;., -Boston,l</p>
        <p>TI,Nv tO A ..</p>
        <p>ano toe itSpiratiollS O jusLleti dile Loose whom were called Trade Unions. 'Deere would never be m .ch play- ing to the gallery and there would be less poll deal tub thumping, but there would be thy by day an honest and heartfelt, intelligent and increasirg I sympathy, with the social aspirations of the workingman and of the masters. Trades Unions were at first viewed by all except those. who joined them with a great deal of alarm because it was imagined that they were formed for the purptse of encouraging strikes. Now trades unions were not a new thing; they were a continuation and al improvement upon the old Trade Guilds, and their object was to help the weak, and to afford temporary relief. There were sick and burial clubs, labour bureaus, and they were to educate, and inform and to discipline members and finally and most important of all the die lies of those Trades Unions were to act as conciliation Boards. So the whole purpose put tegether of the Erodes Uafons was not to encourage but to avoid strikes. A groat change in the condition of labour brought about by the extremely rapid progress in the manner of manufactere and the distribution of the artislee of Commerce took place between the years 1760 and 1785. Tha.; was the time of the greatest pro- gress in the invention of improvements in the manu- facture and the distribution of manufactured articles. Men nn lorger congregatld near et,eame for water power, but in towns for steein power, and so here and there bodies of men through the factory system became assoeiated in labour' and this was the formation of the comparatively new trade unions. But as trade unions had gone on and Lied been approved by the steadist of workingmen, the natural consequence was that the masters had united into unions of one kind and aeother. He (the preacher) thought one of their chief reasons was not a sea% one, but to try end pre- vent underselling, and to keep up the price of an article' so that the wages and profits might be kept up likewise. They would all quite agree with him that strekes were a very brutal form of settling disputes, if ever they did settle them. Net only so' but a terribly costly scheme was started when a big strike took place. They would remember the great engineer- ing strike. Many of them knew from experience the awful co.t of that struggle between masters and men. It was said that at least £lOO,OOO Was lost in wages to working men. They all agreed without geing into details that it was one of the most costly ways of settling disputes that men could have thought of. Was there no other way ? James Sexton, one of their leaders was quite at one with him in very earaostly desiring that same other more humane way of settling disputes 'rather than by strikes should be found. It was his the preacher's) idea that they could have a national system of conciliation and arbitration. Our judges of the law were recognised as the fairest and least open to bribery and corruption of all the great law judges of the world whether in criminal or in other courts of justice. In a law court they bowed without 9uestioning, as a rule, to the decision of their greatest indges. Why should not there be a number of judges paid, and appointed to the post of arbitrator between masters and men in great trade disputes? Like James Sexton, he was not quite in favour of them being lawyers but he thought that they might be specialists in trade matters and if they were paid an adequate salary like our present judges, they would be above sespicion, he thought, of favouring one side more than another. Supposing they had halt- a dozen such men it would cost £25,000 in salary. Why they spent £lOO.OOO in wages in one strike alone. Perhaps it was thought that the working- men would not accept their decisions but they knew perfectly well that Ds strike was continued long, sac- easefully when the public opinion was against the continuation of it. He felt assured that when once these men made a proper examination of the ease on both sides,with the men's representatives as the special plead- er for the men and the masters' representative as their special pleader before the court,and a decision had been given by a couple of judges, public °Onion would sup- port it and there would be an end to the strike. From the masters' point of view the only objection he had ever heard was, that the masters would not agree to show their books and methods of management. The masters had to-day, if necessary to show their books to the income tax collectors or to the surveyor of taxes and that surveyor did not show the methods of business or the amcuets of capital to the public:. The same process would be followed by this new Court of Arbitration and he felt very much inclined to say that it would be successful when once it was tried. Trades Unions had made mistakes, so also had masters Unions. New methods adopted might not always be the best but it was an acknowledeed fact that progress was being 'made, that adverse decisions were borne with more patience, that both partial were working for England's prosperity and therefore there was a growth—a marked growth of mutual respect and a desire to fulfil their duties. They hoped that the Ruskin Hall Labour College, in Oxford, a new labour college established on the plan of many in America, might be the means of increasing the progress towards the ideal to which both masters and men in reality aimed. There were other genial problems that one would have liked to have spoken about but there was not time. He should like to have spoken about the queotion of Sundae Labour, a thine' that was very dear to him. He believed that matter of organization and consequent lessening of what was necessary to any Sunday Labour was entirely in the bands of the workinernen. The rev, gentleman con- cluded his dieentirse by apoealing for liberal contribu- tion to the offertory which was on behalf of the War- rington Infirmary. He said that the institution was the poor man's place of cure. • He dare say it was in the minds of some of them to say "But the Warrington In- firmary is not for Unionists." Partly that was correct and partly it was wrens!. Unionists as a rule had their own doctor and therefore did not require to go to the Infirmary but he was called Net night to a case that he had been attending for about a fortnight where the man was in at least one Union. But because it was thought probable by two doctors that an operation would be necessary the man was taken to the Infirmary. So the 7 would see it was not entirely a fact that unionists did not benefit directly from the Infirmary. It was a matter .for congratulation that quite a third of the total donations given to the Warrington Infirmary came from workingmen and their familiea. It would he hoped be increased to one half in time to crime, if he judged War- rington workingmen rightly. He had had some ex- perience of their generosity and so he felt that whatever they would give that el ternoon was given a. ith a heart full of sympathy fertile cause to which their money was to be devoted. The collection was thrntaken.and the service fittingly concluded with the singing of a verse of the National Anthem. The procession was re-formed and proceeded to the Town Hall where the secietiee dispersed. The amount collected was £4 51. 0.1:, which has been forwarded to the treasurer of the Infirmary. THE MOST NUTRITIOUS.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-05-18_WidnesExaminer_art0031" n="art0031">
        <head>, „up la. S</head>
        <p>, „up la. S</p>
        <p>GRATEFUL-COM FORTING.</p>
        <p>C*Ct A</p>
        <p>aREAKFAST AND SUPPER.</p>
        <p>A WARRINGTON GIRL'S TERRIBLE I DEATH.</p>
        <p>Crushed by a Swing-Bridge. A. shocking fatality occurred at Buttermilk Bridge, Sankey, on Sunday afternoon. It appears that a girl named Gladys Williams, six years of age, daughter of Seth Williams, a traffic foreman, in the employ of the London and North Western Railway Company, and re- siding at 82, Welltield-strset.went for a walk in company with her sister. On returtiing they crossed the swing bridge over the S. Helens Canal, know as Buttermilk Bridge. Some other girls were turning the bridge, and the deeelsed and her sister went back to get on the bridge while it was swinging. Deceased was stepping on the bridge when she was jammed ber,ween the eud of the bridge and the post. See was extricated by a Mr. W arburtoti but life was thin extinct. , Mr. S. Brighouse County Coroner, conducted the in- quest at the Warrington Police Station on Monday even- ing. Mr. J Bland, solicitor, represented the London and North-Western Railway Company. Seth Williams, 82, Welffield-street, traffic foreman in the employ of the London and North-Western Railway Company, said the deceased was his d auehfer, and was six years of aze. Be saw her alive at 2.53 on Sunday efternoon, and at about a quarter-past five the same eveoine saw her dead at home. The bridge was about half a mile from his house. A juror asked the witness whether he did not think the bridge ought to be fenced. Witneis said he had crossed it many times, and did not see what could be done. Maggie Williams (11), sister of the deceased, said that she and her sister left home about four o'clock on Sun- day afternoon. They went for a walk over the canal bridge at Sankey, but did not go far, and then returned to the bridge, which they crossed. Four girls who were at the opposite end ef the bri Igo commenced to turn it. One of them shouted to the deceased to go back, The ' girls then turned the bridge back, and before it elosse, the deceased, who was underneath the rail, tried to get on—the bridge being in metion—and was jammed beneath the edge of the bridge and the post. A man took her out and carried her borne. The Coroner remarked upon the admission of the girl that her sister and she turned back after having crossed the bridge safely. John Warburton, enginer, 4tl, Thewlis-street, raid that at half-past four on Sunday afternoon he was near the swing bridge, known as Buttermilk Bridge, and hie attention was called to some girls who had turned the bridge, and had left it unfastened. He Spoke to them about the danger of the practice, and tried to fasten the hook, but could not. At that moment tile last witness came to him and said that her sister was fast at the end of the bridge. He at once went and extricated her, but she was dead. He took the body to the house. 1 Hannah Dodd (12), living with her parents at 24, Percy-street,said that sheand her niece and two younger girl, went to Buttermilk Bridge on Sunday afternoon. They found the bridge open. There were four lads !here ' and one of them said," Let's have these girls on a batty." They turned the bridge. Witness and her compsnions turned it back, and afterwards turned it again for a bit of fun. Deceased's sister got on the bridge while it was swinging, and asked deceased to get on. It was in doing so that she was cradled. The Conner asked Mr. Bland if this bridge was con- structed as were other bridges on the canal. Mr. Bland said it was. Toe Coroner: It is absolutely necessary that bridges eheuld be made to open and to shot. Mr. Bland remarked that boatmen were passing all through the night. The Coroner: The danger arises when people begin to do what they have no right to do. Continuing, he said it was necessary that the bridge should be easily manipulated. Oi:herwise there would be difficulty in Conducting tbe traffic. This case seemed to come under the same category as those in which lads went to play near a pit and met with an accident. Children had done this sort of thing before, Wf r 3 doing it at the pre- sent time, and he supposed would go on doing it. It seemed to he a part of the English character that chil- dren should be courageous and do these sort of things, and now aad again ass accident occurred. The railway company had to obtain Parliamentary powers for the making of these bridges, and, as in the case of level crossings, the 'construction and working was regulated by Psoliament. A juror suggested that the sides of the bridge should be boarded up. It would not interfere with the work- ing of the bridge. Other bridges were protected in that manner' Mr. Bland said he would lay this matter before the engineer of the c ompany. ge felt certaiu that the com- pany would do aeything they ceuld,even if it were out- side their obligation. A juror remarked that a similar accident could not possibly occur if the place mentioned was boarded off' The jury returned a verdict of '"Accidental death*"</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-05-18_WidnesExaminer_art0032" n="art0032">
        <head>MR. CARR'S CASE.</head>
        <p>MR. CARR'S CASE.</p>
        <p>ASTON:3IIII\I,3 EVIDENCE.</p>
        <p>Proving that Dodd's Kidney Pills Positively Cure Bright's Disease.</p>
        <p>Until the discovery of Dodd's Kidney Pills, Bright's Disease had bafflod medical science. T oa.lay the sufferer need not lose hope; Dodd's Kidney Pills have cured hundreds of cases, and those cured have given grateful testimony to this effect. The following articaa recently appeared in the "Black- pool Herald." A replesentative of this paper gained the particulars of a very interesting case of recovery from that dread malady Bright's Disease. Mr. Daniel Carr, living at 19, St. Helier's-road, South Shore, gave the reporter the following facts: " I caught cold whilst at work, and was soon laid top with what, after a medical examination, proved to be Bright's Disease. I placed myself under tee care of a doctor,but medicine seemed to do me no good whatever, and it soon became impossible to move without clutch- ing at something for support. What I went through I canoot describe,-though I vivi,.3ly remember the pain that seemed to linger in my back,and made itself known on the slightest provocation. "I wasted away until I ould all but clasp the thickest part of my arm b?t xesn my finger and thumb. Fer eight weeks I was in the Manchester Hospital, and on coming out felt better, but before a week had passed had dropped back into my old condition. I was per- suaded to try a box of Dodd's Kidney Pills, and had only been taking the pills two months when I was able to go back to work. I have been working regularly now for the past six months, and am satisfied lam permanently cured." Dodd's Kidney Pills cure Bright's Disease, Diabetes, Dropsy, Rheumatism, Lumbago, Sciatica. Neuralgia, Lame Back, Blood Impurities, Gravel, Stone in the Bladder, Diseases of Women, and all other Kidney Troubles. Remember, Dodd's Kidney Pills CURE these diseases. There is no uncertainty about it. They CURE positively and permanently. Of all Chemists, 2s 9 1 pr box, or six boxes for 133 91; or sent post • free on receipt of price by The Dadds Medicine Co:, 23, Farringdon Avenue, London. E.C.</p>
        <p>Good Templars Convention.</p>
        <p>The Quarterly Meeting of the No. 1. Conven;-,ion of S.W. Lancashire Goo-1 Templars was held on Saturday last-, at Stubshaw Cross, Ashton. The Mission was opened at 4 p.m. Bro George Clarke, C.C.T., Earlestown, presiding over a fair attendance of deputies and representatives from lodges at Ashton, Earlestown, Warrington, St. Helens, Leigh, &amp;c. Credential and press committees were appointed.l after which the quarterly reports of officers were read, which gave an indication of activity in several districts, though in a few instances, slight decreases in membership were reported ; open air missions were urged as a means of strengthen- ing the lodges in the area. The financial statement. showed a fair balance in hand. sister Mrs Woo is, C.S.J.T., reported on the juvenile section, which like the edult, branches showed some Temples doing well whilst others had to report de- creases in their numbers, but which it, was hoped would soon be improved upon. Bro E Newman. D.S.J.T., reported arrangements in progress for a large children's demonstration in biverpool on June 16th, in which all the Temples were urged to join. A resolution was unanimously adopted in favour of a Bill to Prevent Sale of Drink to Children under 16 veers; also one in favour of Monmouth Sun iay Closing Bill, copies to be sent to members of Government and Colonel R Pilkington, M.P., for Newton Division. An expression of sincere sympathy with Bro A Lee, D.C.T, of Liverpool who was very seriously ill, was ordered to be sent through the secretary. The next meeting was fixed for Tyldesley on June 2ncl. Thanks to Ashton Ex- celsior Lodge for their arrangements brought the Session to a close at 5 40 p.m.Af ter tea apublic meet- ing was held when Bro T Wesley-Cowell, of Ashton I presided over a fair attendance, he being supported by Bro. George Clarice, 1).E.5., and Bro. E. Newman. D.S.J.T., of Earlestown. Earnest addresses were given by the Chairman and Bro. Clarke, and an excellent programme of musical and other items was well rendered. Resolutions in favour of pro- hibition of the silo of drink to children under 16 years of age, and Momouth S.C. Bill unanimously adopted.</p>
        <p>TO THII DRA.P.—A gentleman who cured himself of Deafness and Noises in the Head after fourteen 3 etre suffering will gladly send full par iculars of the remedy, on reo,eipt of stamped addressed eavelops.—F. Mum, Ankberle Haase, Strand, Loudon. CC.— ,dvt.</p>
