      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-05-18_WidnesExaminer_art0033" n="art0033">
        <head>FXAMINEII, FRWAY, MAY 19, 1900. OUR LOCAL CHURCHES.IDEATH AND FUNERAL OF MR. JOSEPH MOUNFIELD.</head>
        <p>THE FXAMINEII, FRWAY, MAY 19, 1900. OUR LOCAL CHURCHES.IDEATH AND FUNERAL OF MR. JOSEPH MOUNFIELD.</p>
        <p>Holy Trinity Church.</p>
        <p>The'annual sermoas were preached at the above place of worship on Sunday. At the mornin; service the Rev T. N. Carter occupied thi pulpi, and in the evening the Rev R. S. Mox )n preached. At a childro_fs service in the afternoon an address was given by the Vicar, the Rev E. C. E Carleton. All the services were well attended, and collections were made in aid of the Sun- day Echool funds.</p>
        <p>Early on Saturday morning there passed away a well-known and highly respected Warrington , tradesman in the person of Mr. Joseph Mounfield, cabinet-maker,of Buttermarket-street. The deceased gentleman was 78 years of age and retained his faculties up to the last. Even on the night previ- ous to his death he was in his timber yard con- ducting business and appeared in his usual state of health. He had not,however, thoroughly recovered from his illness during the winter and frequently eomplained of pains about his body. Early on Saturday morning he paesed away with startling suddenness, death being due to syncope. His wife predeceased him about eighteen months ago. Six sons survive him. For over half a century he had carried an business in Warrington as a cabinet maker, upholsterer and general furnisher, and had built up an extensive business. He commenced in a small way in the year 1843 at .premises in Church-etreet. Owing to the increased business larger premises were ulti- mately taken in Dutton-street, bat the business has been largely in the hands of his sons for many years past. He was a menaoer of the Friars' Green Inde- pendent Methodist Free Church and at one time or other had held nearly all the offices in connection with the church. Not many years ago he was pre- sented with a testimonial from the Sunday school in recognition of his 50 years' connection with it as teacher and superintendent. fits had for a number of years acted on the committee of the Independent Methodist Free Church denomination,and frequently represented Friars' Green Church at the conferences. At the time of his death he was treasurer of the church, an office he had filled for a number of years. • The funeral took place in Warrington Cemetery, on Tuesday afternoon. The service was conducted by Mr Thomas Bests!), and Mr Robert Ilenshall. The coffin was of fumed oak with brass mountings, and on an engraved plate was the inscription : "Joseph Mounfield, born February 27th. 1822; died May 12, 1900." The funeral was attended by a large number of the membirs and congregation of Friars'-green and Brick-street churches, for whom tea was provided in Brick-street school. In the brougham preceding the hearse there were Dr McCarthy, and Mr Thomas Barton and Mr Robert Henshall. The following relatives and friends occu- pied carriages :—First Brougham :—Messrs Joseph Nfounlield, George Mounfield, William Mounfield, John Mounfield, Charles Moutfield, Alfred koun- field (sons) ; 2ed, Messrs Arthur lount-101d, Harry Mounfield, Criarles Joseph Mounfield,Charles Mona- field, Stanley Mounfield, Frank Mounfield (grand- sons); 3rd. Messrs Wilfred Mounfteld. Harold Moan- field,Bowcock, William Twiss,Edward Twiss,Joseph Twise,4th. Messrs.W elliam Baswick,Joseph Beswick William FlOson, Thomas Holbrook an i William Wright; stheMessrs Thomas Shaw, Thomas Barton, John Critchley, S Dale. Brayshaw ; 6thl Messrs. Thomas Critchlev, Joseph Critchley, William Mason James Evans, James Hudson; 7th, Messrs Richard Hazeldine, Peter Lawton, Thomas Harding, Thom as Burton, and James Shaw; Bth, Messrs Joseph Mason, James Mason, Henry Eyes.</p>
        <p>, • **lK , ‘1</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-05-18_WidnesExaminer_art0034" n="art0034">
        <head>Gospel Hall.</head>
        <p>Gospel Hall.</p>
        <p>On Sunday ths preacher w.as Mr. J. Halliday, olf Pres- cot, who based his remarks on the story of the Israelites Passover in Egypt (2 Ex ram, 12), Gan's judgment had fallen thick on Egypt, and now Moses had got this me sane from God that death was about to visit the land. The gospel preachlr has to watn people of wrath, death and judgment. With the warning God tells of a way of escape, a lamb without blenaish must bs slain, its blood caught and sly inkled on the outside of the door for the eye of Gmel to see. Ho spoke of the perfect spotless Lamb of God, who died on pilvary's Cross, the just for us the unjust, His precious blood cln and does cleanse from all sin, naves us from eternal death and tits us for life in Heaven.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-05-18_WidnesExaminer_art0035" n="art0035">
        <head>Golborne-street Baptist Church.</head>
        <p>Golborne-street Baptist Church.</p>
        <p>Sunday School anniversary services were held at the Golborm-strcet Baptist Chapel on Sunday when the preacher at the morning and evening services was the Rev.R.D. Darby,of Leigh.A,:; the morning serv,ce the rev gentleman based his remarks upon the words "Feed my lambs" (John loci. 15), and reteired to th 3 duty and responsibility of teachers in the training of scholars. The'subject of the evening sermon was" The character of Heronias." In the afternoon a musical service was held, when the Rev. J.T. S3hos.eld (pastor) presided,and the Rev. R. D. D trhy delivered an inserective address on" The Arms of Jesus.", Hymes and recitations were given by the children, and solos were ably rendered by hiss Hayward and Miss Walker. The scholars, who were under the conductorship of Mr. Owen, sang ex- tremely well at all the services. Mr.F. Fernley presided at the organ. There were large congregations at all the services,and the collections amounted t £l4 123 1-13.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-05-18_WidnesExaminer_art0036" n="art0036">
        <head>Wycliffe P.S.A. Society.</head>
        <p>Wycliffe P.S.A. Society.</p>
        <p>The meeting en Sunday was presided over by Mr. George Wallington. In the course o a few remarks he reterred to the trades upion procession which had taken place in the town that afternoon and said he was very glad to see such a large number walking to church. He thought it was a very proper thing for trades ui o.ists to do. Whatever might be the differencs between inaoters and men, it was well to acktiowledge toot there was one Master above them all. Be noticed on one of the banirrs in the procession the motto" Bear ye one another' burdens." That was a grand motto and was in itself Christianity applied. He knew that many working men endeavoured to live up to that principle. There were many burdens to carry. They could not go thruugh the streets of Warrington without knowing that there was darkeess' sorrow, and trouble in their midst, and all of them could do some- thing to help one another. It might be only a kindly word or a little gift. They could not do much but he wanted them to have the spirit. We needed practical Christianity and that could only be realised inasmuch as the followers of Christ endeavoured to live and act as Christ did whoa on earth.—Mr. Pratt, ,of Manchester, delivered the address for the afternoon and dwelt on the importance of sopportino foreign missionary work. As a business man Who had travelltd in China and the far Evt he said he could bear evidence of the good work which the missionaries were doing. , There were, of course, black sheep amongst the mis- sionaries, as there were, no doubt, amongst the P.S A. societies. He believed as one who had seen the work of the missionaries and not as one who was paid to ttli them of it, that the money subscribed was being well spent and that God's blessing rotted on the work. Lanca- shire people, he pointed out, were kept busilY employed largely owing to the pe t pie of the East wearing their manufactured 2011 S. vBB their bounden duty to support missionary we doing so they would be • rewarded—Mr Farra secretary pointed out that although there loner y organisation con- nected with the P. o I the members supported missionary work. taut( time they might have a separate mission • their own.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-05-18_WidnesExaminer_art0037" n="art0037">
        <head>DEATH OF MR. W. MORTIMER J.P.</head>
        <p>DEATH OF MR. W. MORTIMER J.P.</p>
        <p>Tragic Occurrence in a Railway Carriage. Tne oeath ccurreci in Inadon on Fridac night et Mr. William Mortimer, J.P., of Beaelnhu,st, Fradsham. The deceased gentleman had gone to I.estadon on business and was staying at the Grand Hotel, Charing Cross. On Friday a friend of his lune.ed with him and he then ap- peared to be in the best of health. At night, however, he su,idenly became ill and was taken to the St. George's Hospital, but en exunination he was found to be dead. The news of his death reached Warringtm on Saturday morning and was reeeived with deep regret. The flag at the Conservative Club was hoisted at half mast as a mark of respect. The la!e Mr. Mortimer had been con- nected with Warrington and Orford more particularly for nearly the who!e .of his life. He was formerlv the proprietor of the Orford Tannery now controllei by a company ia which deceased was largely interested. He wag also associated with . several other tacning compani3s and business concerns in Warrington and :A tea. He was a Jestice of the Peace for the Warrington Petty Sessional Division. LsVerly he had not taken a prominent pare in public affaira but during his residence at The Cottage, Orford,he was promineritly identified with polities and took an active part in Parlia- mentary elecions. Up to the time of his death he was a mensly.r of the Eseoutive Committee of the Conner - vative AVOCi tion. The funerat took place on Wednes- day at Bick:rt ma Church. The Inquest.</p>
        <p>At the Westin' nrer C ironer's Court, on Tuesday, Mr. John froutb •ck held an inviry concerning the death of Mr. Wiliam M )rtinier, age i fifty-nine -years, a tanner, lately residine at Beacon Hurst, FroJsiaana, Cheshire, and staying at the Otani Hotel, Inndon„ who died while travelling in a train from Denmark-hill to Victoria, on the 11th inst.—M Alexander Mortimare, of Alnwick House, Catford, Kent, stated that deceased was a friend of his. Oa Friday last •witness lunched with the deceased at the Grand HotA, Charing Crass, and left him, appar- ently ii the best of health outside the hotel.—Miss Edith Bruce-Johnson. of Buskingham Palace-gardens,said that on the 11th last., she had lunch with Mr Mot timer at the Grand Hotel. She afterwards walked with him to Reger.t-street and Band street, and they rode in a cab from Piccadilly to Victoria' whence they travelled by train to Denmark-hill. They had tea at the house of a friend, and returned to town by the 624 p.m'. train. They bad just left Wandsworth-road Station when Mr. , Mortimer had a sudden seizure. Witness at once bad the train stopped, • but it was decided to take Mr .Mortimer onto Victoria. He was quite uncoase,ious. —The Coroner : Were there tie restoratives to ne ob- tained P—Witness N) ; even at a station like Batter- sea-park the .tationma4ter said there was no brandy on that line. Miss Bruce-Johnson went on to say that Mr. Mortimer had shown no previous sign of illness. At Victoria he was placed on an anabulancNand conveyed to Sc. George's Holpitali—Mr. W. B. Winton, house phy- sician at the hospital, spok3 to admitting Mr. Mor- timer, who hid not been long dead. A post modem ex- amination revealei that the heart was enlarged, and the lungs shownd signs of bronchitis, and contained an ex- cess of floid. The heart bad suddenly failed after Some slight stress of an unuitaal character. There was no in- flammation of the er:ril system: A verdict in accord- ance with the medical evidence was returned. At tha Warrington County Petty Seisions on Wearies , day, prior to the commencement 01 the business, the presiding :Innis rate (Mr. Rigby Armitage) said the Bench wish-cl to express their deep regret at the sudden and sad death of a late member of the B,nch, Mr. Wil- liam Mortimer.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-05-18_WidnesExaminer_art0038" n="art0038">
        <head>Warrington Markets.</head>
        <p>Warrington Markets.</p>
        <p>At the Warrington Corn Nlarket on Wednesday wheat (white) fetched 3s 101 (70)b to the bushel) ; red wheat 3s 91 ; oats, 2s 51 ; flour (best seconds) per 2401 b., 19s ; bran, Is pe-: 21b. At the Provision Market prices were as follow:—Fresh butter, is 2d and 1,4 3d per lb ; frHsh egv, 14 for Is ; cheese 8d and 94 ; fowls, 4-4 61 to 53 64 per couple ; English beef, 61 to Oil ; mutton 71 to 101 ;lab, is; veal, 7d to 10d; pork 6,1 to 80. Another Daring Burglai4. - -</p>
        <p>A short time :cgo w-3 refer Ted to a-daring burglary in Rood-lane, Sankey Bridges, where entrance was rjained t,-1 a house by the back window, and a sum of money stolen from a bag. Another burglary of an equally daring character took place last Friday night at the premises of Mes,srs. Clare ,and Ridgway's, slate ant timber merchants, Winwick- street. There must have been sev-ral thieves on this occasion for the window in the side of the office had been taken away, and the safe, which weighs 7cwts., had been carried into the yard. Attempts had evidently been made to force it, but it is probable that the thieves were disturbed, for they lett without succeeding in their intention. The safe was found in the yard on Saturday morning. Nothing else in the office had been disturbed. There seems to be little doubt that the town is be- ing worked by a Rana of thieves, and the police have the cases mentioned in hand.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-05-18_WidnesExaminer_art0039" n="art0039">
        <head>LIBERAL UNITY. An Appeal to the N.L.F.</head>
        <p>LIBERAL UNITY. An Appeal to the N.L.F.</p>
        <p>The Executive Committee of the National Liberal Federation, in torwardiug to the Federated Associations a report of the proceedings settle Nottingham meetings of the Federation, hive addressed the followine obser- vations to each asseciaeinn : While the annual meetings make it plain that on some aspects of the war in South Africa there is considerable difference of opinion within the Liberal party, it was also demenstrated at Ntling- ham that in this matter Liberals can agree to differ,being at the same time resolved to uni e on the many other questions where no differencea arse among Us. The unanimity which marked the proceedings at the second session of our Council was btrikiog and significant. Liberals were never more at one than they are to-day on those questions of domestic and social reform which divide the two great parties in the State. On subjects registration and electoral reforin.the lionee of L mds the Laud Laws and the housing of the working classes, temperance reform, religious evality, the popular con- trol of elementary schools, and era many other issues, the Liberal party is as keen and united in favour of reform as the Conservative party is resolu, e and agreed against it. The Executive Com- mittee invite your association to strive to the utineit that , this unity on general political issues shall not be impaired oy diff ,rences of opinion connected with the war. The note struck at the Nottiagham meet- ings was one of eager desire for unity, and soneeteing may be done to re.alise that desire if Liberals will remem- ber that the war will come to an end, but that the Con- servative party and the vested interests remain herrale and implacable on all the great domestic reforms which it is tee duty and prigilege of Liberalism to advance. For the moment, and in view of that appeal to the coun- try which must come at the latest neat year, and may come sooner, the committee nage the associations to do all they can to ensure (1) good registration this snmmer (the usefulness of which, it only in tracing reeneval.a, will not he lost even if Parliammt is dissolved before the new register came into fore 9, and (2) the utmost pos- sible inaprove,ment in local organisadon. 'Witheut regis- tration and organisation it is impossible to record eur , strength at Weapons. Enthusiasm can do much, bus it cannot win elections unless our supp;rtecte have been previously placcl upon the register, and our organise- tiou in the constituencies has been so perfected as to ensure that the Liberal vote is fully polled. To this preparatory work we most earnestly invite all the asso- ciations at once to apply themselves, and we should esteem it a favour if you would bring this communica- tion to the notice of your felloW-offieers and committee on the earliest opportunity which presents itself.</p>
        <p>Presentation to an Old Latchford :Stationmaster.</p>
        <p>A very interesting ceremony took place on Friday last week iu the General Waiting-room, Lime-str.et Station, Liverpool, the occasion being the presenta- tion of a testimonial to Mr. Joseph Harrison, who after eight years' faithful service as deputy station- master at Lime-street terminus of the London and North-Western Railway Company, has been promo ted to the position of stationmaster at Edge-hill: Mr J B Leach (St. Helens), occupied the chair' and others in a large gathering were Messrs. • Oscar Van Wart, R. F Gladstone, Blackburne, T. Lowtou, di. S. Gibbson, R. Cros, Donald Potts (hon. secretary), and George Plummer,(hon. treasurer), Ur. Peter Stuart, A. R. Noreoan,Runcom and a number of officials of the London and North- Western Railway Company.—The presentation, which took the form of a gold watch, chain, and pendant, and purse of gold, accompanied by a gold bracelet for Mrs. Herrison, was made by the chair- man, who. in fitting terms, eulogised the excellent quelities,business as well as social, of Mr. Ilarrion. The recipient,_who commenced service with the London and North-Western Railway Company, thirty-two years ago as a junior porcer at Haintord, near St. Helens, had won the esteem and regard of both the travelling public and his subordinates. The following inscription was engraved on the watch case :—" .tie Mr. Joseph Barrison, deputy stationmaster for eight years at Lime-street. Given by travellers as a mark of esteem for uniform at- tention to all classes of passengers using the London and North-Western Railway, 1900." Mr Harrison made a grateful response, and the function ter- minated with votes of thanks to the hon. secretary and hon, treasurer, the chairman, and Mr Debt, the Liverpodl superintendent of the railway, for grant- ing the use of the waiting-rom.—Many people in Latchford and Warrington will well remember Mr. liarrison as the stationmaster at Latcnford eight years ago. We congratulate him on again earning and obtaining promotion.</p>
        <p>When asking for Cocoa, insist on having CLDBURYS Sold only in Packets and Tins as other Cocoas are often Bohai 1- tt tad for the sake of extra profit.—Adrt (1)</p>
        <p>ALL COUNTRY PEOPLE should read The Rural World H. weekly, which contains articles or 'ft,. sea, Cattle, this) Dlir,r, Poultry, Bees, Rabbits, Pigs, Gardening, Natural 'History, Botany or the PArm and Garden, the home, Foreign awl Colonial Intelligence. Anwers to Correspondents, etc. It is profusely illustrated and is altogether, a most interest- ing and instructive farming awl country paper. A good medium for servants requiring situations. The Manager offers to send a specimen tree. The address Is 110 and 11a. STRAND, London, W.—Advt.</p>
      </div>
