      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-05-18_WidnesExaminer_art0041" n="art0041">
        <head>An Episode.</head>
        <p>An Episode.</p>
        <p>{BY GODFREY DEAN]</p>
        <p>The sky was of a dull, leaden colour, lending a heavy, oppressive hen-. to this late South African ! summer day; the landscape of a dreary sameness, ' which seemed rather accentuated by parallel lines of kopjes stretching away into the distance. Trooper Kenyon of4the Imperial Yeomanry on scouting duty, and alone, pondered over the farce of stalking nothing : the buzzing of insects was the only sign of life. He had approached the kopjes— tee most dangerous part of his work—and after securing his horse from straying, had left it hidden at the first line of hillocks. A mile of this ground 1i had traversed without interruption, but with watchful carefulness, and now was thinking of returning. A shot rang out. A narrow escape ! He felt the rush of wind past his cheek. His strang nerves gave way: he cursed spiritedly ; and thanked God the moment after.</p>
        <p>The enemy could hopt, evidently; it was also clear he was wide awake. It would be a duel,then, in which skill and wiliness would be the deciding factors. There W 3.9 not much latitude for luck. The shot had come from his front, a little to the right. As Kenyon was the challenged party, he must send his acceptance. He spat the dust frem his mouth ; end laboriously wormed his way to the left, where the ground gradually rose. This done, he raised his body and got on his knees ; then, placing his headgear on the barrel of his rifts, ne brought it int) sight of the enemy and awaited developments. _</p>
        <p>The ruse was fruitless of result. Throwing his left leg forward, sole to the ground, and resting on his other knee, Kenyon, with his gun at the ready, prepared to show himself and take a snap shot. Lie raised hie head and shoulders and glancing in the direction from which the shot had come,sighted rapidly and fired at the dim outline of a figure, immediately dropping to earth again. Atter remaining behind cover for a few minutes the Yeoman, sanguine of the success of his shot, slightly changed bis position a couple of yards to the right, and looked for signs of his foe; only to receive a bullet in his shoulder, as a reward for his indiscretion. From the sound of the shot the Boar had altervd his position somewhat.</p>
        <p>The wound was simply through the flesh, the bullet having sped on without touching the shoulder bone. stung with irritation at being out-manceuv- red in this fashion, as much as with the pain of the wound, Kenyon, after slipping to the ground, daringly showed himself again. This time both rifles spoke and the trooper again took cover, with- out knowledge of the f-fbct of his shot, lie himself had received a second wound; this time in the ribs.</p>
        <p>Convinced be had but one foe to deal with. Ken- yon somew':at painfully changed his position behind a huge boulder and for Some LIMO', while only show- ing a fraction of his person, vainly endeavoured to locate his astute enemy. His blood was fired with the conflict—hie tirs-, experience of war—and his apparent ill-success only aroused his pertinacity. He would be ju,tifiod in returning to camp, for his Boer foe w,te no doubt one of the scouts thrown out by a force near by ; and this information obtained, his duty was ended for the time being. But his personal honour was not satisfied. He was determined this affair should he d outrance. Either a British or Boer home would be one member less, perhaps both—who could tell? The Imperial instinct was strong in him. Ho was proud of his uniform and the circumstances under which he served, lie WA,, conscious of a feeling that this was a.trial which would effectually show the spirit of his comrades of th Imperial Yeomanry. He knew their hopes and f,4tlings. They burned to prove they hsil vlunt.eerPtl for service in no spirit born simply of enthusia:m or emulation ; they were a:ire to tie', ,--enou,nt.,s;, of the step they had taken, and were thril'ed with the pleasure of being chosen to fight for Queen and Empire. These reflections etnly vaguely passed through his mind. lie felt, rather than thought, all this. Guarding the lines of communication had been the lot of the imperil Yeomanry up to the present, but *.;hey had diipirwtql such a st,ady aptitude and serious sense oft heir duty that they were now being used for offensive measures.</p>
        <p>lienyou's wound ia the ribs was not by any means serious. but it would lessen his activity, Re once more warily looked forth trom behind his cover. Nothing was to be seen of the Boer,and he wondered ;f after all his enemy might not be seriously wounded.</p>
        <p>Still careful of exposing his person,Kenyon slowly made his way forward, resting from time to time when he came to particularly good cover. In this way, he travelled some distanCe,not exactly straight forward brit considerably nearer where he judged his foe to be. At last, atter assuring himself that hi 3 foe was not in sight, and wishing to get into another line of kopjes some yards distant, from which his obser- vation would be bettered, Kenyon stepped into the open. At that instant the Baer Cams into view at short range. The surprise was mutual. Both firei at once, The Boer apparently was untouched; but Kenyon got a bullet through his lungs. He telt the end was near. His blood would stain South African soil as a sacrifice, but his death, with others, would he an indelible reminder of what the Queen's subjects would do for tha cause of their pire. The wounded Yeoman shook off the weakness , which threatened to overpower him and, raising his rifle, once more fired at his foe, who dropped on one knee and returned the shot, but without result.</p>
        <p>Kenyon fixed his bayonet and advanced steadily. gpirit, triumphed over flesh—his mind dominated his physical weakness, holing even death in check. His burning eyes were fixed on his foe, who with curious, almost fascinated, look watched his approach. seemingly without the power to move. The Boer painfully moved his leg, still with his eyes fixed on the mortally wounded "rooinek." The distance between them grew less and less. Into the Boer's eves crept a wild, scared look, as the Yeoman persistently advanced. Fear succeeded. Twice the Boer raised his rifle and fired, yet failed to stay the progress of the Yeoman. His nerve was shaken by the appearance of his foe coming on with such re- lentless determination.</p>
        <p>Terror seized him with a nearer view of the gleaming bayonet. He was incapable of getting away; volition seemed to have left him. Once more he fired at his foe, but though the bullet lodged in the Yeoman's ,body the latter only paused and immediately resumed his advance. Only a few yards now separated him from the mad "rooinek." He staggered to his feet. He could have yelled aloud. A step nearer—another—one more—the bayonet lunged; he made a wild attempt to parry with his Mauser, and es the steel sank into his both, the Boer fell back with a sob of pain. The Yeoman stood erect for one brief moment. The blosd gushed through his mouth and nostrils, then be fell across the body of his foe.</p>
        <p>Imperial Yeoman and Boer burgher lay together British soldier and worthy foe! Their blood welled out into one stream on the South African veldt, symbolical of the future fusion of two races.</p>
      </div>
