        <p>SPRING CLEAN3NG COMPETITION.</p>
        <p>We have received such a number of really good parodies on Kipling's celebrated verses that we should like very much to give the prize to several thaens of them. As that, how- ever, is impoEsible. we hve hao to make a choice and have decided upon which we think is really the most appropriate. We publish this one, and also a selection of the others, not adjudging them the best of those which h..ve been sent ifl. but as a representative selection. We desire to thank all those who have taken the trouble to send us contributions towards this competition. 1? 3 was AWARDED to EDWARD 11. DAVIS, Harborne, for the following verses t—BOOTS ADVICE to II01:E WIVES.</p>
        <p>When you're started with Spring Cleaning—and you want to do it clean</p>
        <p>(And you know you ce.nuot do it With your mouth)— Will you kindly buy a bottle of our • k urnite vvrer Cream. Which is used in Haat and West and North and South. It's a powerful kind of compound, and it's very good to mix, And you and Jane can take it as you find it. When his used in active service, cleaning stains from off the " sticks,'' You will find it wont leave many marks behind it. Boots shop, Boots shop, shop of a hundred things, An enterprising Company, its all Lhe rage to-day, hverything sold for the lowest cash (as you can all see for yourselves), Then step in ladies, take your choice, and pay ! pay / pay There are wives who do it secret—asking no permission to For their husbands wouldn't give it if they did; But suppose you use oar "polish"—it will make things look - like new—</p>
        <p>And you nted not stop the work " cos " there's a kid. You could do your cleaning casual—without worry—whea he's gone, For if the beggar's absent never mind him. You might use our "Wills' Ammonia," which would take away the smell Of tobacco—which ha atways leaves behind him.</p>
        <p>Boots shop, Boots shop, etc. You can manage so as later, when you look him in the face, You can tell him what he'll think is only fair, How that while he earned the wages you had tidied up the place And had polished up his special old arm chair. He's an absent-minded begg,r, and he may forget it all. But you do not want his hi dd es to remind him That you locked them in the coal house while you gleaned his house and hall, And were hright'aing up the home he'd lett behind him. Boots shop, Boots shop, etc.</p>
        <p>When you've started on your cleaning, when you're scour- ing all your roorw, Use " Ammonia, Cloudy Cleansing," made by Wills, You can buy it for a shiilihg, and yoa 11 find its pleasant fumes All the dirt nd noxious germs quite quickly kills, Boots' an advertising beggar, but his stores are very great, And 1 and you must take him as we find him, He'd sooner have your cash than put it en a slate. And he's leaving all the other shops behind him. Cooks Boots, dukes Boots, Boots of a hundred kings. Boots of a market public in,he'll fit them all,we say; With Varnish, or Cream. and Paint, and thousands of Other things, Ask for them all for your cleaning's sake and pay. Pay, pay. There are girls who make no secret,'cos they'veliao occasion to. Boots Cream to polish furniture is good, That Gold Paint as made by Morgan is the finest thing that you Can gild upon old ormolu or wood. There are girls you talk to cAsual, will assure you that they love To polish up their brasses, and their tins, With Paste that's sold at Boots, so much better, and above All others, coveting multitudes of tins. Cooks' sins, dukes' sins, sins of a belted earl, Fifty thousand soldiers' sins, gone from thein astray, Buttons and buckle, and gun, will shine our khaki girl, Buy it quick for your polishing's sake, to-day, day, day. There are families by thousands using Booties .Oaken Stain To furbish up bare spots "round their fl on, it saves them yards of carpet, for it will wash again When vanished well to close the open pores. You can polish hp the chairs with the Cream they sell Boots— You need not go to Africa to find him. He has so many branches, from scores of *go.si old roots, He is mostly ,t the front, nor Loks behind him ! Dukes' Cream, cooks' btain, Polish for metals, (fold Paint, These are the things you will ask for going tO • Boots to-clay, With Ammonia, Cloudy Cleansing, to save you from a faint, Take yoar cash in your pocket end pay, pay,pay. Nom de plume—JACOß W 1 LbON, Nottingham.</p>
        <p>If your slavey goes and leaves you when spring cleaning'e coming on, And you cannot get anolier maid that suits. I will tell .iou how to manage with little extra tail, Just walk off to tne neare,t shoo of ,OOTS. They are civil and obliging, you will very quickly find, Thcir goods are cheap and freeh and up to date And you'll buy for say one shilling what most firms would sell for three, As they trade for cash and do not keep a slate. Pure Drug., Gold Paint, Furniture Cream and Pills. Stuff tor saving elbow grease, powders and various roots, Stains and Polish of every kind, things that will cure all ills, So cease to fret, but put on your shoes and just try BOOiS. If you ',think your cha!rs and tables get a little worse for wear, And the :ddeboard is not what it used to seem, Don't break your cups raid B,u:era and say you wish you're dead. For 4d. only need be spent on Cream. With a rag apply it gently, as.y chi,d can put it on, It's as easy if you want to stain the floor. Spend another 4id. on a pot of Varnish S sin, Then your home will look just as it did of yore. Soft Soap, Brunswick Black, Paste for a hundred tine, Oils and Paints of every kind, Cloudy Ammonia. too, Each of them certain todo its work, just as you're told it will, So keep home clean for your credit's sake end you'll not rue. When you've finished in the bedroom, and the parlour end the ball. Although yosere well rep ild do-.'t think you're done, There's the p its and pans and kettles want a little clean- ing out, So again to BOOTS you'll have to son ; Get a tin of Metal Polish, just one penny does the trick, No stains or scratches you will ever trace. Then you'll sing about your housework and be glad you are alive, And in everything you'll see a smiling face. Cash Chemists, Good Chemists, things at the lowest rate. Everything superior for a price th t always suits, 50,000 housewives sing p,aise of one accord, And never deal with another firm but BoOTifs BOOM BOOTS. F. JOHNSON, 4, Victoria-rd., Penketh, flair Warrington.</p>
        <p>When the Springtime surreptitiously steals on us unawares With a sudden liitht of pale and sickly sun, Then the prudent British matron takes to polishing the stair", And there's lots of other house-work to be done. So in order that the mansi,,u rni-ly be thoroughly made brlglAt BOOTS VARNISH STAINS and other things are bought. And the hubby's consolation is in thoughts of speedy flight. For he doesn't understand things Ks tie ought. Rub, rub, scrub, scrub. ain't it awful work. So the hubby thinketh, though he really doeStil know One little bit about it when he fancies terrors lurk, And the best thing he can do is t, go. go, go. Oh, the saving in the coal bills with the corning of the Spring. When the blank bacteria wobble in theli flight, And the income tax collectors and the birds begin to sing. And the dirty smo;cy cAlitigs turn to white. If you view it as you ought to it's a most congenial time, With UNRIVALLED METAL P,•LISII to the fore. In burnishing door handles and removing all the grime From the metals while the women scrub the floor. Rub, rub, scrub, scrub, :It-tidal' things look like FURNITURIT: CR2 A Mand BRUNSWICK BLACK are t. articles that pay, Buy all you want at Boots, you'll keep that well i° View? And do your work when hubby's out of the via. way, way. When the work is nearly over there's a transformation sceoe• And you don't regrst you've tii,-;kied it in time, As you realize the fact that now your hone is nice and cleiltit To have left it dirty would have been a crime. vt'f Oh I the glory of the carpets which mow look as good as Ile Oh I the brightness oF the fenders and the chairs, It's surprisina to the hubby as he takes a startled view. And the wife enjoys his wonder as he stares. d 9? What. what! what, what done the whole lot in a (Heaven seems to open to the guilty man scoots) Wonderful ! how did you do it? Tell me your 002.4 pray, ootS• " I did it fill with things I bought from r Boots, hoots." C. F. JONITS, Solicitor, 6, St. Andrnws-street, Cambridge'</p>
