        <p>THE time has now arrived Li beral when questions affecting the Unity. future must have precedence iilsl'er Past disputes and divisions. Liberals who ave been unable to agree about the origin of the must see the wisdom of standing shoulder to 'enider to secure the final and equitable settle- it. They have been more generally in har- wttielaY on this point than on the antecedents of the rand hence they can put aside the "dead and begin to act together in the "living Z1;6844." The war is coming to its decisivJ wqaPters, and it is evident to everybody that it t etild be imprudent to leave any opening for the beeetirrence of war on any of the issues which have en prominent in the controversy. Some regard list be paid to the general principles of Liberal- ,and to the enormous crop of subjects which is Le.elleaulating for future harvesting. Prebably the thlberal party may only have an indirect,voice in e re-adjustments necessary in South Africa. But must depend on their own unity, and on the et in which they act. Regard must be had to sacrifices imposed upon the country, upon ellth Africa, and upon our loyal colonist who 84Ve been so splendidly working with us in the ;attlefield. In time we shall be able to heal tb.e tietlads created by war, and to reconcile the domi- t.allt Power of Great Britain with the local liber- 411es of the defeated populations. The Liberals it,?l,"t be called upon to devise the settlement 80155 e of them may sincerely regret the nut there are others who will be glad to be Zlieveci of the burden of the work. When the isearli:4l leetion comes the policy to be adopted riot e-c'st certain to have been fully outlined, if Liberaand it will be the duty of tie nat.rstle:ce d, Put themselves in harmony with national sentiment, to approve oi a spirit of recon- trilatioti iii South Africa, and to make the best aetical use of the lessons of the war in reorganis- vng the War Office and the army, Regulars and °lllnteers. But there are many other questions be considered, and they will have to be kept „Well in view. These questions touch finance, local ation, social reforms, and the security of rzntestantism. It would be the height of folly tf.clis allow matters so grave to be pushed into background. The country will not be re- lvtla.lted to simply give a verdict on the past. It 4111 he invited, and in duty bound, to give a man- a Commission for the future. It will per- that a policy of peace and reform, of concen- taratlon and of self-preservation, will be abselutely theeesearY. The country must express its will on e general lines of future foreign policy and 11:1111esti0 reform. Adventure in the one means agnation in the other; and both will be bad for • e°lnmercial, moral, and social interests. Unity ,anwile indispensable condition for effective pres- ,h-re and for intelligent propagandism. Compre- Ahellasive Liberalism will be the result of this unity, .tl7 all Imperial and domestic problems will be .t ?better for it. Present signs are indicative of a ~,11.11.1t of co-operation and mutual good will. It . tt,i4ti,ll. in the power of politicians who have been wit:le,al or indifferent to rejoin the bodies with itifien they have been accustomed to act, and to ptlec into them new life and the desire for cora- Isive reform. We cannot stand still in this 1),:fi• l/trY because there is good and brave work to etle in South Africa. Any persons so arguing kicive ceased to be worthy of public confidence. The century will need to be entere.l upon witha a.ci,tl belief in intellectual, moral, and social elitrve'lleeraent. Liberals must determine, despite th, (nit depression, to have their rightful share in °od work; and they must, therefore, re- pt I:4e themselves now, and subordinate differences e, 14(10:lent on one topic to agreement on general 4eatiexis.</p>
        <p>THERE are some indica- rifyi n g the tions that public opinion 'Stage. the is being moved towards loeic Purification of the stage. They will be °e:led everywhere, not because it is admitted at th bee e stage is worse now than formerly, or 1)11:118,13 all the severe things said about it are true, te,; eeoause attention ' can be wisely directed aetialle a revision of some public representations CprT.ti Bharp eye should always be kept upon the OrasesPondence between public feeling and Ditt:latie art. It may be easy to select a few ;14 aod performances, and to found upon them AtiZeZW indictment of the severest character. 114:"is iS not the way to reform anything. There Ital; been some doubtful plays of late, and the ""erra a correct estimate of them is to go etiti,,:ilLee them, and not to be content with reading th,b7ele upon them or reading the plays A1a.:146e% The dramatic censor reads the 4t3•7 fitrikes out passages, and goes to aelutheel represented. Yet in course of time el these productions are so transformed by that" actors and actresses put into them Roodtile,ir bad points are exaggerated and their enbi,„"3`ete are obscured. In the selection of taths-C8 the tendency has been of late to take to 4vent.r.,iallY matt and treat them skilfully, so as tiors -1' any glaring afonce. We shall .not men-. 447 names, either of dramatißtal plays, or</p>
        <p>performers, and so our allusions must of necessity be general. But we hardly think the statement just made will be seriously challenged. Some of these plays are original and others are adaptations, and perhaps the blame may be equally divided be- tween them. The duty of the censor is to be dis- criminating and fair, and to liberally interpret what is reasonable and proper. A narrow way of looking at life would unquestionably shut out some of our classic plays—plays in which broad humour, delicate situations, and smart dialogue abound. On the other hand, there are plays which familiarise the playgoer with types of character and codes of manners by no means common to this country, and it cannot be denied that their pro- duction must have a degrading eftect, alike upon the persons taking part in them and the public which goes to see them. Such plays may well be forbidden. They never do good to anybody. They do not enable anyone to uunderstand life any better, or to be healthily interested in the evolution of human character. Their effect is depraving. Some of what are called problem plays do not fall into this category. They portray some part, if it be only a limited part, of current life, and they may be helpful. The semi-serious plays, however, by no means exhaust the list, if they may be described as more seductive. There are comedies and lighter pieces over which the eye of the censor needs to be constantly cast. Many are rejected, but some escape censure, and do not improve in setting and in the " gag " allowed to well-known comedians and actresses who cater for he gallery. It is constantly said that persons who want to purify the stage should become theatre- goers, and manifest disapproval at unpleasant jokes situations, and productions. There is something in this view of the case. Dramatic criticism might be more independent than it is. The voice of the people in the press might be more often heard. Public opinion can readily keep down the sugges- tive and immoral if it will but exert itself. Where theatres are municipal property there is a con- venient method at hand of controlling a lessee. With other aspects of the stage the public need not concern itself, and condemnation may easily pass into injustice. The dramatic profession has its perils, but the public is under no necessity to go about prying into private lives and professional morals. Tee desire to purify the stage is laudable and wise, and it cannot be done without discussion and discrimination. The stage is very much what we make it, and what we allow it to he.</p>
        <p>It has now been established beyond all doubt that Mr. Isaac Gordon did not accumulate wealth to the extent he was reputed to have done. A balance of £125,000 over and above book debts, which amounted to £400,000, was net a large sur- plus for so colossal an undertaking. It must be remembered that he had one hundred and fifty branches up and down the country, and had been turning over cash at all of them very rapidly in recent years. He w:..s expected to have died a millionaire, but on the most optimistic estimate of the value of the debts his , estate will not show a balance to the amount of more than half that sum. It is reported that his brothers, one of whom is a Rabbi, are somewhat embarrassed as to the disposition of the estate which falls to them through his intestacy. Two of them intend to settle in England, and, as they have reluctance about benefiting out of this peculiarly-made capital, one of them, according to a correspondent contemplates the erection of a House of Prayer with a moiety of his portion. It might be kindlier perhaps, if the brothers were to forgive the most deserving debtors. This perpetuation of the Gordon memory by a synagogue will be mystifying to future generations. Nobody envied the money- lender his shekels. He had to pass from hotel to bete', so unwelcome was his presence at the table. He died prematurely trom the worry of his en- gagements, which precipitated the attack ®f con- sumption; and, having regard to the extent of his schemes, he was in doubt almost to the end whether his fortune would not melt. His brothers regret his career, and the best thing they could do would be to divide the proceeds of a considerable realisa- tion between themselves and the Liberator Relief Fund. The gift would leave no trace behind.</p>
        <p>It is thoughtful of the British Medical Journal to inform benighted provincials how to free the towns from mosquitoes. Certainly the cost for a town of 50,000 inhabitants—E4o to £6O a year— will not alarm the ratepayers ; only the trouble is that just at present .we have no mosquitoes, and are not in any pressing hurry to entertain them. There is something in the ancient proverb, how- ever, "Forewarned is forearmed." They have had mosquitoes in London during the phenomenal heats of the last two summers, and we have even had them in Latchford and, given similar weather conditions, it is on the cards we shall have them again, in increasing multitudes. It will be a testi- monial to England's weather when mosquitoes multiply in the provinces as well as in the capital; but it is the kind of testimony one would rather be without. Where the mosquito settles you may find fevers and plagues—as well as a decidedly irritating puncture—in a degree which our temperate climate and wise sanitary system has hitherto saved us from. Under these circum- stances, it is pethaps as well to know that the breeding places of the mosquito are drains, cisterns and puddles, and that the most efficacious treat- ment is petroleum, generously administered twice a month. But when the time comes for most of us to go hunting about the towns with petroleum cans in that manner it will he time to consider the Australian contention that theheart of the Empire should be removed to a more convenient spot than this island.</p>
        <p>The Warrington Health Committee might have been saved a lot of anxiety and worry attendant on selecting a site for the small- pox hospital if they had only known where to go for assistance. But they did not, and ignorance, in this case, was far from bliss- ful. The Great Sankey Parish Council, and Mr. T. Halton in particular, are the people who could have helped the Committee out of their difficulties. Bless you! they have a most profound knowledge about the sites for infectious diseases hospitals, sewerage,sanitation,and the like at Great Sankey. They have decided that the Health Committee's final choice is a had one, and having come to the conclusion that it behoved them to set the com- mittee right and enlighten them in several needed particula-s they met in solemn conclave on Mon- day evening. Isolation, subsoil, surface-water,and sewage were glibly talked about by these learned individuals. Mr. Halton, their chairman and prime mover in this question, soon convinced his hearers that he knew "a site o' things " about sites. He had no objection to the hospital as a small-pox hospital, but the site !—he was really distressed about the committee's selection for a site—so unsuitable' one of the worst !places to be found, etc. The Local Government Board must be asked for a public inquiry ere the thing was irretrievable, and they are to be asked. The Parish Council swallowed Mr. Halton's intensely learned—but not very well informed—dissertation on sites and this site in particular with a gr. nip. They are very blind to their own interests at Great Sankey, for they overlook the fact that it is in- cumbent upon them to make their own provision for sill-pox patients unless someone offers to do it for them, as the Warrington Corporation has already done. However, they will go their way,we suppose, and get the snubbing :rom the Local Government Board which they appear to be asking for.</p>
        <p>Lord Roberts bas again silenced his critics by the rapidity of his march, the brilliancy of his strategy, and the completeness of his preparations. It was to have the whole vast organization of army-supply in, full working order that made Lard</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-05-18_WidnesExaminer_art0053" n="art0053">
        <p>ItatE EXA_ Eli ,</p>
        <p>MAY 18Th 1900.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-05-18_WidnesExaminer_art0054" n="art0054">
        <head>EXAMINER, FRIDAY, MAY 18, 1900.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, FRIDAY, MAY 18, 1900.</p>
        <p>Roberts remain inactive so long at Bloemfontein, and even submit to predatory raids by adventur- ous Boer Commandos. Arm-chair critics on this side the water grumbled unceasingly at the "waste of time "but they are remarkably silent now that the delay has been so amply justified. The Com- mander-in-Chief knows the game of war thoroughly and he allows no ulterior considerations to interfere with his method of playing that game. What could have been finer tha a the way he has this week broken up the Boer re- sistance, and demonstrated the enenay's wonderful mobility in what the Mayor terms" the right direction" The advance to Pretoria has begun and we do not expect to see it stop more than tempor- arily until the goal is reacned. One of the belli- gerent States is almost wars now,and though there may be harder fighting it is improbable that much time will elapse before we annex the second one also.</p>
        <p>Her Majesty's Birthday 1900 will surely prove one of the most memorable days of a memorable year. The National Badge, for the sake of the great cause which it champions, namely, that of our dead and disabled soldiers, is seizing on the public imagination. The committee of the great National Bazaar, which is to be opened at Ken- sington on May 24th, have issued half a million badges,to be worn in honour of our beloved Queen. The sale of these unique badges is estimated to swell the fund by about £lO,OOO. The badge costs 6d.—postage id, and apart from its value as an emblem of patriotism is a neatly designed souvenir of the occasion. Thousands of people who have not had opportunities of practically helping the fund can now do so by purchasing a badge. Unity is strength, and we hope that all our readers will help to make this deserving undertaking a huge success. Orders (large or small) should be sent at once to the War Employment Bureau, 171, Queen Victoria Street, E.C., and will be executed in strict rotation. Any lady or gentleman willing to assist this worthy cause in disposing of a few of the badges would oblige the Hon. Secretary by communicating with him at once.</p>
        <p>general state of employment during April has re- mained good in most of the importaw the tendency is reported to have been 6ligLtly downward. We can only trust that we have not yet seen the turn of the tide ot general prosperity, although past experience causes us to look more closely at the proverbial " straw " when general election rumours are afloat.</p>
        <p>Plymouth Town Council have taken a step for- ward in deciding to add measles and whooping cough to the list of diseases of which notice must be given to the sanitary officers, and to make con- sumption notifiable at the discretion of the medical practitioners. In regard to every ailment of an in- fectious character it is, of cause, highly desirable that precautions should be taken to prevent uis- semination, and if the effect of notification is to lessen the number of outbreaks, other authorities may learn a lesson.</p>
        <p>THE MISSING GOAL-KICKERS. We have at length succeeded in tracing the goals scored against Altrincham. Houghton scored six and Farmer two It thee are added to the interest- ing review we published last week it will be seen that Houghton and Farmer should be credited with two more goals each.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-05-18_WidnesExaminer_art0055" n="art0055">
        <head>MATTERS OF MOMENT.</head>
        <p>MATTERS OF MOMENT.</p>
        <p>A Provincial Letter.</p>
        <p>Automobilists of the thousand miles trial returned to their destination, the Crystal Palace, early in the week, most of them thoroughly tired. For the pur- pose of letting the public know how the machines have passed through the ordeal they have since been on view at the Palace. Perhaps it goes without saying that they have given every satisfaction to the drivers, and it is equally a matter of course that there should have been a few breakdowns, a few punctures easily remedied,but It must be admitted to be an important point in their favour that they have given excellent demonstration of their staying powers. Their organ, "rhe Autocar," is a little con- cerned about an impression which it fears has got abroad that the automobile pastime is not a cleanly one. But after the explanations given the impression is not likely to remain with readers of that particular paper, and though covered With dust, the cats did not fail to go, being heard in the towns through which they passed with their hc3ters in full blast, on their way back as cheery as when they went. Yet some of the drivers were ready to admit teat automobiling in four figures is not exactly a joke. Whether as the result of the thousand miles treat, or otherwise, every observant provincial must be ready to admit that within the past few days there have been an ever increasing number of autocars on the highways and in the main thoroughfares. What the humble pedestrian will. have to do if they continue to multiply as do the " bikes " and " trikes," he has yet to consider. Even the tricycle now appears to be as much in the way of the bicycle as is :the pedestrian himself. As on the Continent, we shall soon begin to hear something about epecial tracks for cyclists and " wheeler" generally. There is still a good deal of doubt in people's minds as to the possibility of curing consumption when it has once asserted its hold upon the human system; but if there is any denbt as to the eMc icy of prevention it is clearly not share I in by the executive of the National Aesoeiation for the Pre- vention of Consumption, which has gone to the ex- pense of cuing notices to be posted in beolcing- offiees and other public places requesting the public not to spit upon the floor. Irrespective of its being a disgusting and uncleanly habit, it has been shown to conduce largely to the spread of consumption. A mem.nt's thought will suffice to show that this would naturally be the effect of spitting on the 11 or by patients suffering from a disease such as this and it is sincar,ily trusted that the notice will have the desired effect.</p>
        <p>It has been stated at Boerds:of Guardians meet- ings in more than one Union, that there is every probability of a great increase of the number of children coming under their care, as a result or the war, and it has occurred to other persons that the responsibility in this matter should be of a national and not of a local character. Of course, we are all proud of the fact that every soldier is such of his free will, and he is therefore entitled as a matter of common justice to such compensation for himself and family, as has been indicated in the House of Commons. When this has been accorded, there will still be no lack of scope for showing local gratitude by contributing freely to funds for the benefit of those whom the stress of battle and the rigours of climate have played havoc upon. Lord Selisbury'2 advice not to waste time in writ- ing midnight letters to the pspere, or in criticising the War Office, is not universally accepted, and his urging in the place of these things that every citizen should forthwith learn the use of the .ifle lute led to various pertinent inquiries. The War Office is asked to say what steps it is prepared te• take in order to place the means of learning the use of the rifle within reach of the cottage of every man, and particularly what assistance can be counted upon by rifle clubs in the matter of ranges, rifles, and ammunition. It is felt that, if there has been any Spathy,it has been on the part of the authorities and of recent Governments, and that the serious advice which the Premier gave should be folle wed by seri- ous and energetic action at the War Offige. It appears to be generally satisfactory that Lord. Salisbery should continue to discountenance con- scription, in any guise, and to he considered that although gun club 4 are good as far as they ge,Volun- teer Corp* are better. Certainly the latter not only teach members how to use the rifle, but also mili- tary drill and discipline, which would render them all the more effilient as defenders of their country. If gun clubs are intended to be supplementary to Volunteer Corps, or to be formed in rural districts where there are at present no facilities for rifle practice, they may answer some neef al purpose ; 'lent the object which it is most desirable to attain will he best achieved by the Government providing the 'Volunteers with proper rifle ranges, when they would be able to practice with the regularity that would make them good marksmen. It is generally felt that Governments, of both parties in the state, have played with this subject too long, and that the delay has increased the difficulty of obtaining suit- able rifle ranges and will become greater rather than less as time goes on.</p>
        <p>It is thirty-three years since any event to com- pare with the introduction on Monday and the first reading of the Australian Commonwealth Bill, to* place in the British Etotp,e of Commons and that, was when. in 1867. representatives of the United Kingdom gave their tasent for the establishment of the Dominion of Canada. In some respects the foundation of the Cammonwealth of Australia differs from that of the Dominion of Canada; but the result is the same, and it cm be truly said that nothing appeals more directly to the sentiment and the imagination of the average Briton than the growth of a nation, and the dawn of independence and unity. Freedom and the right of a pet:l74e to work out their own salvation have been distinguishing characteristics of the British for centurieF, and all that. checks such tendency is regarded as abhorrent. Since everybody, especially in manufacturing districts, is anxious to know when the war is likely to he over, bond Roberts's formal entry into the occupation of Kroonstad is regarded as a matter of greet and even personal satisfaction It is rightly looked linen as marking an important stage in the progress of the campaign, and has prodaced an appreciable moral effect in this country. as well as in the Free States and the Transvaal. It is fealised that Lord 11,oberts has driven a wedge completely through the Free States, and that shortly we may expect to have the proclamation announcing the annexation of the Republic. Our chief concern for the moment is once more centred upon the brave and long-suffering garrison at Mafeking. The monthly statement by the Labour Depart- ment. of the Board bf Trade, just, issued, is not quite so epcouraging as these statements have usually been for several years past. While the</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-05-18_WidnesExaminer_art0056" n="art0056">
        <head>RUNCORN AND WIDNES.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN AND WIDNES.</p>
        <p>A WIDNES CYCLIST WINS.</p>
        <p>At the Poolstock Cycle Club ports held at Wigan on Saturday, E. Cook, of Widnes, won the mile race with lOU yards start. RUNCORN FOOTBALL CLUB ANNUAL MEETING.</p>
        <p>The above meeting will take place on Tuesday, May 29th, at the Bethesda schoolroom. We call particular attention to this fact as the membership ot the Club has increased so greatly, during the last year, that the usual place of meeting at Mr. Riley's has to be changed for a larger building. See advt. BRUNNER, bIOND, AND COMPANY.—ANOTHER</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-05-18_WidnesExaminer_art0057" n="art0057">
        <head>HUGE DIVIDEND.</head>
        <p>HUGE DIVIDEND.</p>
        <p>On Wednesday, at Winnington flall, Northwich, Sir John Brunner presided over a meeting of direc- tors of Brunner, Mond, and Company, i,imited. It was resolved to declare a dividend of 40 per cent., making with the interim dividend 35 per cent for the year, which is five per cent above the previous dividend. £llO,OOO-are carried forward. ALBERT-ROAD FREE CHURCH, WIDNES. Special flower services were held in the above place of worship on Sunday. The pulpit was occupied by Mr J. Thompson, of Liverpool, who preached to large congregations. A service of sone, entitled "Budding snowdrops," was given in the afternoon by the choir of the Gospel Union Mission. Mr T. Locke presided. The story was read by Mr J. Betnay, weilst Mr Marshall conducted. The col- lections were devoted to the trust fund. RUNCORN SONS OF TEMPERANCE. The usual fortnightly meeting of teis society was 'teld en Saturday evening. The Cnairman (Mr. Thomas R. Wnyte), gave a short but interesting address, in which he pointed out the many advan- tages of total abstinence, From a pecuniary point of view it was better for a man to give up drink. I Instances weie given where, by teetotalism and , thrift, men had been raised in the social scale. A large proporti.en of the members present were the owne.s of ttalAiouses in which they lived, and they I had been able to provide better furniture and many comforts. An interesting conversation followed, in which Messrs Thomas J. Holt, Titoratte Cooke, and D. Bisbrown took pare. PLEASANT SUNDAY AFTERNOON AT WIDNES. Lest Sunday's meeting of the Milton P.S.A. was presided over by Mr. It. Webster, who delivered an address on "Lost," which was much appreciated. Mr. Abbott sang very effectively" Our Lord and King" and "A word unwisely spoken." The Rev. W. 11.Jeffries,of St. Helens, gave a forcible address on" An object in life." and spoke of the class of men with only one idea in life, who made every- thing subservient to their fancy. Yet thoei men were better than those whose aim was simply to get through life with the least possible eff.irt. The object determined the value in life, and a definite object would assist, enc-eirage, and stimulate the man,who would he greatly improved as the result of pessessin:: such an idea.</p>
        <p>PRIMITIVE METHODIST SABBATH SCHOOL, ItuNcoßN.</p>
        <p>Tao anniversary of the above was observed on Sunday. l'he pulpit 'morning and evening was occupied by the .Rev J T Horne, of Crewe, who de- livered thoughtful and encouraging sermons on the subject of Sunday school work to lArge cngrega- tions. The scholars had benefited from a careful and painstaking training at the hands of Mr. nittens, the organist, and their spirited rendering of specially selected hymns added much te the tightness of the services. The choir also sang the anthems" Earth and Reaven " (Nichol) and " Bless the Lord, omy soul " (McKenz'e) The solo parts 7'ere well rendered by Miss Davies, Miss Dora Stubbs, and Mr T Williams. The anniversary proved most successful, and the collections, which were in advance of last year, totalled up to £33. The money is to aid the cchool funds. FIRE IN ELLESMERE-STREET. RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>About ten o'clock on Wednesday morning a small tire was discovered to have broken out in Ellesmere-street. Runcorn. The wooden gable end of the house occupied by Mr. Peter Gregory, had in some mysterious manner became ignited. Several police offirters were first on the scene, and as the inmates were preparing to " fiit " the good natured officrs assisted them in get,..,ing out their household goods and furniture. This had barely been accomplished when the Runcorn Fire Brigade, under Superintendent R Wright, appeared, and put out the fire in record time. The damage was very insignificant—The blowing of the fire alarm was at first takel as an intimation of the relief of Mafeking, and when it was found to be otherwise, keen disappointment was felt.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-05-18_WidnesExaminer_art0058" n="art0058">
        <head>ALARMING FIRE AT RUNCORN.</head>
        <p>ALARMING FIRE AT RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>About 9.30 p.m. on Thursday last, an alarming fire was discovered to have broken out at Bottom Lacks. Investigation showed that a large pile of old sleepers and waste timber, which was packed behind the large wooden warehouses in the vicinity of the Manchester Ship Canal General Offices, was all ablaze. Dock-constable R. Moores was soon on the Beene, and summoning the aid of about eight or nine of his fallow constables, spades and buckets were requi,Aitioned and the work of subjugating the confligration taken in hand. It was not until nearly midnight, however, that the task was yetis- f ictprily accomplished. The pr )mpt action alone saved the warehouses, and the Dock Police deserve every credit for the smart way in which they set about a very difficult task.</p>
        <p>PRINCES-STREET_ PRIMITIVE METHODIST SUNDLY</p>
        <p>F.CHOOL WIDNES'</p>
        <p>The anniversary of the above schoal was celebra- ted on Sunday. Sermons wer4 Preached miming and evening by the Rev J Anglis, of Wigan Csntral Hall. The scholars and choir rondered the anthems "Lord God the Holy Ghost," "Alleluia. King most mighty," "Then, 0 " Lead me to Jesus." 'the Better Land," and " Sweet Evening flour." There were good congregations. In the afternoon, a children's vrviee was held, when Mr W J Stainer occupied the chair. Mr Swale gave an address. Solos, &amp;c., were rendere I by the following :—L Wharton, MiS9RS E Smith, Wilson, and F Sage. A musical service entitlsd "The River Singers," wan Riven on Monday evening by the choir, assisted by friends Th rthildren hal been very efficiently trained by Mr G Owens, who also conducted. Mr A E Calvert presided at the organ, The collections were a considerable advance on last year.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-05-18_WidnesExaminer_art0059" n="art0059">
        <head>AN ANTAncTic EXPRDITION.</head>
        <p>AN ANTAncTic EXPRDITION.</p>
        <p>At the kind invitation of the Rev. T. S. Lea,vicar of St. Ambrose Church, Widnes. about 10 teachers from Widnes pill a visit to Liverpool on Friday evening to hear a lecture on "Why we wish to ex- plore the Antarctic, with special reference to the. Projected. expedition," by Professor W. A. Hardman D.Sc., F.R.S. (member of the Nqtional Anta'W.,ic 14'4- pedition Committee). The lecturer described the number and scope of previous Antarctic expeditions He then referred at length to the Englist qpedlti-In which was at present being equipped at a cost of £lOO,OOO Towards this £45,000 had been collected in subscriptions, and the Government had given an equal amnunt,bringing the funds at present in hand up to £90,000 or a little over. The steamer which was at, Present in course of construction, would cost £85,000. The Admiralty was helping the :Aeheme very considerably. It had promised' to Provide two naval officer. One would be the cap- tain of the ship, and the other would be the chief officer. The Admiralty was alzo giving all the scientific instruments,and a considerable amount of stores. They had also designed the ship. which would he of wood and possess engines of 450-horse power. Professor Gregory.who had worked at their own Liverpool Marine Biological Station in its earliest stages on Puffin Island,would be the leader of the scientific bpd v. Including two doctors,there would he ex scisntific men in all.—The party were entertained to tea, and a naost enjoyable and profit- able outing was spent.—'4r. J. stainer very ably carried out the travelling arrangements.</p>
        <p>A CHANGE FOR THE BETTER Is the change yon want when the bloo4 get.. nut of condition, and Dr. JACOB TOWNEIE4D'S SRS&amp;p&amp;BrLL&amp; pirforms thi needful with splendidly nlaasing eflicloy and comfort. TdY Fr. Volumes conldn't say m ze.--Advt. UNDER THE ()LOOK FOR GOOD BOOTS.—Advt</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-05-18_WidnesExaminer_art0060" n="art0060">
        <head>MILL BROW SUNDAY SCEIOOLS.</head>
        <p>MILL BROW SUNDAY SCEIOOLS.</p>
        <p>Oa Sunday next, May 20h, tna Mill Brow Sun- day School Anniversary will be celebrated at Halton Road Wesleyan Chapel, Runcorn. Services will be held throughout the day and sermons preached morning and evening by the Rev. J. Hanby, of Bradford. As Mr. Hanby was latterly c mnelted with the Runcorn Circuit many of his old friends will flock to bear him. Special hymns and anthems will be sung by the children, assisted by the Halton Road Prizl Choir. CAlections will be made at each service in aid of the School Funds. THE RELIEF OF NI &amp;FEKING</p>
        <p>Wednesday last at early morning several of Run- corn's prominent residents and tradespeople might have been busily seen running up flags and making big preparations for decoration and jubilee, being under the impression that Alafeking had been re- lieved. Liter news, however, proved that once again the jubilation was premature, and in a very snort time the whole had been withdrawn. THEFT OF PROPERTY AT RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>Before Mr. W. Handley, at the Runcorn Police Court on Wednesday morning, Sarah Helsby, Cross- street,pleaded guilty to stealing one dolly ant two saucepans valued at 53 61, the property of Emily Burro vvs, Egerton-street. —iuperintendent D. Pear- son asked for a remand to toe following day.— Acting-Sergeant Gregory gave evidence of the prisoner's arrest. She admitted the offenc3 to him when charged.—The remand was granted.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-05-18_WidnesExaminer_art0061" n="art0061">
        <head>S/R JOIIN BRUNNER AND THE TEACHERS.</head>
        <p>S/R JOIIN BRUNNER AND THE TEACHERS.</p>
        <p>On Wednes6ay afternoon Sir John Brunner,lll.P., visited Runcorn, and entertained to tea at Lea's Temperance Hotel a number of the members of the National Union of Teachers. There were representa- tives present from the Cheshire District Union, and Chester, Northwich, Runcorn, and Widnes Associa tions,and these discussed with Sir John his attitude upon the question of corporal punishment in schools. It was pointed out that the whole National Union wits strongly agginst the punishment book, and many reasons were advanced. The teachers also brought before Sir John Brunner's notice the new code, and commented upon the inequality and gross injustice of making the e,fficient schools nay for the lesA efficient schools. as brought about by the new rule as to grants. Sir John said in the main he was in agreement with the teachers.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-05-18_WidnesExaminer_art0062" n="art0062">
        <head>MERSEY MISSION SALE OF WORK.</head>
        <p>MERSEY MISSION SALE OF WORK.</p>
        <p>The workers in connection with the recent Sale of Work held at the Nlersey Mission the other day, met ou Wednesday night to draw up the balance sheet,and also for the purpose of spending a social evening.—Mr. Digges (the secretary),submitted the balance sheet from whiel it was seen that the total receipts reached £lOO 14s 10J, and the expense but £lB lOs 61. They had therefore a balance of £B2 4s 8d to devote to the cleaning and renovating ot the mission. Those figures did not include the OES promised by Miss Naylor, of Warrington, which had not yet come to hand. lie felt he must heartily congratulate the stall holders at the result. Where the money had come from, he did not know, but he did know they had no one outside the mission to thank for it. (Applause). Mr W :Shaw (missioner), pro- posed that a vote of thanks be given to Mr Digges, for his services as hoeorary secretary,to Mr Rhoda, the chairman of committee; Mr. Crosby and Mr. Priestly, Miss Boywer, Mrs Callaghan and Miss Bate the treasurer of the ladies' sewing meeting de- served special mention :—The vote of thanks was seconded by Mr T Wakefield and carried unanioa- ously.—Dancire2 was indulged in, and the company 'broke up about 12 o'clock.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-05-18_WidnesExaminer_art0063" n="art0063">
        <head>AN EXHIBITION OF BOLTON FIGHTING.</head>
        <p>AN EXHIBITION OF BOLTON FIGHTING.</p>
        <p>At the Widnes Police Court, on Friday, before Alderman H S Timmis (in the chair), and Alderman J W Wareing, Alfred StockforLh, 9, Violet-street, was summoned for assaulting Wirliam goss.—Com- plainant, it appeared, was walking by Vicker's Works when the defendant accosted him and asked For a flower. He gave him a dandelion. Defendant then struck witness under the ear with his fist, and felled him t 3 the ground. Witness asked him what that was for, whereupon defendant gave him several kicks on the loWer portions of his body. Not content with that he pulled off his cear. saying he would show him "The style of Bolton." Witness did not know the man, and did not give him any provocation, Defendant was not sober. Witnes, had been receiving mAical attention from Dr Hutehins;on since,and had not been able to attend to his work.llis week's was amounted to 24s.fie had had to pay for a warrant as the deleadant was going to leave the town, and there were the doctor's fees. —Defendant, who said he did not knew sy:lat he was doing, was ordered to pay £1 and the costs (14s), or go to gaol for 14 days. -</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-05-18_WidnesExaminer_art0064" n="art0064">
        <head>VOLUNTEERS AT ST. PAUL'S RUNCORN</head>
        <p>VOLUNTEERS AT ST. PAUL'S RUNCORN</p>
        <p>On Sunday morning the Members of the Runcorn Volunteer Detachment attended divine service at St. Paul's Wesleyan Church, accompanied by the Camden, Brunswick, and nalton Companies of the Boys' Brigade. Tee volunteers and brigade boys met at, the Drill Hall and marched to the church, beaded by the Volunteer Band; The Volunteers on parade included :—Three offieers viz., ' Captain George F. Ashton (in command), Major Harrifson, Lieutenant Lineker ; nine sergeants, 30 band and buglers, and 118 rank and file. The service was cen'ucted by the Rev, J. A. B. Alalvern,and a num- ber of anpropriate hymns were sung, several of them being accompanied by the band. The rev. gAntlemart preached a patriotic sermon from Timothy ii. 3. 4 —"Thou, therefore, endure hardness as a good soldier of Jesus Christ. No man that *warreth eetangleth himself with the affairs of this life ; that he may please Rim who hath chosen him to be a +; oldier." During the coarse of his address he said that the pivot of the text was hardship. This, in fact, was the pivot of all cam- Paiene. Whatoccupied the minds of experienced soldiers were hardships and:the preparation aea'n hardships. One of the most disappointing contrasts he knew was the outward difference between a soldier 03 parade in full regimentals and the same seldier in prison. Instead cf that superb headgear magnificent coo', and tartan stripes a prisoner was dressed in bleck, loose-fitting garmente. Here Paul, the Christian soldier, was in prison, and in writing to Timothy. afraid lest the contrast might shock and scare him, tells him not to be ashamed but rather to endure hardship. because the Christian campaign did not mean full regimentals always,nor a mere parade. The general and the cowman soldier were always comradea in hardship. One of the plea- sant features of the present war was the comrade- ship displayed by New Zealand, Australia and Canada. with the mother country. The rev, gentle- man dealt with many phases at the present war. nßri narrated many incidents which had come under his observation. At the conclusion of the service the Volunteers and brigade companies returned to the Drill Hall, where they were dismissed. The nffi eers in charge of the Boys' Brigade were—Cam- den, Captain Woodland ; Brunswick, Captain Leach; Halton, Captain Smith.</p>
        <p>RUNCORN FOOTBALL CLUB.</p>
        <p>Nominations for Next Year's Offices</p>
        <p>At a largely attended meeting of the Runcorn Foot- Club Intinbers,held at Mr. Riley's cace Bridgc-atreet, the foll ming nominations for the respective offices of the Club and flr members of the C )mmittee were re. orivei :—Prcsident, Mr. Samuel Taylor J.P., Rydal Mount ; treasurer, Mr C Powcall ; secretary. Messrs. J Ellison ; Assistant Secretary, Mr. A Abel, and W B Brimelow. Auditors; Messrs. T C Cooper and P Lunt. Captain of firet team : Mears. 8 Houghton, and J Butterworth ; Deputy Capin: Mnssrs. S Ilouthton, J Butterworth, J Faulkner, T Warder, and W Liehtfont. Captain of second team : Messrs. Inshardson, and J Nick-on. Deputy captain : Messrs J N.cksmi. and J Richardson.—'3ommittee : Messrs W Jones, W Carroll, A Liehtburn, P Deakin, El Cftwlev , Mackie, S Atrams, T Robinson, G Robinson. A Taylor, W Faulkner, A Abel. H Wright', D Liehtfont. R Garnett. G M-orea, W B Brimelow, F Hancock, J F Mouiton,T Moore., H Grounds. and W Shaw. jun —The following are the vice-presid-oit; of the Club : His Gr,ren the Duke of We itmhyter. Sir Richard Brooke. flirt., Sr John Brunner, Hart. M.P., Maio Harrisson, Captain G F Aahton, Dr F McDougall, Dr M McKenzie, Dr McLennan, Dr Robinson. Messrs E Imis 0.0., F Norman, J.P., A R Norman, J P., R Able, W Cotlir, J K Diggea. C Dutton. C r, Evan , T C Haddock, W Han k,nmen, A Florigh, J J Hewitt,. T Jones, A Jordan, T Kirkham, J W Lighthurn, J Meadowcroft, W T C Price, C ASpealtman, E Speakman, J.P., Eaton Speak- man, J Steele.,G Summerlield, A Timmins, W R Tort, J Urisworth, J'Wilson, F Wiswall, and J H Weston.</p>
        <p>'PIMA° EMNEFIT UNIDHR TEtF. CLOCK FOR BEST OOTS IN LANCASHIRE.—Advt. LINUM CATFIARTICUM PILLS. digestive, correc- tive. and agreeably aperient, Sid. 130. Of ail Ch,msts. Advt. PREMIER CYCLES price £9 175 63.—J. S. GARLAND, Cycle Factor, Bridge-street. Warrington. NEW RAPID CYCLES with Bin cranks and free wheels, J. 84GADLAND, Cycle Factors, Bridge-at., Warrington,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-05-18_WidnesExaminer_art0065" n="art0065">
        <p>RUNCORN NOTES. {BY A CORRESPONDENT].</p>
        <p>It is very pleasant to see that the President (Mr. S Taylor) ; the treasurer (Mr. C Pownall) ; and the secretary (Mr. J Ellison) ; of the Runcorn Football Club are re-slacted without opposition for the en- suing year. The annual meeting is fixed for Tues- day May 29th, at Bethesda scho3lroom., and at that meeting the following new rule will be put forward for consideration "That the offices of treasurer, secretary, assistant secretary, shall not come up for annual election, but that these officials shall hold office in the same way as paid officials of public bodies."</p>
        <p>As far as my memory carries me, I believe thi4 rule to be the outcome of the remarks of Nfr J F Nioultoo, at the last annual meeting, when he very ably prepared Coe minds of the members for" what was coming." On that occasion he asked that all would think it over. Of course, there are not many who have given the subject a further thought, but that does not detract from the advantages ta be gained by the new rule,which aims at providing the officials with a reasonable security of tenure of office. It is to be hoped that the new rule will be adopted.</p>
        <p>The game ot cricket in R.ancorn is at a low ebb ! This year the premier team is a very "premier one" indeed still we must not despair of it. They title themselves "Runcorn Parish" and have secured a ground on Halton-road, and I wish them a very successful season. It was a beginning of this kind that gave birth to the famous Runcorn Rugby team; and who knows but that Runcorn may not figure respectably in the National sport of cricket.</p>
        <p>Sir John Brunner, Bart. I.P. was on Wednesday night at Runcorn, awl had an interview with the local members el the National Union of teachers re- specting several matters embraced in the New Code. The result of the deliberations I am informed is not to.be made public. Some of the subjects under discu,sion have leaked out,but at the request of the Union I refrain from adding anything further.</p>
        <p>The Mersey Mission to seamen has jut accomp- lished a creditable feat in raising sufficient money to put the place in thorough ship-shape order. I call particular attention to this fact, because the mission has done this without any extraneous as- sistance. There are two churches.—The Parish Church and Trinity—in Runcorn, that one would look to, to take the missioa under its wing as it were. Being of the same creed it is but a natural exuectation. The unvarnished truth, however, is that neither puts itself oat of the way to assist the mission, though it is a well-known fact that the mission aids one to the tune of about .E• 25 per annum. This is a case of the weak assisting the strong, and the mission can congratulate itself upon it.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-05-18_WidnesExaminer_art0066" n="art0066">
        <head>FRODSHAM.</head>
        <p>FRODSHAM.</p>
        <p>THE LATE MRS. RINGROSE.—List Sunday evenine, at the Trinity Chapel, Frodsharn, Mr. E. J. Guest, 8.A., after the usual service made t9uching reference to the death of Mrs. W. D. Ringrcae, The interest she had con- tinually manifested in the work of the church and the assistanc she had rendered to hr husband in the very valuable work he did on behalt of the church had always been a charac':eristic of hers.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-05-18_WidnesExaminer_art0067" n="art0067">
        <head>EARLESTOWN &amp; NEWTON.</head>
        <p>EARLESTOWN &amp; NEWTON.</p>
        <p>POLICE CASES.</p>
        <p>At the Earleitown Police 6tation. on Monday morniug before Mr J rAr Niminett, James Wynde Col- lier, of Bt. Helens, and Elizabeth Allinson, a tramp, were both charged with being drunk in .filarlestown, on Saturday night. They were discharged with a caution.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-05-18_WidnesExaminer_art0068" n="art0068">
        <head>JUVENILE TEMPLA.RY.</head>
        <p>JUVENILE TEMPLA.RY.</p>
        <p>The Pride of Earlestown Temple No. 117 S meet- ing was held in the Co-operative Hall, Wargrave- road, on Monday evening: Sister E Jones C.T., presiding. Two Howls vvtqe admitted to memb,ir- ship. Bro. Dring deliverad an addresa which Wee muert enjoyed ate" A pleasant temple." A vote o: 11.:-; to 3 • Dt44-0. NARROW FSCAPR FROM FIRE.</p>
        <p>On Wednesday night a house in Houghton-street. Earlestown, occupied by Mr. A. Grundy and his family narrowly escaped destruction by fire. Mr. ttnl Mrs Grundy had gone out, leaving a couple of young children in the house. Some time aUerwards one of the youngsters ran outside and told some men that the house was on fire. The men rushed in and found thnt some rubbish under the stairs was on fire, the children having been playing with matches. The men speedily extinguished the fire, but had it not been for their promptitude, it is lik,4y serious damage°woulel have been done. SUCCESS OF AN EARLESTOWN BAND,</p>
        <p>The annual brass band contest promatod by Baal- ber Bridge Subscription Prize Band, took place on Saturday. and was well attended. Ten bands com- peted, eight less than last year. The t,bst-piece was, "Beautiful Switzerland." set of waltzes, W Round, and the result was : —l, Coppnll Subscription; 2, Pickup Bank •, 3, Earlestown ; 4. Walsdea Temper- ance; and 5, Longridge St. Lawrence. In the quick-step Earlestown came first with "Sans of Vic- tory." and Pickup Bank second with "Avondale." Hr. George Wadsworth, of Holaifirtb, judged. GOOD TEMPLARY.</p>
        <p>The Hope of atrleatown Lodge meeting was held in the Co-operative Hall Wargrave-road on Monday. Bro G Clarke E T opened the meeting the Chief Templar being detainee. Two friends were admitted to membership. Bro Woods. L D read the District Lodge Digest and G C TemDlara circular. Bro New- man brought geeetings from Wesley Hall Lodge No 312 and reported that Bro Lee. D C T was improv- ing but was suffering from sleeping in a room with an escape of gas and not with rheumatic fever as at first supposed. Bro Davies, Pioneer Ladze, St. Helens, read a letter from a member and volunteer at the front. Bro Clark also read one from a friend at Maritzburg, which prove! both humorous and interesting. the writer giving a graphic description of li re and scenes in South Africa Lodge closed by Bro Cutler, C.T.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-05-18_WidnesExaminer_art0069" n="art0069">
        <head>HUSSAR DINNER AT NEWTON.</head>
        <p>HUSSAR DINNER AT NEWTON.</p>
        <p>At the Pied Bull Hotel,Newton on Saturday night this year's recruits to the " C" troop of Lancashire Yeomanry Hussars,to the number oF 46 were enter- tained to dinner. The meal was excellently served and was much enjoyed. After the cloth had been removed the proceedings were presided over by Sergeant Major Pearea, sunported by Regimental Sergeant Major Strange, S3rgeant Holgate, and others. The usual loyal and patrotic toasts were cordially honoured and the name of the Colonel of the Regiment, Lord Gerard, was received with enthusiasm. The !musical programme was a lengthy one, the rule of exacting a song from every recruit being rigidly enforced. The genial Chairman con- tributed to the enjoyment of theevening with some amusing and iriteresting anecdotes.</p>
        <p>EARLESTOWN WESLEYAN SUNDAY SCHOOL. The anniversary services in connection with the above school were held on Sunday in the Wesleyan Chapel. Though cold, the weather was fine, and there were consequently large congregations at each service, this being especially so in the evening, when many people were unable to obtain admitt- ance. Special hymns were sung by the choir, who were assisted 1:13, a specially trained choir of chil- dren, conducted by Mr. Thomas Clare. Solos were sung by Misses Hall, E ferrgher aud J Adams, 0 Clare and E Jones. The preacher at, the morning and evening services was the Rev R A Mitelseson Brown„ of Liverpool, whose discourses were thoroughly interesting and enjoyable. In the after- noon a sacred cantata entitled "The Fountain of Life" was rendered Er: the choir and children. The various solos, quartettes and choruses were well sung, and the subject was excellently treated. The soloists were Misses II: Clarke,C Berry, 0 Roberts, L and M Barr, Messrs R Robinson, F Clare. R Barr end Twemlow. Miss E L Greenall presided at the organ and Mr Clare conduetod. The collections during the day amounted to £6l 141 6d, nearly £4 less than last year</p>
        <p>Street Accident at Warrington. On Wednesday, James Radcliffe, aged seventy yars, of 31, Winwick-road, Warrington, was walk- ing along Sco`land-road, when, being rather short- sighted, he did not notice a man-hole which was being constructed in Winwick-itreet. and walked into it. Police-constables Clough and Reddington, who were in the neighbourhood, came to his assist- ance, and it was found that he was bleeding pro- fusely from the leg. The constable rendered first aid and then took him to the infirmary, and it Wftl discovered that he had burst a vein in the lower part of his leg. After being attended, he was taken home in a cab.</p>
