      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-05-18_WidnesExaminer_art0071" n="art0071">
        <head>LATEST NEWS.</head>
        <p>LATEST NEWS.</p>
        <p>YESTERDAY'S CRICKET.</p>
        <p>Close of Play Scores.</p>
        <p>LANCASHIRE V. WARWICKSHIRE :—This match commenced yesterday, on the Aigburth Ground, War- wickihire, taking first innings. They were all dismissed for 125, Mold securing 6 wickets for 58 runs. The score at close of play was :—Loncashire, 199 for 6.</p>
        <p>SUSSEX v WORCESTERSHIRE :—Woreestersbire all out, 238 ; Sussex, 86 for none. DERBYSHIRE v SURREY :—Derbvebire, 320 for 8. ESSEX v GLOUCESTERSHIRE :—Eisex, all out, 264 ; GlGucostn, 80 for 3. CAMBRIDGE UNIVERSITY v YORKSHIRE :—Cam - brilge, 268 ; Yorkshire, 20 for 1.: OXFORD UNIVERSITY v. Ma. A. J. WEBBE'S XI. —Webbe's XI, 230; Oaf 4rd, 33 f.):. one.</p>
        <p>MANCHESTER v. WAHRINGTON.—Manches:er, 230 Warrington, 49 all out.</p>
        <p>Whole Ship's Crew Found Murderde,</p>
        <p>One Man Shoots 12.</p>
        <p>COPENHAGEN, THURSDAY.</p>
        <p>The following telegram has been received here from living (Sweden). As the steamer oping passed the steamer Prins Carl, last night, at cleven o'clock a man sprang up on the deck of the latter, and shouted "If anyone comes near I will shot !" At the same moment it was seen tint a woman was shrieking for help and hanging over the ship's side. The man escaped in a %mt. The Prins Carl was then boarded, and it was found that twelve men on board of her had been shot down. Six, including the captain, were already dead. The remainder of the people on bard were kicked ia. One of the wounded persons died to-day. The murderer is beim!, putku-d.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON. Local _Government Orders.</p>
        <p>In the House of Commons, on Tuesday, leave was given to bring in Bills to confirm local government provisional orders relating to Warrington,Leigh and other towns.</p>
        <p>Music in the Park.</p>
        <p>The bandstand in the Park to-morrow (Sunday) afternoon will be occupied by the Buckley Street, P.S.A. Band. The following will be the programme: Larch "Invader," Southwark; overture, "Crown of Victory," Ripley ; selection "Oberon," Veber; selection. "Bogey," round ; fantasia, "Dawn of Redemption" Gray ; "Gad save the Queen." Mr. J. T. Richardson will be the cenduct.or.</p>
        <p>_ The Lancashire Volunteer Brigade, Definite orders for the encampment of the Lan- cashire Volunteer Imfantry Brigade, whi3h includes the battalions at Bury, Rochdale, Salford, Warring- ton, and St. Helens, from June 2 to 16, were issued oa Saturday by Brigadier-Colonel Leith Ross. Inquirie4 at Bury Barracks show that there is a steady flow of applicants for admission to the Royal Reserves. Special inducements are being offered to saddlers, wheelwrights, collar-makers, and chain- makers to enlist for twelve months in the Royal Artillery.</p>
        <p>- Volunteer Encampments. The Dukelof Lancaster's Own Ye)maury and the Lancsshire Ilu,sars have arranged logo into camp at Altcar about the middle of June. The site selected is on part of Squire Blundell's estate near the station, wnich could not be more favourably situated DTI account of its clese proximity to the rifle range. The Warrington Volunturs will be at Altcar frern the 2nd to 16,,;h of June, on the site occupied last year by the Militia, to the north of the ranze. The LyTT,na Volunteers go with the Cheshire Brigade in June to Salisbury Plain for the manceuvres. ,</p>
        <p>Ohmy's Circus. An exceptionally interesting programme has been placed before the public at the Queen-street Circus this week. The great attractions are the who, es trick cyclists, are unsurpassed. Some of their feats almost take one's breath away and their performance throughoutemerits the approval of the audience. The seven Savones in their musical en— tertainment are must successful. Their skilful handling of a variety of instruments gains for t!lera the hearry plaudits of the onlookers,and eacores are frequent. Teo Java bamboo bells are remarkably sweet-toned and selections by tr.e saxophone band are a distinct feature of the programme. Tim Misses Minnie and Lily Ohtny display ability as equestriennes and Mons. Alrantee's exhibition on the slanting wire is full of daring. Hess and Lisbon crciite uproarious laughter with their amusing skat- ing act, and Mon. Belleto goes through evolutions on the trapeze. Nero is a clever performer on the flying rings and Claud Ohmv's leaping act on horseback is rewarded with cordial approbation. The same artiste is very popular in a globe and juggling act. The clowns Cortland and Walker add humour to the entertainment by their orieinal jokes. Altogether the programme is one of the best ever presented before a Warrington auiiienee and n 1 one should mi-s the opportunity of visiting the C</p>
        <p>We have rneived from Messrs. Allan Brithers and Co.. of 19. James-itreet, Liverpool, an illustr,tod pamphlct dealing with their fleet, and, more particularly their two Now Twinslraw Steamers "Tunisian " and " Bavarian." Of thesa magnificent vessels ntimerous illustrations are given, and of the latti‘r both in " Peace and War," for she is Tr inspoit No. 16. Th 3 letterpress is pleasantly informative, the illustrations excllleilt, and thA rtisti c eet-un rerr fine.</p>
