      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-05-18_WidnesExaminer_art0074" n="art0074">
        <head>BEFORE the MAGISTRATES</head>
        <p>BEFORE the MAGISTRATES</p>
        <p>IMPUDENT ROBBERY BY A "DOMESTIC SERVANT." At the Warringtcn Police Court on Monday, Ada Sum- num, who as described as a domestic serva at was charged on a warrant with stealing three lady's gold rings, a watch and chain, and a silver desertopoon and fcrk valved in all at abont £2O, on November 29-h from the house of Arthur Percy Grange, the }lollies, Manchester-read.—Superin'endent Monaghan said that on November 28th last the prisoner obtained a situation as a domestic servant at the house of Mr Grange. The following day she mysteriously disappeared, and her mistress becoming suspieloui examined the house and found that the articles of jewellery named were missing. Information was given to the police, and inquiries were made. It was amertained that the prisoner bad pledged the jewellery at Birkenhead, Liverpool, and Bir- mingham. She was arrested by P.S. Cheeney in Not tingharn on Saturday, and admitted the offence. Two et. tire articles had not, yet been recovered and he alred for a rem‘nd until Wednesday.-- Prisoner was accordingly remanded.—She was again brought tefore the magistrates on Wedo Superintendent Menaghan again stated • Facts of the case and added that prisoner reelized£9 b 7 pledging the property. All tbe property had been r frcm inquiries the palieo bad nade,it ape- )ocl that the prispner had for two or three years been goin;:. ,ae country and obtaining cituadons and comrni:tic ? •n- -berins. She co-habited with a man. Since lea va:g War- rington, the bad committed similar offences far which she was WA nteri by the police. The man was also in custody. It seemed that the man wrote characters for the priscner who obtained situations and immediately decamped with all the prop4rty she could find. The man disposed of the property.—Prisoner was Gent to gaol for four months with hard labour. SINGULAR CASE FR(M LVALTON.—A FARM LABIUREE'S DELUSION.</p>
        <p>• On Monday at the Warriegton Occasional Court be- fore Mr. James Tinsley (in the chair) aod Mr. John Michael Fox,farm lab.ourcr,Waltcn, was charged on remand with having assaulted John Walsh,a. labourer e'iployed on the same farm,on the Bth inEt. Mr. Browne appeared ler the defendant. The complainant, an elderly man, who€e face was badly marked, stated that he and defendant woAced en Mr. Tornerss farm at Walton. They slept in the same room. On the nLzht, of May Bth he (comnlainant) was in bed when defendant came into the room Lind stood by him without speaking, afterwards leaving the room. He came back in twenty minutes and , jumped up on him (complainant) hurting him with his elbows. He (defendant) then began to punch him, hitting him in the face several times. There was a light in the ream. De- fendant did rot kick him. He did not wish te press the case.—Mr. Browne : What did he do this for P—Com- Valliant : I gave him no causa to the best of my belief. Did be not think yon had cursed him with a cross that you carry P—No.I did no such thing.—Did he not think 63 P—He may nave done.—Had he not teen unwell for several days, and did he not ask if you had the power to cure him ?—No. He could go to a doctor.—Did he not say that you had shown him the back of the cross ? He said 1 bad prayed against him,—Do not these men believe that you. have power to either bless or curse with the cross P—l do not say I have the power.—Do they believe it P—l don't know.—Do they not believe that you can cure them ?—They might believe it.—Did ho not ask ycu in the pre,ence of two other men to cute him with the cross ?—No. If he had, I should cot have done it. That is for the doctor to do.—On Thursday you said you had more power than you say now.—l be- lieve in the sign of the cross. I am not a heretic —Do you wish to press the caee P—l do not. I have had no trouble with him tiefote. We have been the best ot friends. I treated him like a man. I would not go against the b7lik I have kased—not for all that England Is worth.—l leave the matter to the Court.— Mr Browne said the defendant was slit ported by his brother and another man who lived on the premises. They implici; ly believed that the complainant bad the power to curs or tales them. and Mr. Tinsley would remember the admissions complainant mate to him on the previous Thursday. Ho certainly said then that he bad the power to bless men. Defendant had been ill, and thought the complatant could cure him. He asked him to do so and because he would riot he lost his tem- per and hit Cim. Complainant did not press the case, and he (Mr. Browce) thought jostice would be met if defendant paid the complainant £2 compensation. That wouldsatisfy complainant, and would be a considerable punishment to the defendant.—The Chairman said an assault had been proved, and defendant would be fined 10a and costs.—Defendar t paid the fine, and voluntarily gave the complainant £2 compensation.</p>
        <p>ASSAULT ON A WIDN'FS LADY CYCLIST.-A VIOLENT</p>
        <p>PRISONER</p>
        <p>At the Warrington Police Court no Tuesday befov Alderman Sutton (in the chair) and Mr. Birch%ll, Jahn Hooper was eherged with being drunk and disorderly in Green-stree-, at 4.30, in peevious after: CI and with mak- ing use of obscene languege in Irlam-str. e • t 5 o'clock. fie wits terther charged with committing wilful damage to the ue itorm of Constable Benne..t to the amount of is 6d. —A Mrs Woods who resides at Sankey Bridges, said she was near Bank Quay Bridge at 4.30 on Monday after- noon when she saw the prisoner and also a lady who was riding a tecycle. At the bottom of Bank Quay Bridge prieose.r Commi need to run alter her and when she got iuro Green street she " dropped "off the bicycle and. primer struck at her and pled the machine on the greund. The key said sue did not know the man.— Ann 137)a:damn said she was with the last witne,s and saw prisoner run alter the lady and get hold of the bicycle. The I,oly was attacked by him. Prisoner was drunk.—P.C. Smith stated that at the time in question he was on duty at 3S/freers. Cros6eld'a works and saw a crowd collected in Greer-street. He proceeded there and saw three or four men heleing the prisoner who was waeding against the va4ll. He asked them what was the matter and they told him that prisoner had knccked a lady off 1:o,r b'cyelis. He saw the lady and sbe told him that she vs.? not hurt and did not know whether fly, itachint, was damaged or not. She gave, the re.me of mire; yed, 120, Hursei,trest, Widnes. iVitnesa brsught the prisoner, who was very drunk, to the Polir ation. He ael'ed like a madman e St laud made use of very feu' laneua.ee.—P.C. Bennett said he sena on duty at the Police Station when the prisoner was brought in. He refused to be searched, and whilst they wore conducting Lim to the cells he struck at witness several times. end also tried to bite him. He broke the chain of his whistle in three places —P.C,P nnington said the prisoner acted like a mad-. man, aro !eok five officers to i et him in the cells.— Pre•onfr said be was very drunk and did net remember anything about wha". had occurred. He was very sorry.</p>
        <p>—He was committ3d to gasl for a month with hard labour.</p>
        <p>THE SERIOUS CHARGE AGAINST A WARRINGTON BOY.</p>
        <p>At the Wnrringtort Police Court on Wednesday before Mr. Birchall (in the chair), Councillor Smethurst and Mr. Geddes, Fred Lucock, a toy, was charged on re- mand with c 'using grievous bodily harm to Alfred Bockett, by striking him on the head with a stone in Bewsey-street, at 10 15 p.m. on Friday the 4th inst.— Mr. Browne appeared on behalf of the prisoner.—lt was stated that the boy Bsckett was not yet out of danger, and the prisoner wa3 remanded for another week.</p>
        <p>SA.VAGE ASSAULTS.-A RUFFIAN WELL PUNISHED.</p>
        <p>On Wednesday at the Warrington Police Court before Alderman Sutton and Mr. Hepherd, John Carroll, a farm labourer, was charged with being drunk and disorderly Arpley-road at 1.45 the previous afternoen, and with assaulting P.C. Johnson. He was further charged with assaulting John Bridge in Bridge-street at 1.35,and with assaulting George Spinks in Wilderspool-road at 1 .40. P.C.Johnson stated that at 1.30 he was on duty in Bridge- street wneu he was called to the Higher Seven Stars. On proceeding there he saw the prisoner, who was act- ing in a very disorderly manner. Ho reques- ted the man t 3 go home, and ho waiked down the street in the direction of Warrington Bridge. From information witness subsequently received he went into Wilderspool-road and apprehended the prisoner. He struck out right and left like a madman, striking witness in the ribs. With assistance he took the man to the Police Station. He was very violent all the way.—John Bridge, a groom, residing at 67, Legh- street, spoke to seeing the prisoner ejected from the public-house. He :truck witness above the templrs rendering him uncoeseious.—George Spinks, a time- keeper, who lives at 19, River-road, said that at 1.40 he was in Wilderepool-road. At the railway crossing he met the prisoner, who, without saying anything rushed at him, struck him in the chest, kicked him on the 1“e-, and gave him a blow with his fist above the eye. He bad to go to the Infirmary to have the wound above his eye attended to.—Ephraim Quarmby. "The Model.,' Friars-green, said the prisoner hit Mr. Spinks in the eye and knocked him down.—Thomas Walker, Cedar-!ane, Tnelwall, saw the prisoner strike P.C. John- son. Ho was very violent.—Superintendent Monaghan said the prisoner had been before the Court six times.— The Chairman said it was quite evident that prisoner was not fit to be at large. On the charge of being (hulk and disorderly he would be committed ta gaol for a month, for assaulting the constable he would be ilea- , prisoned for another month, fgr assaulting Bridge he would receive two months and for assaulting Spinks two montbs-3X months in all with hard labour.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-05-18_WidnesExaminer_art0075" n="art0075">
