      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-05-18_WidnesExaminer_art0076" n="art0076">
        <head>MINOR CASES.</head>
        <p>MINOR CASES.</p>
        <p>Warrington Borough Court. FRlDAY.—Before the Mayor (ehairmin), Councillor Smethurst and Mr. Mermaid.</p>
        <p>A Pat NICER WOMAN.—lettbella Chappel. of 9 Morris-court for being drunk and disorderly at nine o'clock on Thursday morning was committed to prison for 14 days' bard labour. A WIFE ASSAULTS HER Hussarrn.—Charlotte Shearn, of 6 Mill-yard, pleaded guilty to a charge of assaulting her husband, Patrick Shearn, on Saturday night.—The com- plainant stated that on the night in question he returned from the circas and found his wife drunk. He complained to her of her conduct and told her she ought to give up taking drink. She then lifted a scraper and struck him a violent blow on the hand.—The defendant said she thought it was time she did something:to him when there was nothing in the cupboard for the children. She complained that he did not give h,,r any of his wages.— Questioned by the Mayor, the compl dn- ant stated that he gave his wife 2a 6d a day. He dare not give her his wages for fear she would spend the money on drink. —The Mayor bound the defendant over ta keep the peace for six months. Warty'. DAMAGE BY A Boy.—Eflward Lowe, a boy. nine years of age, living with his parents st 43. King-street, war charged with wilfully committing damage to the frame of a window at 3, Flag-lane on the Bth May, the property of Henry Clara—When asked to plead, the lad admitted cutting the window frame.—Mr Clare said he had been troub!ed for some length of time by re- peated damage to the frame of his shop window, and bad offered a reward for the detect on of t...e offenders. On the date named he saw the defendant cutting the frame with a knife. Two strips, about twelve inches long had been Cut out. He estimated the damage at ss.- Detective Barlow stated that his attention was cane ato the window by the last witness. Two strips had been cut off from 10 to 12 inches long. He saw the shavings on the floor.—The magistrates Imposed a tine of 5s and costs.</p>
        <p>MOND&amp;Y.—Before Mr. F. Monks (in the chair), Councillor Srnethurst and Mesars,T Birchall and S Wright</p>
        <p>THE ALLEGED THEFT OF LEAD.--jamas Barratt,-who last week was charged on suspicion with being in unlawful possession of a quantity of lead and failing to give a satis- factory account 6f it was again brought before the magis- trates Superintendent Monaghan said the prisoner wts remanded in order that inquiries might be made. The police had been unable to discover the owners of the had and with the magistrates' permission he would withdraw the charge and the police would keep the lead until they found the owners.—This course was agreed to. THEFT BY A Bo.—Jehn Taylor. 13 years of age, residing at 31, James-street was charged with stealing from the stall of A.tfred Peacocke in the Market-place a horn watch cover value ld.-1-te pleaded guilty.—Superintendent Monaghan said that at 11.30 on Saturday morning P.C. Clough was on duty in pl,in clothes in the Market and saw the pri,oner loitering about the stalls In a suspicious manner. fie watceed him until 2 o'cicck and saw him take the watch cov-r from Mr. PeacOcke's stall. He brou ht him to the Police Station sod on being charged he admitted stealing the article. Prisoner Faye a false name and address.—Toe magistrat .5 ordered the boy to receive six strokes with the birch rod. A DESERTER.—A respectably dressed young man named Isaac Houghton was charged with being a deserter from the 2nd Royal West Surrey R ,ghnent, stationed at Woking Barracks Once May 6th, 1893. P.S. Cheeney said that (al account of information he receiysd he stopped the prifoner on Sunday night on Bewsey Bridge and told him that he was wanted for dose ting. He at once admitted that he was a deserter, and said he had been decoyed away frorn the Bar- racks by another man.—Prisoner was remanded to Manehes- ter to await an escort.</p>
        <p>OB3TRUCTING A PoLICEMIN• —James Quigley. 25. Naylor. street, was charged with comraittin4 a breach of the peace by acting ha a disorderly m nner in Scctiand-ro id on the sth Of May.--P.C. Clough stated that at 6.45 on the date in question he was persuading a man who was drunk to go home with his wife. Defendant who was un •er the influence of drink was near Cockhedge-lane, Lill commenced making ive of bad language and shouting at him causing a crowd of 200 people to assereb.e. Had not P.C.'s Fish and Taylor came on the scene 1 e would probably have been seri 4usly assanit-d.—Defendant was bound over to keep the peace anti ordered to pay the costs. Dloftin Gammisci.—john Ellison, 8, Arnie-street, James Bigby, 79, Ellesmere-street. Charles Beckett, 13, Wellington- street, and James Grady 69, Birehall-street, were summoned for playing at pitch and toss near Howley Tannery on the 6th inst.--P.C. Turner stated that at 4 p.m. on Sunday the 6th Inst. he and PC. dough were on duty in plain clothes in the neighbourhood of Howley. They saw the defendants tossing with money. They caueht Beckett and Ellison and brought them to the Police Station and afterwards ascer- tained the other defendants' names. G tmbling was very prevalent in that neighbourhood anti the police had a very difficult task in arrestieg the off.mders as they had a number or seonts.—A. tine of 5s and 00315 1s), was imposed.</p>
        <p>_ HAVING YOUNG BIRDS IN HIS Posimestors.L-Samuel Mil- lington. 36, Cozkhedge-lane was summoned for having young birds in his possession after March sth (the el se season) P.C. Clark stated that on the Bth inat he was passing de- fendant's house. In consequence of what defendant's daughter told him he visited the house and found that defen +ant had two young blackbirds from a month to six weeks old in his possession. He said he bought them for 3d —lnspector Brown. R.S.P.C.A., said that he saw the defendant the same night. He t id him that he knew he was doing wrong it d he should not have done so if ho had not had some drink. Witness asked to be allowed to see the birds and defendant said he had turnel them out since the constable's visit. The magistrates reprimanded the de- fendant and fined him in the cdsts (2s 6 I). WEDNESDLY. —Before Mr. Biretta!! (in the chair) Coon.</p>
        <p>Runcorn Petty Sessions.</p>
        <p>Momps.Y.—Before Messrs,. Henry . Reynolds (presiding), and</p>
        <p>W. Timmins</p>
        <p>TEMPORARY TRANSF ER. —JORH Lithtn Wal granted temporary authority to sell intoxicants at the Mill Stone, Mill-street, Hurscorn. lately occupied by Henry Vorton. SCHOOL ateEs.—Charles Twigg, Halton-road and William Eaton, Gilbert-street, wero etch fined 5s including costs for neglecting the education of their cbildren.—Schoul-visitore Bowyer and Howard proved the cases. DRUNICENNESS.—WiIIiam Price, Hughes Court pleaded guilty to being found drunk in Bridgewater-street, by Con- stable Bratt, on the 3rd inst., and was fined ss.—Thomas Carrington Duke-street admitted being drunk and dis- orderly in High street,on Saturday afternoon last —Constable Wilson said the man was most unruly in a farniture shop,and be was called to him on several occasions. The defendant also threatened the shop boy and in consequence witness felt obliged to look him up.—Fined 5s and 83 6d costs ory days.</p>
        <p>Ali ECCENTRIC YOUNG MAN.—Thomas Micidietoll, a respectable-looking younq man, lodging in Vine-street, was cnarged with being drunk and disorderly in Devonshire- square on the 12th of May.—He pleaded guilty to being drunk, but as for disorderly conduct" he only spoke,' and was not guilty of that offenee.—acting-Sergeent Samuel Gregory deposed to being in the vicinity of the Square in company with Constable Brett, when he saw the d dendant and several other young men came past. They were all drunk. They went past and the defendant called him a vile name. Witness went up to him and told him he would summon him.—Superintendent D. Pearson said that the defendant had been before the Court previously. He was a man that acted in a very extraordinary manner when under the influence of drink. On the last occasion ha had climbed up a post.—Fined Sc. FOOTBALL IN THE FTREETS.—Asidrew Bowman (13), Walter Woodward (14), Alfred Avis. Thomas Spender and Edward Hinman, of Albert-street, were summed for play- ing football in the streets to the annoyance and danger of the passengers using the same on the 21st of May . —Spender and Rieman did not appear ; and, on the representation of Avis' mother, that witnesses of the boy's innocence would be forth- coming, the cases against Spender, Hinman and Avis. were adjourned to the next sessions.—Constables Wilding. Gregory, and Brett swore to seeing the defendants playing with a ball in the street.. They were kicking it and throwing it about. Wilding further said he bad been detailed off, in plain clothes, specially to leok after Albert-street, en amen ,t of written complaints having bean received by the Superintendent, of the great nuisance from , the practice of football playing there. He first observed the boys about 6.15. Then at half-past seven and at 8-15 he and Constable Gregory suece-ded in capturing some of them and got the names of the others.—Bow nan and Woodward were each made to pay 6s 6d costs, as first offenders A FIRMER AND HIS Don's LICENSE. —Alfred LOMAS. farmer. Runcorn was summoned on the information of John Donnellan, of Revenues, for oe the 12th of Febtuary, keeping a deg without a license.—Defendant pleaded guiliy. , —John Donnellan said ),(3 visited the farm kept by the de- fendant where he found three d)gs. As a farmer the defen- dant was entitled to an examption for two. dogs.--Defend- ant said his third dog was not sis months o'd —Mr. Lowry (supervisor ot Inlend Revenue, Chester), said if that was to be the defendant's defence, he woul pursue a different course 04 prosecution. He was dealing en the aseurniAlon that he p'eady guilty.—Defendant amended his plea and said he was not guilty.—Mr. Lowry theu explained that the defea- dant was entitled to an exemption for two (legs —(if he ap- plied for it)— but he had not applied for exemptions. And as the condition of the exemption implied the binding of the defennant over in £l.) that he woull use taiadogs,for no other purpose than tending to his sheep and cattle ite., possibly he might have some very good reison for his not applying for the exemptions. He contended th-y could even hive taken pro- ceedings against the defendant or having three unlicensed dogs. Therefore even it the dogmas not six months old,tt was clear that the Bench would be justified in convieting. —John Doninellan said last December he had forwarded the:necessary notices to the defendant for him to fill up for his exemp- tions and on the 30th of December he also called upon him Defendant did not claim any exemptions. Witness also had couversati n with the defeedant, and he had said " the dog was nine month, old to the best of his knowledge, but not 12 months old."—Defendant said he did not recollect that c ,n- -versation, as the dog was not six months old. As for his exemptions he was under the impression he had sent in the claims for them until Slr. Dannehan called.—The Oh Orman. said he was afraid that the defendant bad not taken the trou le to apely for the exemptions, and he would be fined 10, and 145 63 costs.</p>
