      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-05-18_WidnesExaminer_art0080" n="art0080">
        <head>STOCKTON HEATH.</head>
        <p>STOCKTON HEATH.</p>
        <p>A VAGRANT SENT TO GAOL.—Before Mr Thomas Tinsley and Mr. John White, at the Warrington Occasional Court on Monday, Henry Shaw was charged with sleep- ing in an outbuilding at Stockton Heath early on Sunday morning and having no visibto useans of subzistence.— Sergeant Bourne said he found the prisoner asleep in a staple near the Loudon Bridge, Stockton Heath. He had h,id complaints from a Stockton Beath gentleman that the prisoner had been sleeping on a stack belonging to him. Prisoner was c )ntinuatly drunk and was in the habit of sleeping out.—Ho was sent to gaol for 83Ven days.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-05-18_WidnesExaminer_art0081" n="art0081">
        <head>SHOCKING SUICIDE AT MARTINSCROFT.</head>
        <p>SHOCKING SUICIDE AT MARTINSCROFT.</p>
        <p>A Labourer Cuts His Throat and Shoots Himself.</p>
        <p>A determined suicide occurred at Martinscroft at about 3-30 on Monday afternoon. It appears that a labourer named Thomas Owen who resides in Battery-lane proceeded to his work in Warrington on Monday morning, making arrangements with hisswife to meet her in town. Mrs. Owen was sur- pried to see him return at three o'clock, and when she asked him for the reason be replied "All the people I meet are laughing and talking about me." Lie at once proceeded to a drawer and took out a razor. His wife attempted to take it from him,and in so doing received several cuts on her hands. She then ran out.for .assistance to Owen's mother, who resides a short distance away, and on returning they found the deceased lying in the kitchen, dead, with his razor and gun by his side. There was a gaping wound in the throat, and a gun shot in the left breast. It seems that the deceased, who was 40 years of age, had been suffering from muscular rheumatism, and had been attended by Dr. Fox of Warrington, but on Monday morning he appeared to be in good health. The Inquest.</p>
        <p>Th? inquest was condueted at the Elorse and Jockey Inn, Marooscraft, on Tuesday evening, by Mr. S. Brig- house, County Coroner. Mr. James Daintete was fore- man of the jury. Sarah Been Owen, the deceased's wife, said her husband was 30 years of age and was a labourer. He left his work at Cooper's Brick Works, Woolston, about three weeks ago sufferiug from muscular rheumatism. 'The workmen coliec;ed a sum of money for him and gave it to ' witness. She told deceased what they had done. While ne was ill he often said to her that people were staring at him and talking ahout him. He also eaid he had heard a gang of men say that he was lazy. That seemed to upset him and he was ceatinually talking about it and was low spirited. Oa the 10th inst.. ha, went to work at Messrs Greening's new works in Lovely- lane, Warrington, and continued to do so until Monday. At half-past five that morning he left to go to work, and returned at half-past three in the afternoon. She asked him for the reason of his return and he said "I've jacked up." She asked him what for, and he replied, " Why,l they have all been talking about me, and staring at me, SJ I thought I would finish up." He then ran to the cupboard and got his rani.. Sae tried to take it from him, and in the strugele cut her hands. See said "Don't Tom." Be opened the rant- and cut his throat. She ran for assistance to lee mother'seand when she got back he was lying on the ki;chen floor with a gun beside him. mere was a gun shot wducd in his left breast. He was not quite dead. See held him n up,and he expired almost immediately. He was a good husband, and as far as she knew had no reason to take away his life.—The jury returned a verdict that deceased committed suicide wnile of unsound mind by cutting his throat with a razor and ebooting himself with a gun. It is a curious coincidence that the lanquest held at the Horse and Jecky Inn, Martinscrofe.,.* was upon the father of the deceased.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-05-18_WidnesExaminer_art0082" n="art0082">
        <head>DEATH OF THE REV. !J. WARNOCK, M.A, B.D.</head>
        <p>DEATH OF THE REV. !J. WARNOCK, M.A, B.D.</p>
        <p>Warrington people generally will hear with regret of the dente of the Rev. J. Warnock, M.A., B D., for 24 years the pastor of Sr. John's Presbyterian Church Winwick-street,,who passed away at hie residence in Crosfield-Areet,on Wednesday morning at a quarter past nine. Ia our last issue we reported that be lay in a critical condition. He was suffriring from a serious attack of typhoid fever. While on a visit to Ireland at Easter be became ill, and though he preached at his church in Warrington on Sunday,April 29tbehn gradually became worse-and expired at the time stated. He was 52 years of age and came to Warrington in 1876 from Whale, Donagbadee, County Down, Ireland. He was educued at the Queen's College, and Presbyterian College, Belfast, and at the former college he obtained his M.A. Me subsequently pro- proceeded to Riinburgla and there graduated B.D. Coming to Warrington he was ordained putor of Sr. John's and continued in that capacEy until his death. He conducted his pastoral duties in a quiet and unos- tentatious way, and though he did not take any promin- ent part in public questions he was deeply interested in the Free Church movement. At the United C.)mmunion services in connection with the Free Churches of the town he was invariably present and took part in the services. He also asseciated himself with the Free Church Council of Warrington and District and was frequently I seen on the platform at its public meeting's. • •</p>
        <p>His remains are to be int,..rrtd at Woitu Churcb,Bally- waiter, Ireland.</p>
