      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-05-18_WidnesExaminer_art0084" n="art0084">
        <head>BRITISH WORKMAN'S AND GENERAL ASSURANCE 00, LIMITED.</head>
        <p>THE BRITISH WORKMAN'S AND GENERAL ASSURANCE 00, LIMITED.</p>
        <p>In common with most other leading insurance offices, the British Workman's and General has arranged to close its accounts in December 31st, instead of, as hitherto, at the end of April. The directors report (extracts from which appear in another column), covers a period of eight motaths only, but in these eight months the pro- gress has been continuou, and most satisfaatory, and judging from the fieures, the prominent position to which the Brit sh Workman's and Iteneral haa attaieed justifies its asriertion of a place in the insurance world, in ordinary as well as induazrial business. that should not te regarded as secondary to any kindred institution. During the eivnt months now roeerted upon, a skim of upwards of £60,000 has been added to the aesurance fund are largest increase ever made ,luring a sender period, which added to the sum of -£448,857, the amount of the assurance fu id at the end of Aprd 1899, Orings the amount up to £509,018 and sho.as teat th amo ant of this fund has hen practically doubled in less than three years' time. The increase in the pre- mium income during the period under review was £35,889 which is equivalent by computation to an increise of over £53,000 per annum. In all, the premiums for the period ;tau:tinted to £431,782 or act- ually more than was raie•rd ia the whole year 1896. Facts like these prove at what a rate the business of this grar: campany has been growing since the reformed manage. ment took office. Considering that the claim rate had been excepturially heavy, doub:lrse accelerated by the severity of the weather the influenza epidem;e, awl the depressing ev, eta of the closing months of 1899 sal also ' that no less a sum that £36,485 has been paid out in r‘ls pect of maturing endowments and endowment a4sur ances, it is the more remarkable that such round pro- gress should be made.Netwi hstanding the large number of policies falling in the directors report, .to: only has this business been rep:ace I but a nets bacreale h in addition been made equivalent to an increase of 57650 new policy holders proaucing a new anr,ual premearn income of £42,353. Another rati?fictory fe.tore about the report is that at tlfs increased bus-ne, s has b •nn coo- ducted without any increase in the managteneat exoeasee Altogether this report for two-thirds of a year once more proves conclusively that a great industrial and ordinary life assurance association may carry out plans for the reduction of expens's without in any way interfering with its business progress, its high ammercial standing or with the confidence of the public in the wisdom of it, management. The main features of the report name!y the increase of business and the Mirked growth of th- assurance fund are results of which the management may well be proud, and augur well for the future in- creased success and pr,strrity of the British Workmen's and General Asrurauce Company, and we believe, to tirt the words of one the Director, that the Company has enrived at a stage at which its businese will increrve bg leaps and bounds. To MOTHEB.9.—Mirs: Winslow's Soothing Syrup has been used for over fifty years by millions of mothers for their :3hildren while teething, with perfect. suceliss. It will relieve the poorsufferer immediately It is pleasant to taste ; and produces natural nnfet steep, oy relieving the child from pain and the ittst. ehernb awakes '• as bright as a button."o, all Chemists Is lid per bottle.—Advt.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-05-18_WidnesExaminer_art0085" n="art0085">
        <head>ALLEGED STRANGLING OF A RUNCORN YOUTH.</head>
        <p>ALLEGED STRANGLING OF A RUNCORN YOUTH.</p>
        <p>Prisoner Committed to the Quarter</p>
        <p>Sessions.</p>
        <p>The alleged strangling case at Runcorn, which has caused considerable interest in the district, was heard at the Runcorn Police Court on Tuesday last, before Messrs F J Norman and W Handley. after two adjourn- ments necessitated through the injuries to the boy Sargent being so great as to prevent his appearance at Court. On this occasion, however, the boy had been brought to Court in a cab, and accommodated with a chair is Court. Ha was attehded by his two sisters. The prisoner, Edwin Parkinson, a well-klown char- acter, occasionally in the employ of the Ship Canal Coaq.any, was formally charged with on the 28th of April, at Runcorn, unlawfully and maliciously causing grievous bodily harm to one Leonard Sargent, and the following evidence was taken :</p>
        <p>Ann Miller said she was the wife of John Miller, and resided at the Duke's Stables, off Waterloo-road. Her husband was in the employ of the Manchester Sbip Canal Company, Li charge of the stables. About 10 o'clock on Saturday night the 28,h April, she was in the house which adjoins the stables, and in consequence of hearing a disturbance she went to the drivers' kitchen and SAW the prisoner standing over the boy Sargent, who was on a form. Tne prisoner had hold of him by the throat and was trying to strangle him. She was frightened and ran back home and told her husband. ' She returned again and the priso ler was then on top of ' the boy at the back of the walk just at the entrance to the yard. Prisoner was trying to strangle him again. She heard prisoner say " I'll murder you before I have done with you." Two or three men then got hold of the prisoner and pulled him away and Sargent was carried into the drivers' kitchen. He appeared to be exhausted and could not speak. The doctor was then sent for and she was present when Dr. MeDougall examined the by. Sue heard the doctor say that his windpipe was broken. The prisoner was present at the time. The doctor ordered the boy to be put to bed and kept very qaiet. Walter Harvey said he was a horse driver in the employ ef the Manchester Ship Canal Company, and resiied at 24, Eoglish-row' Runcorn He knew Sargent, who was alio employed by the Can' 1 Company as a driver. On Saturday, the 28th Aprilrs Sargent came to the Duke 3 stables from Preston Br -,ok about half-past one and put his horse up. Soon after prisoner came into the stable and commenced to spaarrel with Sargent about the horse. Prisoner said to ohm ".Why don't you let one of the other drivers have teat horse as you are the youngest. driver." Sargent replied "I have been given the horse and I am going to stick to it until I get orders to give it up." Witness said "That's it, you do as Mr. Roustage told you to do with the horse and keep it." Prisoner then came to witness and said : "If you can do anything you had better come outside." Prisoner then got hold of witness by the collar and chucked him outside into the yard. After the prisoner began to curse and swear at Sargent who picked up his basket and went upstairs. The prisoner followed him and witness heard a scans. A few minutes afterwards Sargt nt came down, and made a complaint to witness. They went to the Tcp Locks and told the canal police- man, who came to the Duke's kitchen and told prisoner he had better go into the kitchen and get off to bed. The prisoner went on to the Waterloo Bridge. Witness re- turned to the kitchen a few minutes after ten. Sargent was standing by the fire place, and the prisoner was close to him, and without saying a word he struck Sar- gent and knocked him down. Sargent ran out of the kitchen followed by the prisoner. Witness did not see S agent again until he was lying on a form in the kitchen. The prisoner was standing close by and said to some people who had carried Sargent in that they had nothing to do with what he had done to Sargent. Witness did not hear prisoner threaten Sargent. _ _ . _ _ _ _</p>
        <p>P.C. Edwards stated that from information received at 11.15 p.m. on Saturday the 28th April, he wont to the Duke's Stables, where he found the accused with a number of other persons. He was under the influence of drink, and had his cat and vest elf, and was behav- ing in a disorderly manner. Sargent was lyieg on his back on a form and was partly unconscious. Witness asked him where he was hurt, and he said something in reply in a low broken voice but witness could not under- itand him. Sargent then raised his hand to his neck. Witness opened his shirt and examined his throat and found that it was injured. He sent a messenger to Dr. McDougall, who came and examined him in his presence. The prisener was there also. Witness heard the doctor say that Sargent's windpipe was broken, whereupon he arrested prisoner and took him to the Police Station. By direction from Soperictmdent Pearson, witness after- wards charged the prisoner with doing grievous bodily harm to Leonard Sargent ky attempting to strangle him, and he replied : "I did not intend to do him any harm I only intended to push him." The muffler producsd was banded to him on'the night in question by the wit- ness Ann Miller.</p>
        <p>D. William Bethume raid he was a surgeon and was at present locum temens to Dr. McDougall. On Monday, the 30'.h April, he saw the injured boy at 59; Wellington- street, where he had been removed. On examing him he found that he was rec pvering from the effects of the shock, and complained of much pain in the upper part of the neck. He had a little difficulty in swallowing. On examining.his throat externally he found that the upper part of the windpipe was severed from its adjustments or otherwise broken. He had been under his care ever since and he was not quite out of danger. What he was now suffering from was chiefly weakness. Leonard Sargeet, the injured youth, who spoke with considerable diffieuity,stated that he was a horse driver in the employ of the Manchester Ship Canal Company.On Saturday the 28:h April, he went to Preston Brook with a boat, and on the way the horse he was driving picked up a nail and was not fit to come back aeain. Mr Rona- tage, the foreman at Preston Brook, gave him another horse and be got back to Ruocern seine time between 12 and 1p m. About 3 p.m., in the afternoon he was cleaning his horse in the Duka'a stables when the prisoner wanted to take the horse from him, but he would not let him have it. Prisoner then pulled off his coat and said : " I'll knock a horse out of you if you do not turn it up." Witness then walked away and left him standing by the horse, and did not see him again until 8 o'clock in the evening. Witness was in the kitchen belonging to the stab'es having his supper. John Miller, foreman over the stables, came in and gave him orders to get ready for Sutton Weaver for 11 o'clock. He packed his basket up and left it on the table, and then lay down on the form to have a sleep. Whilst on the form the prisoner get hold of him and threw him over the table, and said "I'll finish you before morn- ing" A man named James Worrell came in at the time, and took.prisoner away from him. As soon 53 witness got up he took up his basket and went upstairs. A knife he had in his band he laid on the bed and went to hang his batleet; on the wall. On turning round he saw that the prisoner had followed him, ani had the knife in his hand to strike him with; Witcess caught hold of his hand and pushed him on one side and ran downstairs. The prisoner followed him, and as ha ran into the yard prisoner tripped him down with his foot. When on the ground prisoner again got hold of him by the throat with both hands, and said " I'll murder you before morning." Be tried to strangle him, and witness became unconscious. The next thing he remembered was being carried away by some men. He did not say anything to prisoner that he remembered to provoke him. He had since been under the doctor. He was out a little on Sunday fcr the first time since. He bad the scarf produced round his neck at the time.</p>
        <p>/liked if he had any questions to as Sargent,prisoner "I have nothin4 to ask him, but I never used a knife of any description. I ne.ver saw a knife."</p>
        <p>Prisoner was then asked if he had anything to say and he replied, "No, sir." Mr. jihnsin (magistrates' clerk) : You have no wit- nesses to call P Prisoner : No, sir. Mr. Johnson : You can give evidence if you wish. Prisoner • lam very sorry to see what I did to the lad. I was in drink.</p>
        <p>The nmistrates thrYn committel prisoner to the Quarter 583610118, July 4th.</p>
        <p>Farm Fire at Acton Grange.</p>
        <p>Serious fiarnage was done to the farm premises of Mr. Peter Hall, Acton Grange, on Sunday, by a fire which broke out shortly before noon. Immediately on the receipt of information, Superintendent Inca ' of the Wilderspool Fire Brigade proceeded to Acton Grange with seven men and the engine. On arrival at the farm it was found that a Dutch barn, con- taining from fifteen to twenty tons of baled straw, was blazing fiercely. A strong wind was blowing at the time,and the brigade directed their efforts to- wards saving t:se adjoining building. In this they were successful. A number of implements, includ- ing a traction engine, a thre3hing machine and a straw press were safely removed from the burning building. The remaining contents were totally destroyed. The fire is supposed to have originated from some burning chaff being blown into the barn. The brigade returned to Wilderspool about half-past six in the evening. The damage is estimated at between £lOO and £2OO. Mr. Ilan was away from home at the time of the fire.</p>
      </div>
