      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-05-18_WidnesExaminer_art0115" n="art0115">
        <head>WAR.</head>
        <p>THE WAR.</p>
        <p>LATEST TELEGRAMS.</p>
        <p>MAFEKING. Saturday's Attack, Official Report by the Boers. (Press Association War Special.) PRETORIA., TUESDAY. The following official war bulletin has been i•sued The federals stormed and occupied the forts around Mafekiag on Saturday morning. The same night the federals wlre surrounded with the result, so far as is known, that seven wera killed and seventeen wound ed, an that a number were taken</p>
        <p>prisoners.</p>
        <p>The British clsualt les were fifty killed and wounded.</p>
        <p>Reported Capture of Commandant</p>
        <p>Eloff.</p>
        <p>A Ceatral Now Capstown tslegram, Thursday after- noon, states :—The " Argus " publishes a telegram from Lcurenco Marques, which states that Mafeking was hotly attacked. The Bosrs were determined to do all in their power to take the town. They were repulsed, but with the greatest difficulty. aThe Baas lost heav Hy, Commandant Eloff. the Presi dent's grandson and 90 men were captured. The enemy lost seventeen killed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-05-18_WidnesExaminer_art0116" n="art0116">
        <head>Boers ask Terms of Surrender.</head>
        <p>Boers ask Terms of Surrender.</p>
        <p>Roberts telegraphs that Rundle is at goo°lan. Boere near Ficksburg and B3thlehem appl7ing for terms of</p>
        <p>surrender.</p>
        <p>Christiania Occupied by Hunter.</p>
        <p>It is officially announced that Christiania has been occupied by the British troops under General Hunter. Sir J. Kitson Announces the Relief.</p>
        <p>The Press Agsoc7ation says th it Sir James Kitson announced in one of the Committee Rooms of the House of Commons to-day that Mafeking had been relieved. There does not, however, appear to be any official con- firmation. Sir James's statement was received with cheers.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-05-18_WidnesExaminer_art0117" n="art0117">
        <head>GENERAL BULLER'3 ADVANCE.</head>
        <p>GENERAL BULLER'3 ADVANCE.</p>
        <p>Nearing New c. ast le The War office yes'erday issu?d the fol:o;ving from General Buller : •</p>
        <p>DANNHAUSER, 17 MAY, 12.20 p.m. Second Division have reached Dannhans3r, and I hope my advance patrols are in Newcastle. Fifth Divisioa is escheloned from Elands Laagte to Glencoe repairing the railway, and Fourth Division is at Sundays River Drift on old Newcastle Road. Several Natal farmers are handing over their arms. All reports agree that about 7,000 enemy pacised north very hurriedly on the 14th and 15th.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-05-18_WidnesExaminer_art0118" n="art0118">
        <head>Colonel Blackburne Progressing Satisfactorily.</head>
        <p>Colonel Blackburne Progressing Satisfactorily.</p>
        <p>Mrs. Ireland Blackburn° writes from 17, Roland Gardens, Queen's-gate, SW., to Miss Boyle, 56, Church- street. Warrington, thanking her for having asked her friends to send comforts for Colonel Blackburne's regi- ment (the 3rd South Lancashire Militia), and acknow- ledging the receipt of several articles from local trades- men. She concludes" lam sorry to eay my husband bas enteric fever. He is progressing satisfactorily to recovery, I am thankful to say."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-05-18_WidnesExaminer_art0119" n="art0119">
        <head>RUNCORN VOLUNTEERS AT THE FRONT.</head>
        <p>THE RUNCORN VOLUNTEERS AT THE FRONT.</p>
        <p>The following letter has been received by Captain G. F. Ashton from one of the" RUDC3/4 Volunteers." : Karree Camp. Good Friday, 13th April, 1900.—Captain G. F. Astiton,—Dear Sir.—Just a tew lines to let you know lam going on all right. The voyage was a fairly good one, bar the Bay of Biscay, which was a bit rough, the only place to anch ». down at being Teneriffe. On reaching Capetown we lay to anchor for a wept° of days. We left the S.S. Gascon and went to Green Point Camp, the base of operations. The d Hi we had there was pretty bard. Before breakfast every morning we bad full marching order for over ten miles, that being our captain's idea of colonising us. Fatigue par- ties are told off very often. We had the j3b of pitch- ing tents in the sports ground for the Boer prisoners (Cronje,'s men). A week's stay at Cape- town brought it to a close. We next wee% to Naauwpoort. The journey took two and a half days during which our food consisted of bisc :its and bully beer- We all have a hundred rounds of ammunition in our pouches. Scorpions,tortoises,and snakes are common out here. We caught a scorpion in our tent. General French started his fighting here, the trenches are c'ese to. A week's stay at Naauwpoort brought it to a close, the next place being Norvals Pont, which is situate on the Orange River bank. I hear it gets its name from a Termer who used to punt people over the river before thl bridge was made. his charge being R 1 a time. The bridge here now has ten spans and is a grand piece of work, but the Boers have fairly wrecked it. We ham 0 had plenty to do on it rivet cutting some of us, othare navvying on it with pick and shovel. Af ten ten days we got orders to march to Bloemfontein. we started:on Sun- day. After three days we had the pleasure of escorting a train that had been broken into f rem Springfontein, to Bloemfontein the escort being only halt of the Company ; the others are still on the march. After one night's stay at Bloemfontein in the "Real Camp" we received orders to join the regiment at Sane Camp. We got the train as far as a place called Glee, then marched to the camp; from there the distance being about seven miles. Tents are out of question at the front, we have bivouacs here. Those we make thus :—Two blankets, riflss for poles, bayonets for pegs, and straps to ant as tent ropes; They hold foal., our four being Platt, Timmins, Douglas (a Ist Cheehire chap) and myself. The day after joining the regiment we had the alarm, all men to arms, which all fell to in quick time. We thought there was a bit of fun for us but we were not wanted, the Mounted infantry had captured a thousand head of cattle and a 1 number of Boerstcok after them but they aeon retreated when they saw our artillery. We have been on outpost duty nearly three miles away from camp on the top of a kopje. The rain has been falling now for a few days. Hoping you are quite well,—Believe me, yours obedi- ently, Davin CEUDWICIC. p.S„—We shall be la the next engagement. General Tucker came round while we were on outpost duty and spoke a few words to us.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-05-18_WidnesExaminer_art0120" n="art0120">
        <head>Another Interesting Letter from the</head>
        <p>Another Interesting Letter from the</p>
        <p>Rev. S. H. Ravenscroft. Meeting of Runcorn Men.</p>
        <p>Toe following is a copy of a letter received by Mr. D Sisbrown, Runcorn, from his brother-in-law, the Rev. S. H. Ravencroft,now a Wesleyan Chaplain to ELM. Forces South Africa :—" 92, West-street, Maritzburgh, April 20th, 1900, My dear Brother and Sister, you will be interested to learn that Jam doing duty here as chaplain to the Wesleyan troops. I commenced my work to day and strange as it may seem to you, I came across a Runcorn man, who belongs to the Lancashire Fusiliers. I happened to ask from what part of England he hailed and he replied "Cheshire." I then said "and what part of Cheshire ?" and he respocded " Runcorn." How his face lit up when I informed him I was from •Runcarn ! On mentioning my name to him, I was told he knew it well; although being 20 years my junior he did not know meeAllis name is Collings and he used to live in Vicar's-street. Ho has a brother in the army. Be Spoke of Brunswick Sunday School, and St. Paul's Chapel and of Mr. Jordan who bad been his school- master. I met Collings in the fever ward of the Maritz- burg hospital and found that he was recovering from an attack ot enteric fever. I hope the poor fellow may pull through. We had a good te lk about the best nines. Another man I convers,d w. h told me he was in the front firing-line at Spion Kop, and in three mioutes three men on his immediate right were shot dead, and two men on the left, wte at ha escaped without a scratch. "There leanly One ' saps he "who can bring you safely eut of such great danger." There were two Boers in one of the wards I visited. One of them had been shot through the liver duricg a fight with the British troops near Celenso. As I spent seven years in the Orange Free State I wi.a able to speak to him in his own lang 11 v,e, and be was very pleased to have a coat with a minister. The other Boer was taken prisoner by the British in Natal. He ercaped unhurt frem the fieht. but is now down with pleurisy. I said to him as I to7e to leave "I hope the war will soon be over." Whereupon he made ansaw r " They (that is the British) won't let it bi over. The Africenders watt the Orange Free State and the Transvaal to have their independence, and the British say they shall have no more independence. So they will light it out." I replied "We will talk about the matter another time." The account I sent to the " Recorder " of my visit to some Natal h!ttle fields formed part of the ill-fated 3joeicaes "mail, .and as nearly all the Maritzburg bags were hut, it is very unlikely that it</p>
        <p>reached its dfstination. All the members of my family ars well. We came here this week—two days aro. We occupy a furnished cottage at £9 94 per month. the rent is or course paid for me. We cxpnrt to be here until Dundee is op.n am,iin. With much affeation, I rerriaia, yours as ever. SAUITEL 11. RASENtCROFT.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-05-18_WidnesExaminer_art0121" n="art0121">
        <head>Widnes Man and the Pieter's Hill Charge.</head>
        <p>A Widnes Man and the Pieter's Hill Charge.</p>
        <p>Sergeant O'Connor, 2ad Royal Irish Fusiliers. 6th Battalion.3rd Divisinn,writing from Surprise Hill Camp, Ladysmith, to Mr. B. Baker, Widnes, says that he was in the Union Brigade, comeosed of the 7th Royal Irish, 21st Royal Scots Fusiliers, 23rd Royal Welsh Fusidere. and last, but not least, the 89th Royal Irish Fusiliers. General Barton was in command. He was a brava old man. Everyone was surprised that General Buller did not mention that brigade.as it deserved to be mentioned over the taking of Pieter's Hill. The honour certainly belonged to the Royal Irish Fusiliere and the Scots Fusi- liers of Barton's Brigade, and the three Companies of the Royal Irish Fusiliers who stormed and he'd the hill all night. Three nor-censmissioned officers and a private Of the Irish Fusiliers) were recommended for distinguished conduct on this ecca- g on. Thi ir lames were Colour-Svp„eont O'Brien, Sergeant Finning, Private McNally (F Company) and Sergeant Mclver (H Company). Colour-Sergeant O'Brien took command of the company, all the officers being either killed or wounded. He hoped these brave fellows got what they deserved. Even then, after the lapse of so many weeks, one cow(' see the effects of Cie terrible suffering endured by these poor fellows during the siege written in their faces. One could not help, on looking at the impregnable positions wrested from the Bcers,but wonder how in thename of fortune our fellows ever took the places held by the Beers. The reserve men had " proved " themaelvee in this war, and he thought when it was over the people in England ought to petition for their return home at once. There was a terrible lot of sickness not to speak of the poor wounded lads, who bad accomplished almost impossible work. Writing with regard to the" wearing of the green" he said the honour was appreciated by all ranks. He concluded with good wishes to those in Widnes.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-05-18_WidnesExaminer_art0122" n="art0122">
        <head>St. Helens Woman at Kimberley. Interesting Letter.</head>
        <p>A St. Helens Woman at Kimberley. Interesting Letter.</p>
        <p>Mr. D. Curren, who is wel;-known in St. Helens and district as Sir David Gamble'e gamAkeeper, has received a letter from his sister, Mrs. E. Eck 3rslev, proprietoress of the Wolseley Hotel, Transvaal-road. Kimberley, who with her husband, endured the seige. Sue says :—"We received your letter all right, and were very pleased to hear from you, hoping that you are all right, as this Leaves me sn at present. I caneot tell you how low, this cruel war is ening to last. It is no use telling you how the Boers surrounded us, as yeu can read all about it in the papers. I think some of the Ye,manry are stationed at pres-nt ab eat five miles out of Kimberley, at Cartel's Farm, when we lost so many ,four brave man in a sortie with the enemy. Amongst them is Sirgeant-maj sr Brady who with six other non-coms, of the Cape Mounted Police dined with us on the Sunday evening previous to the sortie, which Was on the Tuesday, and a very inter- esting party it was. The event was talked of amongst them, anti each one pledged the others in case of falling to see them properly buried. They also made me join them, and I kept my promise to the dead. We lost thirty-two men in that affair,amongst them Major Scott Turner. also J. Hastings. a man who boarded with us in 1892. He was in Koffyfentein, in the Free State and was commandeered to fight for the Boers,but be escaped and joined the Light Horse. We also lost one of our hoarders at Dornfield after the relief. He was a young Irishman named Sutton, and, strange to say he was a nephew of Delaney who was hanged at Manchester. One man dies fighting for his country, and the other the reverse. He was a fine youne fellow, 6ft. 2in., with a genial smile and a kind word for everybody. He was employed at D 9 Beers, and volunteered in the Light Horse. Mr. Gattrell sends his love to all of you. He was in Johannesburg when the war broke out. He was a boss in one of the mines. He is one of the refugees. I am sending some books with illustrations of the siege of Kimberley. They will net be ready until the end of May. I think they will be very good. With the South Lancashires.</p>
        <p>Mr. W Rosterne has received the following letter from Lance-Corporal J Robinson, dated from Elandstante, April 16th :—" Just a line or two to let you know I am still alive. I found W Price when we arrived at Sur- mise Hill. He was in the best of health at the time, but lam sorry to say that he took fever at our last camp but one, called the Battlefield Ridge. I expec:; he was Sent to hospital. for I have not heard anything of him since. The night we left Battlefield Ridge camp we marched about eight miles to the bottom of a hill which the Boers held a few days ago. Tbey lett the day before we got there. We arrived at nine p.m.,and were ordered to lie down. At one o'clock we again formed and marched or scrambled in skirmishing order for about two miles to a small mound, where we haltot Majcr Hall told lea that the Bler positions were in front of us and that wetmust expect a heavy shell fire the , next morning a daybreak. He also told us to make r stone sangars and trenches. We worked until about half-past three in the morning, and then lay down for a !lit of sleep. We were awakened about half-east four and looked to our rifles, but we were disappointed, for when daylight came we found that the Boers had done another 'bunk.' At eleven o'clock we marched to Mount Gana, where we encamped until Good Friday. We then struck camp and marched about four miles up country. We expect to stay here for a few days. There are plenty of Boer horses about here. I saw eight sold for Ss and 103 each. Any man can have one if ha takes the trouble to catch it. We were on escort to the naval guns yester- day, ard on outpost all last night. The scouts have just brought-in a Boer prisoner. He is arum looking fellow, but would not say anything that would lead teethe dis- covery of his party. News has just been received that Roberts has had a big victory, but we don't know whether it is true or not. You in England know more about the war thee we do. We are quite in the dark as regards movements other than our own." Lancashire Lads Second to None.</p>
        <p>The following has been received by Mr. E. Newman of Esrlestown :—Army Pay Corps, Maritzbury 20:h April, 1900,—1 am delighted to hear of the enthusiastic manner in which the men at your Earlestoo n Works showed their sturdy patriotism on hearing of the relief of Ladysmith. I glory in the manner in which they, in common with all who have the Nation's welfare at heart, showed their intease appreciation of the grand work of good old Baler and bis men. When you re- member that Ladysmith was within a few days of starvation, you will partly understand what the fearful strain on the minds of the two Generals most particu- larly concerned was likely to be—the relieved and the relievinv. There were tremendous crowds at the Maritz- burg railway station to we come the military and civilian friends returning from Ladysmith, and I had the pleasure of a long chat with a civilian who came to stay a few days with my landlady, as soon as possible aft !I. the seige was raised. He brought many interesting relics thouch gruesome, amongst which half a " ships " biscuit. Oh! such a biscuit. A day's rations never- theless ; bits of shells (not cockle shelk),Nlauser bane's, . . . .</p>
        <p>siege post cards, Sze., &amp;c., were not unentertaining altnough many painful memories were aroused. You mentioned a Lancashire regiment in your letter, as having had a rough time of it. So they have my by, but there's one thing you must please not forget. every Lancashire lad, still in p)ssession of health would rough it again ; nay is only too anxious t ) ron..:h it for the sake of the " Great White Mother " and the glorious traditions of the dear old flag. lam perfectly certain Earlestown is proud of the Lancashire lads at the front I know the Lancashire Reziments, know them well,and you may tike it from me that in pnysive, grit, pluck. smartness and dogged determination they are second to none.</p>
        <p>Boer Spies Captured by a Warrington Soldier.</p>
        <p>A letter has been received by Mr. George Mounfield, Manchester-road, Warrit.gton, from Private G. Fearnley, Volunteer Cimpany, lsl-..Fouth Lancashire Regiment. In it the writer says ;—" V. 3 are at present camping at Elandslaagte. lam sorry to say we buried one of our men at Durban. His name was William Blackwood. We had a bit of a skirmish at Elandsliatzte. 'there were about 40 men of the Somersetsbire Regiment wound.A. 'the South African Ligkt Horse and the Boers had a race for a big hill. The Light Horse took the bill first and they made the Boers retreat. We then followed them up and took the position. Beyond Ladysmith the first place where we were under fire was at Surprise Hill. We had a " rough do "at Sunday River.—Snells were dropping in the camp. One man and two horses were killed. After we had a skirreith we made tne Boer's scamper out of the hills. Oor naval guns put the Boers guns out of action. We are at present following them up. We do most of our marching at night. I stiall net be at home this Good Friday. We shall have no hot cross buns here. We shall perhaps have Some bread or oiscuits and jam. I think I shall be at home about Walk- ing Day." A letter which has been written by Sergeant J Howard of the South Lancashire Regiment alludes in a P.S. to Privates! Herbert and G Fearnley. Sergeant Howard says" they have this moment brought into camp four Boer spies and twenty horses—and they got their deserts too."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-05-18_WidnesExaminer_art0123" n="art0123">
        <head>Interesting Letter from Driver J. Whitley. The Boer Prisoners at Capetown.</head>
        <p>Interesting Letter from Driver J. Whitley. The Boer Prisoners at Capetown.</p>
        <p>Writing to his brother, Driver James WhiAcy, of the Royal Horse Artillery 4th Cavalry Brieale, who is the sin of Mr. James Whitley, of stockton Hoath as "We had it pretty rough for the first we k during the passage, especially whilst crossing over the Bay ot Biscay. One sailor died on board and 13 horses. At Las Palm's we got a supply of coal. Ve had on board 450 horses and cattle, acout 40 waggons, seven guns,</p>
        <p>forage and other requisites and about 530 troops all told. We had a spleeditt bill of famigetting extras for breakfast, dinner and tea, which I think is a feather in the cap of the Johnsen line,end the "Pinmere " was like a floating town. We exercised our to,esss on board as often as possible. We arrived at Capetown on the I.Sith of March and cot our neountaie battery guns, baggage, wagons, and horses off as quickly as possible. Here we saw thousands of Boer prisoners, and Sir George White and his staff came in from Ladysmith. There is very fine scenery around here. Table Mountain stands out most prominently. We were kept pretty °wry on board: Talk about roughing it, nothing beats it. We were up to the eyes in dust all day, watering, feed- ing, cleaning and exercising horses, etc. We had never finished; but I could do my work nicely, taking every thing in good part, working and whistling at the same time, You ought to have seen me then with my goggles on to protect my eyes from the dust. We are like proper ' warriors now: We had horns and mules fastened to the , waggon wheels and our harness just behind them ready for any alarm or emergency that might occur. We were working all day and had only a small tent to rest in. IThere were 12 in a tent and everything was reaiylfor moving at a minute's notice. The troops are getting treated well in Capetown. They have grapes and cigars and everything else they could wish for. I saw thousands of prisoners and I never witnessed such a sight in my life. There were grey beaded old men and beardless youths all properly dejected and worn out. I can assure you that they are glad they are pris:mers. Some told us that they got nothing to eat before. They were just like a lot of filthy ragged looking tramps. Here the Union Jack of old Ensland was flying on the top of every house and building and the Kaffirs and Zulus wore red white and blue in their jackets. There were a'so thousands of troops waiciag for orders to proeseed to the front. We came up from here with a large quantity of ammunition, &amp;c. All here are in high spirits and desire to be sent on to the front to capture Kruger and his army. Some say they want to get in his garclee and taste his grapes and other fruit. Some say they will have a smoking concert in his best room and others say they will have a tune on his best piano and make him sit in the corner while they sing " Soldiers of the green." Some say they will have a look at his cash box and ask him how much he wants out of it and all thoroughl;, enjoy the sport. Tell my brothers I. am fairly on the warpath now ; not in the cycling club but a galloping club, driving my gun carriage and ax horses and have e,ot bully beef and biscuits going on a tour through Africa: We don't stop till we get to Pie- tons. I shall have some wonderful things to tell you when I comp back to old England: At Norvals Pont we entered the Free State Territory and at the Orange river there is a fine bridge which was blown up by the Boers three Weeks ago. It was six o'clock in the evenilg when we arrived at Bloemfontein which is a beautiful place and he order of Lord Roberts no one was auowee out after eight p.m. We had to mareli with en - barges, guns, ammunition, hearsay., Sze., for three rnii-4 out Ade the town into camp where we arrived late for is was piteh d trk. You may imagin 3we had a nice thing on. I couiated 102 stations between Capetown and Bloemfon- tein. It was a long, tiresome journey: lam now with Generals Pole-Carew and French under Lord Roberts in the 4th Cavalry Brigade, Royal Horse Artillery and we are supp reed to make a general advance up the country about the 15th. There are nearly 100,000 troops here ready for the job. Lord Roberts' headquarters are at the late President Steyn'e residence, a fine and roomy building from the top of which floats the Unicie Jack of old England. Just to finish up I hope all are well at home also all my Mends in and near Stockton Heath. Before you get this I hope to be well on my way to Pretoria."</p>
        <p>It will be remembered that Driver Whitley left War- rinaton for Aldershot with the first batch of reservists cane,' out. He was kept horse training at Aldershot, Wo9lwich and Curragh camp, Ireland until February and left Liverpool for the Cape on the" Pinmore "on the 22nd of that month.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-05-18_WidnesExaminer_art0124" n="art0124">
        <head>"Kruger's Illness."</head>
        <p>"Kruger's Illness."</p>
        <p>Private 'I. Moran, Shroohire Light Infantry (batter known locally as Trot) has sent the following cuttings from" The Friend of the Free State," to Mr. William Dillon, Rayon Hotel, Fennel-street :</p>
        <p>HOW KRUGER LOST HIS SWEETHEART. The other day when I had dined, I sat down to read the news, But feeling rather tired I thought I'd have a stuoze ; So as I slept I had a dream, Which I now will tell to you, And woes you've heard my story, No doubt you'll think it's true. I thought I saw Paul Kruger, He was lying ill in bed, And by his friends all standing round Some Natar tears were shed. "it is too late," I beard him say, " To mend my great misdeeds; I feel my time is getting uhort. Pray seni for Dr. Leyds." The doctor soon was on the scene, But he gave them all a fright, For at once be told the patient He was looking General White. "Now describe to me your symptoms," Said the doctor, "and be brief ; You know I am so anxious 'to grant you quick relief." Said Paul -;." I courted Lady-Smith But from her I've had to par:, She's run away with Buller, And she's nearly broke my heart. I hoped that I might Kitchen-sr, But how my poor heart throbs To see her go away and wed A friend of Little' Bobs.' I have those nasty shooting pains, My poor'old bones they rack, Ano I can't forget the bruises That I get from fighting' Mac.'</p>
        <p>I've General French upon my chest, And Methuen on my feet, And Roberts he keeps rising up On everything I eat. I've xot the Baden-Powell And Kimberley 'on my brain, And when I think of Cronje, It hurts like an Armoured Train," The d-ctor said "Your only chance I.—take a chanee of air; The Transvaare getting much too hot You cannot long live there; So from this place without delay, At once you'd better trek, Or you'll have the British Lion With his paws around your neck."</p>
        <p>As the doctor finished sp-!aliing The old man's heart seemed broke, I tumbled off the sofa So from my dream I woke.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-05-18_WidnesExaminer_art0125" n="art0125">
        <head>Warrington Post Office Clerk</head>
        <p>A Warrington Post Office Clerk</p>
        <p>for the Front.</p>
        <p>An interesting presentation took place at the Warring- ton Post Office last Friday evening in connection with the departure of Mr. Jaseph Collin a clerk in the pastel branch for the front.—Mr. Collin has joined the 24th Middlesex Volunteers (P.O. Corps) and last Friday morning he received intimation from the War Office Co attend et Orford Barracks, to he medically exam med. This little function haein,g been got over suceessfully, he was inst'uc-ed to proceed the same evening by the 11 50 p.m. train and report himself at Wellington Barracks, London. The news of his departtire Came as a surprise to most of his c elleagues but a movement was soon on fo-,t to present him with a handsome pipe in case, tobacco. etc.—Mr. Pemberton said they had nad very little time te consicier tuts best means of giving Mr. Catlin a he.arzy send-off, but they meant to do it. In the absence of the Postmaster, who was away on annual leave, he would call upoe Mr. Broa.ciburet to make tbe presentation.— Mr. Breadhurst said the flees of Mr. Collin's departure had come upon them as a surprise, and up to tee moment of being asked to make the presentation he had co conception that any- thing of the kind was to take place.—ese scss glad to see such a prompt and tangible expression of eoed fellowship, and felt sure it wouiel be gratifying to Mr. Collin when tar away fern here te be constantly reminded of this kindly act of his calleagues.—ln con- gratulating Mr. Collin on his good fortune he knew he wa 3 expressing the feelings of all pre,ent. In going to the front it was understod by them that he was not going on active service in the fighting line, and did not, therefore incur the same personal danger as the soldiers. He was going out to help in a good .7orit however, that of assisting in a more expeditious t-ansit of soldiers' letters to and from their friends, and when engaged on this work he trusted lee would maintain the reputation of the Warrington office for sending out good all-round officers. They would have been glad it some of his c dleagues could have accompanied him and should any be subsequently sent he trusted they would find their way to the same camp to work shoulder to shoulder with him. fie was sorry they were so short of staff at present as otherwise he might have expressed more sauguino views as to the prospect of other reponses to the call being:, accepted. TiM9 was pressing and he would close his remarks by again givine voice to what they all felt in :vishieg hurl God-ipeed and a safe return. Mr. Celan suitably acknowledged theeift and was afterwards escorted through the silent streets by ablut 40 of his collengues to the Central station. There was no martial music mad the familiar strains of" Soldiers of the queen " were conspicuoiss by 'heir abscnee hut although the procession was of a iinewhat quiet nature—for which tic:tit:ideas those War- ringt who were in bed at the time will return thanks—no one could have had a heartier reed off And the rinsing cheers and vocal outbursts of his col- leagues must have come upon the innocent travellers in</p>
        <p>the train as a rather rude awakening for some cf them appeared to have quietly passed into a delicious and re- freshing first sleep. Heads were soon popped out of carriage windows and with a kind of "What means this s;r.inge commotion pray" expression on their faces the occupants were curious to see whit the matter was. They con grasoel the situation and when it was found that the train was still on the track and they were in no danger of a colliskn the smile of relief which came over them showed that the uproar was forgiven. As the train steamed out of the station the cheering was again renewed by his colleigues who eeteeen it no small honour to have been cAIPd uoon, and, t 3 have so oromptly furnished one more link in the Empire's Great South African cbsin. 559 J. Co!lin, sailei on Wednesday 16;h iusZ in the Mon; Eagle from Southampton.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-05-18_WidnesExaminer_art0126" n="art0126">
        <head>MILITARY NOTES.</head>
        <p>MILITARY NOTES.</p>
        <p>Captain Denham. Liverpool Regiment, who wae lately trAnsterred from the Depot, at Warrington, to the new 4th battalion of toe regiment, has now been appointed to command a company in the new 3rd battalion of the regiment. Lieutenant Wilkinson, of the 4th Battalion Royal Lancaster R,giment-, has been given a commission in the South Lancashire Regiment, and joins the Ist Battalion in South Africa. Lieutenant Wilkin- son only jeined the Ist Royal Lancashire Militia last January. Toe Colonelcy of the 2nd South Lancashire Regiment which has become vacant owing to the death of Lentenant-General Fox-Pitt-Rivers, will it is understood be given to Major-General Sony- Flood who was many years an officer of the 2nd Battalion South Lancashire Regiment. Major' General Solly-Flood, who is now on the retired list ha inquestionably the best claims to becoming the new honorary coief of the south Lancashire Regi- ment. lie joined the Army in 1849 and served for a time in the Shropshire Light Infantry being appointed to the 2nd Battalion South Lancashire Regiment in 1860 and served with the core until 1877 when he gave up command of the battalion. General Solly Flood served in the North West frontier of India Campaign of 1851 ; the Indian Mutiny Campaign participating in the battle of Cawnpore and siege of Lucknow when he was severely wounded. Alter the war he be- came Aide de Camp to the Commander in Chief in India. He also held many of the important staff appointments, and becoming Major General in 1885 he subsequently commanned a division of the Indian army, being placed on retired pay owing to age in 1891. He is a veteran officer 71 years old. Major I? S Charles, Lancashire Fusiliers. who was a few years ago adjutant of the 4th Volunteer Bat- talion Ciieshire Regiment, will become second in command of the Ch battalion Lancashire Fusiliers.</p>
        <p>Captain Hutson, sth Battalion Liverpool Regi- ment with headquarters at Warrington, on Satur- day embarked for South Africa where he will be emp'oved on special service. Captlin Fras3r, Ist Battalion Cheshire Regiment, has been promoted to the rank of Major in the regi merit which he joined in 1882. He became Captain in 1888.</p>
        <p>Second-Lieutenant Backhouse, sth Volunteer Battalion Chestiira Regiment, has been given a commission in the regular service bec3naing Second- Lieweriant in the Lancashire Pubiliers. He joined the Cheshire Volunteer Battalion, above indicated, only last December.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-05-18_WidnesExaminer_art0127" n="art0127">
        <head>NOTES ON GAMES AND ATHLETICS.</head>
        <p>NOTES ON GAMES AND ATHLETICS.</p>
        <p>Association Football.</p>
        <p>has beon decided tnat the Sc. Helens Rers..Faotball Club, which is connected with Messrs. Pilkington's extensiv: glassworks and collieries stiall, in future, be run strictly on emerr lines. 'rho Recreation C:ub wnen first frsuLded some twenty ycars ago played Rugby football. For the past three years they have adopted Association, which has resulted in a num- ber ff strangers being introduced into th 3 team as pro- fcssi-mals. M. Pakington told a press representative th,t t ler° were a variety of reasons why this de:isi,in should have been come to, but the most important con- sideration was that the club was originally started w t the idea of providing recreation for the firms' employees to indulge in. L ittorly this idea has ham lost sight of, and proiessionalistm has been adopted. The annual meeting of the eub will be held on Saturday night, when the members will have to decide the coarse they will take under the altered cireurnsr,ances. There is a demand for a good Association team in the town, and it is very difficult t) see what course can be adopted by the Recs. to secure the continuance of the position they have long held an the football world.</p>
        <p>Cycling. The Warrington Royal Cycling Club, headquarters, Cairo-str:.et Sctiools, hold their second ladies' run this season to Great Bud worth. Weather permitting. a large turn-out øf the fair sex is anticipated. The cub is in a healthy cond.cion,its paid-up members numbering 34. All tbesl nrimbers it may he remarked are yang enthusiasts of the wheel. The boa. secretary i W. El. Robineon, kin., Wilson Patten-street.</p>
        <p>Th St. Helens C.C. held its annual 50J mires unpaced ioad tim3 trial on Thursday last week. There were 11 eat::e3, but tin woather was all against fast riding, and five only el cted to start, three of whms gave up boforA the turning point at Gangleton was reached. L H Chestarton, who won the competition last year, went through in three hours five minutes, and he was the only man to finih in the time alio tos d (three hours ten minutes). WCo catrapietrid the distance, but throuth tahinz a wrong turn he lot much time, and he failed to get through in the time allowed. Next Thursday week the club holds its 50 miles path race.</p>
        <p>When there is a tiny puncture in your tyre, difficult to disc)ver, make some soapsuds. SJak a sponge in this, and then rub the sponge over the tyre. The escaping air, however small is the puaiture,wi.l produce soap bubbles, and so you may easily locate the spot.</p>
        <p>T. G. Gascoyne's unpaced mile in 2 min 3 2-5 sec at the Surrey meeting was a A plendid performance.</p>
        <p>Teddy Cale certainly does not pick out the most Lay wanl: roads for his daily hunJreds. Last week the veteran was at Tunbridcfe Wells, round about which the) roads are re-y billy ; 24,030 up and still going strong is the latest ',port.</p>
        <p>Many cyclists in Cheshire will doubtless have scsn a notice at the Swan at Cuff's C3rner, at the top of Buck- low Hill, to the effect that motors are net allowed in the yard. Lois is greatly against the interests of the house, and we cannot undoreand why the management da not ca:er for the coming paslme. Other and more wide awake houses will profit by this independence.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-05-18_WidnesExaminer_art0128" n="art0128">
        <head>tettfrs to tbt attar. _ The Rev. F. E. Powell's Protest Against Gambling.</head>
        <p>tettfrs to tbt attar. _ The Rev. F. E. Powell's Protest Against Gambling.</p>
        <p>Sir,—! wis pleased to read in your issue of last Satur- day the letter by the Rev. F. E. Powell in reference to the method of olainirig a considerable sum of money for an £lB watch for the benefit of the Ragged School. If we wish to improve the conditions of huinsn life we 011;; tit not to practice the evils which produce the condi- tions that we try to prevent, relieve, or ameliorate, and gambling is a curse which has brought many a child to a raßged achool. People generally do not sufficiently realize the gigantic evil of wrong methods and the gi.e it impiolance of good ones. Some- times ambLions people adopt almost any means to obtain theirSPlfi3ll pUrpOi.B and SoCe-S4 often covers a muhitode of sinF, but if wrong methods could not be pra3tical they would not succeed, they would not be in the positions they occupy, for better people would be there. I :rzs glad Mr. Powel has called attention to the und,sir ibleness of annealing to selfish feelings and adontirig wronv methods even for the attainment of right and unselfish objects. Yours.</p>
        <p>S. KENYON,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-05-18_WidnesExaminer_art0129" n="art0129">
        <head>May Bth, 1900. Alderman Bolton and the Painting of</head>
        <p>May Bth, 1900. Alderman Bolton and the Painting of</p>
        <p>the Baths</p>
        <p>Sir,.—As a memb?r cf the Operatives Committee of the Warrington Branch of Operative Elouse and Ship Painter, and Delnrators Society, I beg leave to emphatically protet t against the statement of Alderman Bolton, at the last Council meeting, that there were no painters to be got in January to do the baths. Our secretary can prodir,o his unemployed book, to show that there were upwards of 20 respectable and steady men out of work in the month of January—and some of them had been nut of work weeks then. I don't wish to incriminate anyone, but still somebody ought to he painted black. We want more men like Councillor J3MO3 Evans in our Municipal Assembly, all honour to him, for it is ridiculous to close the baths, when they should now be a source of profit.—Yours etc. ' A GENTLEMAN OF COLOUR.</p>
        <p>May 6th, 1900. FREI TO RHEUMATIC AND PARALYTIC SUFFERERS, an Illustrated 1310 x" on the complete horn Cure a these Diseases, preface by P. W. Cole. M.D., who regards the an,hor as the highest authority. Send st.tmp for book to the VENO INSTITUTE, 18, Devonshire-greet, Man- chester. LINqEED COMPOUND Trade Mark of Compound Essonce of Linseei. for Coughs and Colds,—Advt.. EXCHLSIOR CYOLOS at £lO Ws. Sole Agent, J. S. GARLAND, 51, 53, and 55, Bridge-street, Warrington.</p>
        <p>WORKMEN'S COMPENSATION CLAIM AT WARRINGTON A Pulley Accident. On Thursday, at the Warrington County Court, before his Honour Judge James, Joseph Hewitt, of 3, Cloth Hall-yard, Warriugton, claimed under the Workmen's Compensation Act, damages for injuries received whilst following his employment at Messrs. Garton's, seedsm,n, Sze , Limited, of Priers- green, Warrington, on January 9th.—Mr. Browne represented the applicant and Mr. Leslie Scott, harrister,of Liverpool.instructed by Messrs. R,odney, Barrett and Wilkinson,of Liverpool appeared for the respondents. Prior to entering into the case Mr. Scott stated that the respondents admitted the applicant's par- ticulars but contended that the aendent did not arise out of or in the course of ordinary employ- tnent,and secondly that the applicant was guilty o serious and wilful negligence. Mr. Browne, in opening the case, said the applicant was in the employ of the respondents ana at the request of the foreman he helped to pat on a belt round a revolv- ing pulley wlaieh was making 700 revolutions a minute. Be understood that his learned friend suggested that the applic int instead of getting on some sacks of corn to put Ott the belting ought to have used a pair of steps. But on the other hand he wished to point out that the steps could not be used owing to the sacks beine in the way. Whilst standing on the sacks putting on the belt- ing, the applicant's coat was caught in the pulley and he was turned round about a dozen times, receiving severe injuries. Joseph Hewitt, the applicant, living at 3, Cloth Hall- yard, Warrington, said he was engaged as a labourer by the respondents at Friars'-green, Warrington. He was working on the 9th January under the orders of Mr. Harrison, the foreman. He received orders from Mr. Harrison to go and help him to put a belt on the meele;ne, which was used for sieving. He had helped him many a time to put on the belt whilst the pulley was receiving. Witness went on the 9th of January on the top of SOlll6 sacks to unhook the belt, and whilst doing so he was caught and whirled round and round the pulley. For five weeks he received his wages.—Cross-examined by Mr. Scott : The machine was staneing still at the titan he went to put the belt cn,ohnt the shafting was going. The shafting was nearly 8 feet frem the ground. There !was a step ladder to be used for putting on the strap on the pulley. By his Honour; He could not tell where the step ladder was at the time ol the accident. He could not have used the ladder because of the sacks which were piled up within six inches of the shafting. By Mr. Scott: Mr Harrison gave him his order as he came down the staircase. It took two to put the belting on—ono person putting it on the shaft and the other putting it on the machine. He did not hear Harrison say" Give me a hand to put it on the machine ; the ladder is theme." By His Honour: He had always used a ladder before this occasion. There was not room to use the ladder for the reason he had mentioned. The bolting he had to place on the shafting was on a nail in a joist. Tee joist was above the shafting. Mr. Scott suggested that a great deal of time and trouble might be saved if his Honour would visit the works. His Honour said the witness's evidence had not sati?- fied him and he was therefore qaite agreeable to visit the works. The Court adjourned for lunch and his Honour utiliz-d a portion of the time in visiting the works. On resuming the hearing of the case, William Harrison, the manager of the works, said be had shown the judge the ladder which was to be used for putting the belt on. The order be gave to the appli- cant was "just give me a hand with this belt. The steps are there. Get on them."—The steps wertibetween thi ele- vator and the machine. The belt was hanging on the shaft. The belt was only attached to the nail when the machine was stopped for a length of time. On this occasion it had not he,n stepped for any length of tj,TIA. He did not see Hewitt until he was on the sacks. That was be- fore the accident. His Henour remarked that it was marvellous bow the man bad escaped with his life. Witness continuing said that before he could shout the man was going round the shaft.—Croes-examined : Applicant might not have heard what he said about the steps. Dr. Battle, senior house surgeon at the Warrington Infirmary, said he rememnbrei time applicant being ad- mitted to the Infirmary on January 9th and he had been in since. He examined him yesterday. The right hand was permanently partially disabled. lie conld not grip anythieg. He was quite better from the spinal affection. —Cross-examined : The injury was to the third finger. ;That incapacitated the hand entirely. Thomas Rimmer said he: was in the room when the accident took place. He heard Harrison give the order to Hewitt. Re said "Come and give me a hand with this belt." He did not hear him part'cularly say any- thing else. He could not say where the steps were at , that time but they were within ten yards of the machine. His Honour said that Mr. Scott had bettor left the witness at home. Mr • Scott agreed. Mr. Br, wne I have nothing to a9k him. (Laugh- ter.) The witness Harrison recalled by his Honour said that it was his duty to take the straps off when the machine vaeed working. Mr- Scott for the defence said the accident clearly happened in the course of the applicant's employment. The ocly question was whether it as as out of his em- ployment. His Honour : What you say now is against your case. Mr. Scott : I am not going to another court (Laugh ter). His Honour: I think not What is your distinction. Supposing a man were injured in the course of his employment how can you say that it is not out of his employment. Mr. Scott said that the word employment was used in two senses. In one sense it referred to the relationship between master and man. His Honour: There is no question about that in this case. Mr. Scott said that "in the course of" was used in that sense. " Out of " referred to the nature of the work performed. In this case the work was to put the</p>
        <p>bAt on the machine in a pa:ticular way. That was what the man was empioyed to do. He d:d i however in another way, a dangerou4 way, anti injured himself. If his Hooour thought that arose out of employment he did riot pursue Le argument. His Honour : N. H tying seen the prcmises and having had tho lucid assistance of the last witness—</p>
        <p>Mr. cot: : I admit the assistance. (bauebter.) His Honour : There is not the sliehteet doubt about what my decision should be. Tnere is no evidence whatever, as regards the second offence, namely that the accidene wss the cause of the serious and wilful mis- conduct of the applicant. Coetiouing he said that it had been suggested that the acoieent had arisen in the course of, and not out of his employment. He must say that be was quite unable to see the distinction in the facts of the ease. Tkey were not, however, there to argon that nice point of law for ! the reason that he found as a fact teat the belt was hang- ing upon the nail, and if the letter was whore the res. pondente alleged—between the elevator and the machine—lt was not reasonable to suppose that a man ought to have removed the belt while standing upoe the ladder. There was strong evidence that the telt was upon the nail. Had is not beeu, is would have been very easy for Mr. Harri Ton zo have stated where lee placed it on the last occasion, he having admitted that it was his duty to take the belt orY the pulley when the machine stopped. He did not say he 6,1 done so on the last occasion and he was not asked. Tee applicant had not been bright in the manner of giving his evidence, but he certainly was a most honest whness. Mr. Rimmer was called by the respoudents to support Harri- son but he contradicted bins in every material fact. He said the order was "Joe come and give me a hand with this belt." That supported the applicant whi!e entirely contradicting Harrison's evid- ence. He (Rirnmer) also said that the ladder was ten yards away. He (the Judge) believed Rimmer and he disbelieved Harrisen both as regarded the order and the place where the ladder was. Ho had no evidence that the applicant ceuld do any work. Respondents might have called some evidence, and he was quite sure that had it been possible, respondents ha ving the assist- ance of counsel and solicitors, some witaess wou:d have been called. Mr. Scott : I have a doctor.</p>
        <p>His Honour observed that he did not wish to see any witnesses who had not been called. He wished them to go home. (Laughter.) Ho must decide the comp-usa- tion in the absence of evidence called by the respondents to prove that the man could now work. He would have been entitled to 50 per cent of his earnings had he been unable to do some work. It must not be supposed that because a man could do some work that he was not entitled to receive 50 per rent if the Judge thought fit. Mr. Sott . The respondents have promised to to take him on at his old wages. His Honour said the respondents '.:ould come before him when the man was able to work. He would make an order of 2i ed per week commencing from the 13 h of February. Costs would follow on the B. Scale.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-05-18_WidnesExaminer_art0130" n="art0130">
        <head>PEN KETH.</head>
        <p>PEN KETH.</p>
        <p>DEATH OF Me. WILLIAM HOUGIITON.-011 Sunday morning, Mr. William Houghton, Hall Nook, P,gnketb, passed away after a short illness, at the age of fifty-two vcarF. Mr. Houghton was the eldest son of Mr. W. D. Houghton, the proprietor of Sankey Wire Mills, at, which place the deceased gentleman also held an official posi- tion. He has resided in Penketh tor a great number of years, and was highly respected by the residents of that village. He was a Cnurchman, and a Conservative in politic. He leaves a widow and several children. The uneral took place on Wednesday.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-05-18_WidnesExaminer_art0131" n="art0131">
        <head>ST. HELENS.</head>
        <p>ST. HELENS.</p>
        <p>EXTRAORDINARY SUICIDE AT ST. HELENS.</p>
        <p>Mr. Brighouse. county coroner, held an inquest at the Ramfore Hotel, Parr, St. Helens, on Tuesday, on the body of Marcus Forest, aged sixty-one, 278, Bamford- street, who hung himself on Monday morning, the act being carried out with extraordinary deliberation. Sarah Elizeneth Burrows, granddaughter, said she went to deceased's house Thursday.to nurse her grandmother, eche was very ill. Deceased was a foreman bricklayer in the employ of Mr. Peter Tickle. He was very much upset by the anxiety causN:l by his wife's illness, and said it had given him his deathblow. On Monday it was noticed that he was carrying a clothes line about, and when he was asked what he was going to do with it replied,"Mine your own business." He went into a house next door, which beleneed to him, which was not quite finished, and said he was going to no3il the doors up. Witness heard him hammering for some time, and then the noise ceased, and she became alarmed. Along wile a neighbour she went to the hull eine.', and found the doors fast. Oh looking throueh a joint she saw deceesed hanging.—John Platt, of 166, Bamford-street, !Aid he Cut deceased down. There was no staircase in the build- ing, but deceased had gone up a ladder and fastened the rope to aneeem. lie had the., tied his legs together and pat the rope three times round his neck. Then, by means of an ingenious slip-no'; arrangement, he had contrived that when he threw himself off into space the cords would tighten and prevent him absolutely trom using his hands in any way. He was dead when cut down.—The jary returned a verdict of 'Suicide while of unsound mind."</p>
        <p>Action Against a Warrington 'Bus Proprietor.</p>
        <p>In the Chancery Court of Lancaster, held at St. George's Hall, Liverpool, on Monday, before Vice- Chancellor Hall, the ease of Marston v. Connor came up for consideration. This was an action to restrain the defendant, who is a well-known carriage and bus proprietor, from selling or exposiug for sale collecting boxes applicable to tramcars and other public conveyances, in infringement of the letters patent held by the plaintiff, and dated March sth, 1898, No. 5,455. The plaintiff carries on business at Birkenhead.—Mr. Durandu, who appeared for the plaintiff, said that defendant now submitted to the injunction asked for and consented to pay £lO dardages and the costs of the actien,—Mr Lawrence f3r the defendant, assented, and the order was made. BEST BOOTS FO ALL, UNGER THE CLOCK.— vt ASEs. or CUNNINGHAM' HIGH-CLASS MINERAL WATERS— Advt LINSEED COMPOUND' (Trade Mark) for Coughs and Colds, of proven efficacy, 13,1 d. Sold by Chemists only.—Advt.</p>
