        <p>TO THE BURGESSEt: OF THE TOWN HALL WARD. LADIES AND GENTLEMEN,— Having been requested hy a large number of influential ratepayers (and also by the Master Butchers' Association) in the town to contest this ward. I be to offer you my services. In the near future it is the irit.ut'on of the Corporation to erect Public Abattoirs. This is a question which seriously affects the master butchers, and ieeing all insportsuat body of traders they think it only just thAy should hay, a represen- tative to look ta their interests in this matter. I hive had several years' experiencs in municipal natters, and in this respect claim to have a distinct advantage Over my opponents. Under the present system of electing Councillors to fill vacancies, the whole of the ratepayers are practically disen- franchised ; this I consider unjust, sad shauld you elect me as your representative, I will endeavour to serve the interests of ail classes (independent of party or polities), particularly the burgesses of this ward. I coafidently appeal for your support on the day of election, and have The honous to remain. Your obedient servant. HENRY SINGLETON. Warrington. May 15th, 1900.</p>
        <p>MUNICIPAL BYE-NLECTION, 1900.—TOWN HALL WARD. TO THE BURG-8991 OF THE WARD. LADIES AND GENTLEMEN, I am desired by a numerous body of Ratepayers of this Ward, of both political parties, to allow myself to become a Candidate for Election to represent you in the Town Council. Being a large ratepayer myself, you can quite understand that if electeu, I shall certainly keep a watchful eye ever your interests, and anything that might tend to the progress and advantage of the borough would certainly have my sup- port, consistent with the proper and economical expenditure of the Revenue of the Borough. EWarrington being my native town, I shall always have its welfare at heart,and if any Burgess chould have any matter to bring forward,by which the town might gain an advantage, I should be pleased at any time to discuss the matter with him or her and put it forward In the right quarter. With respect to property owners, if anything can be done to relieve the rates sr to make the payment of the cost of paving the streets more easy for them, it will have my best attention and most cordial support. Hoping to have your confidence on the day of Election, 'Relieve me, Ladies and Gentlemen, • Yours faithfully. WM. J. FORSHAW. 42, Sanitey-street. Warrington, May 15th, 1930.</p>
